window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__["vol4/12_enforcement"] = {"key": "vol4/12_enforcement", "sections": [{"id": "sec-1", "level": 2, "title": "Purpose"}, {"id": "sec-2", "level": 2, "title": "The Per-Tick Gatekeeper"}, {"id": "sec-3", "level": 2, "title": "Stopping Envelopes"}, {"id": "sec-4", "level": 2, "title": "Refusing Proposals and Enforcing State Invariants"}, {"id": "sec-5", "level": 3, "title": "Closed-form quadratic programming and torque projection"}, {"id": "sec-6", "level": 3, "title": "Execution timing in the sub-millisecond loop"}, {"id": "sec-7", "level": 2, "title": "Admission, Cyclic Checking, and Monitor Independence"}, {"id": "sec-8", "level": 3, "title": "Where the thesis bends"}, {"id": "sec-9", "level": 3, "title": "Industrial stop categories and the safe torque off trap"}, {"id": "sec-10", "level": 3, "title": "Certified safety functions beneath the permission path"}, {"id": "sec-11", "level": 2, "title": "Fallacies and Pitfalls"}, {"id": "sec-12", "level": 2, "title": "Summary"}], "html": "<h1 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h1\">Safety Enforcement</h1>\n<h2 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h2\">Purpose</h2>\n<p><em>Why does an admitted plan still need permission on every control tick?</em></p>\n<p>A trajectory can be feasible when it arrives and become unsafe while the machine follows it. The body lags its reference, a payload or a hot motor erodes braking authority, a person steps into the path, or the learned proposer stalls with its last command still in force. A machine that checks only before motion begins discovers these changes after the energy is already moving, and the proposer cannot be trusted to notice them, because nothing in its output bounds stopping distance, actuator effort, or timing. Learned proposals therefore need an independent permission path that re-checks current conditions on every tick, keeps useful motion inside a stopping envelope, corrects a command when a feasible correction exists, and holds a response it can execute without the proposer before the remaining margin is spent. Each of those guarantees depends on bounded sensing error, available actuator force, and a decision delivered on time. Enforcement joins these conditions at the causal boundary, where a permitted command becomes motion that software cannot undo.</p>\n<p>{{&lt; pagebreak &gt;}}</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Learning Objectives</div>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Calculate the finished stopping budget and its speed ceiling, treating the tracking bound as a premise the enforcer monitors on every tick</li>\n<li>Derive how momentum and commanded effort change the remaining safety margin, and the contact force at a latch and at a handover that caps approach speed</li>\n<li>Explain how a control barrier function limits motion while its model, sensing, actuator, and timing assumptions hold</li>\n<li>Formulate a quadratic program that minimally adjusts a proposal within feasible actuator and safety constraints</li>\n<li>Select state-matched fallback responses when sensing, computation, or actuator limits invalidate normal enforcement</li>\n<li>Distinguish the permission path's unrated performance checks from the certified drive, scanner, and arm safety functions that carry the protective claim beneath it</li>\n<li>Construct an enforcement record and formulate the admitted command that binds each check to its detector, its fallback response, and the records it consumes</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"sec-2\" class=\"cs-h2\">The Per-Tick Gatekeeper</h2>\n<p>Every physical AI system ends in the same act. A command leaves software, becomes current in a motor winding, and puts momentum into a world that does not roll back. Safety enforcement is the runtime mechanism that decides, one control tick at a time, whether the next command may cross that causal boundary. Checking a plan once before it runs does not settle the question, because admission judges the plan against the state the planner predicted, and the machine then executes it against the state it actually reaches. Between those two moments the body can lag its reference, an actuator can run short of torque, a person can step into the aisle, and the learned proposer can stall or emit a setpoint the plan never contained. Enforcement exists so that none of these events is discovered only after the energy has been delivered.</p>\n<p>The central constraint is that the learned proposer can neither be trusted with that decision nor be discarded. Its competence is what makes the machine useful, yet nothing in its output bounds stopping distance, actuator effort, or timing. Enforcement resolves the tension by separating proposal from permission, the third law (principle \\ref{pri-vol4-proposal-permission}), and by giving the permission side three jobs. It must hold a stopping reservation, the clearance the machine needs to arrest its motion from the current state; it must correct a proposal minimally when a feasible correction exists; and it must select a response it can execute without the proposer when none does. The chapter builds the three jobs in that order.</p>\n<p>On the warehouse mobile manipulator, the safety microcontroller (MCU) admits the run down the aisle only after the matched stop suffix named in its trajectory record is resident in the MCU's memory and feasible from the commitment entry state defined in @sec-planning-two-tier-deadlines. Commitment is the instant after which the machine can no longer abandon the plan's motion without that latched suffix, so the suffix is matched to the state the machine will have at that instant, not to the states it passes through on the way. Before commitment, a different response must cover the motion. Admission requires an independently executable, state-matched fallback from every state reachable up to that instant. It also requires a bounded representation of the S-curve suffix that the MCU can decode, validate, and execute within its declared workload (the polynomial layout of @sec-appendix-systems-wire-trajectory is an illustrative encoding rather than that S-curve representation). Because that admission step checks the plan only once before motion starts, the machine still executes the plan one millisecond tick at a time, and enforcement is the check made on every one of those ticks together with the response it selects when even the resident stop no longer fits.</p>\n<p>↩ The trajectory record and the matched stop suffix it names are established in @sec-planning-trajectory-contract.</p>\n<p>The enforcer, the permission path's per-tick gatekeeper, sits on the permission side of the proposal boundary. It is a hard real-time component that evaluates candidate proposals, verifies invariant state bounds, and holds the exclusive hardware authority to permit current flow or command a fallback stop, keeping the task competence of learned policies while denying them authority over geometric clearance, thermal limits, and human-safety invariants.</p>\n<p>↩ The per-tick permission cadence on which the enforcer runs is established in @sec-nervous-time-constants-phase-lag.</p>\n<p>In the architecture this chapter follows, the enforcer executes on a dedicated microcontroller wired to the sensing and drive interfaces it needs. It checks each candidate command before the drive can apply it. A packet firewall offers a limited software analogy: a sender may request an operation without having authority to perform it. Unlike a packet filter, the enforcer must also account for momentum already present in the plant and preserve an achievable response when it refuses a request.</p>\n<p>↪ The silicon placement that keeps the permission path independent of the proposer is developed in @sec-placement-isolation-decisions.</p>\n<p>The architecture separates a capable proposer, a resident protective controller, and an output selector. This is the <strong>Simplex architecture</strong> [@seto1998simplex; @sha2001using], the runtime-assurance pattern that @sec-boundary-machine-model first named. The proposer supplies useful candidates, while the permission path retains the exclusive ability to select a physically feasible response without waiting for it.</p>\n<p>The rest of the chapter follows the running machine through that per-tick check. The starting point is the deficit planning left open, when the jerk-limited stop carried the base's stopping budget past the rack-end clearance at the drive limit while the body's departure from its reference remained unpriced: the first quantity the enforcer needs is how far the body can stray from the reference it is asked to follow, because every clearance the permission path defends must also contain that error. Once the tracking bound supplies that missing spatial term, the finished stopping budget fixes the reservation and a candidate aisle speed that still requires physical qualification. Actuator saturation then reveals how quickly that reservation shrinks once gravity or thermal limits spend braking authority. A control barrier function turns the reservation into a constraint on the next command. A least-change filter can then correct a violating proposal, both in free space and in contact, inside a declared cycle budget on a processor the proposer cannot preempt. When a check fails or a lease expires, the fallback hierarchy supplies the surviving hardware response, and the chapter closes with the infrastructure failures that break these premises and the enforcement record that binds every check to its evidence.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Simplex Architecture</strong>: The design came from Carnegie Mellon in the 1990s as a way to upgrade the controller of a running plant without stopping it. An untested new controller drove the plant while a simple verified controller and a switching rule stood ready to take it back [@seto1998simplex]. The learned proposer occupies the untested controller's seat, and the design's premise, that the region from which the verified controller can still recover is known in advance, is the stopping envelope this chapter computes.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ TIMING CONTRACT AND THE LEASE-EXPIRY WORST CASE\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: the lease paragraph below; the cycle timeline, @tbl-12-fallback-hierarchy,\n# │          the enforcement record, and the summary reuse these strings.\n# │ Goal:    state the three timers and the delay from last renewal to brake onset.\n# │ Show:    chunk rate, lease, heartbeat rate and timeout, self-watchdog,\n# │          tick, bus cycle, brake onset, renewal-to-onset delay; the\n# │          evidence-age refusal threshold (budgeted observation age plus the\n# │          clock-conversion bound) read by cycle stage 2 and the record. The\n# │          base stall is priced in calc-enforcement-stopping-budget, after the\n# │          spare is set.\n# │ How:     the same RM/AISLE fields that size the lease in @sec-nervous-lease-sizing.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_latency, fmt_frequency, check\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass EnforcementLeaseStop:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    chunk_rate = RM.chunk_rate  # category: chosen\n    lease = AISLE.t_lease  # category: chosen\n    heartbeat_rate = AISLE.heartbeat_rate  # category: chosen\n    heartbeat_timeout = AISLE.heartbeat_timeout  # category: chosen\n    self_watchdog = AISLE.mcu_self_watchdog  # category: chosen\n    tick = AISLE.t_tick  # category: chosen\n    bus = AISLE.t_bus  # category: illustrative\n    onset = AISLE.t_brake_onset  # category: illustrative\n    t_age = AISLE.perception_age  # category: derived (observation age the budget charges, row 5)\n    eps_sync = 0.2 * millisecond  # category: illustrative (clock-conversion bound, @sec-perception-observation-contract)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    chunk_period = (1 / chunk_rate).to(millisecond)\n    renewal_to_onset = (lease + tick + bus + onset).to(millisecond)\n    age_threshold = (t_age + eps_sync).to(millisecond)  # dispatch-age qualification threshold\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(chunk_period &lt; lease, &quot;One chunk period must fit inside the lease.&quot;)\n    check(heartbeat_timeout &lt; lease, &quot;A total host stall must trip the heartbeat before the lease expires.&quot;)\n    check(abs(lease.to(millisecond).magnitude - 60.0) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;The chunk lease is 60 ms.&quot;)\n    check(abs(renewal_to_onset.magnitude - 82.0) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Brake onset comes 82 ms after the last valid renewal.&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_age.to(millisecond).magnitude - 51.6) &lt; 0.05, &quot;The budgeted observation age is 51.6 ms.&quot;)\n    check(abs(age_threshold.magnitude - 51.8) &lt; 0.05, &quot;The dispatch-age qualification threshold is 51.8 ms.&quot;)\n    # fig12_canonical_fallback_ladder.svg and parts/images/svg/fig_part3_record_chain.svg draw 1 kHz, 60 ms, and 51.6 ms from these guards.\n    check(abs(tick.to(millisecond).magnitude - 1.0) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;The hand-drawn figures label the MCU tick 1 kHz.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    chunk_rate_hz_str = fmt(chunk_rate.to(hertz).magnitude, precision=0)\n    lease_ms_str = fmt_latency(lease, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    heartbeat_rate_hz_str = fmt(heartbeat_rate.to(hertz).magnitude, precision=0)\n    heartbeat_timeout_ms_str = fmt_latency(heartbeat_timeout, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    self_watchdog_ms_str = fmt_latency(self_watchdog, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    tick_ms_str = fmt_latency(tick, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    tick_us_str = fmt_latency(tick, unit=microsecond, precision=0)\n    loop_rate_str = fmt_frequency((1 / tick).to(hertz))\n    bus_ms_str = fmt_latency(bus, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    onset_ms_str = fmt_latency(onset, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    renewal_to_onset_ms_str = fmt_latency(renewal_to_onset, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_age_str = fmt_latency(t_age, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    eps_sync_str = fmt_latency(eps_sync, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    age_threshold_str = fmt_latency(age_threshold, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n]```\n\n## The Tracker Handoff and Dynamic Reference Envelopes {#sec-enforcement-handoff}\n\nEven when communication latency is zero and the permission path admits every reference command without delay, physical robots follow reference trajectories imperfectly. Mechanical compliance, inertia, and non-ideal motor dynamics produce tracking discrepancies. The feedback tracker, the closed-loop controller that turns admitted references into actuator effort, must bound this deviation across the entire maneuver. The bound is a **worst-case tracking error tube** ($\\TrackingErrorBound$), an upper bound on the spatial departure of the tracked position output from its nominal reference. For Cartesian point tracking, $\\CartesianPosition(\\TimeInstant)$ and $\\ReferencePosition(\\TimeInstant)$ denote the actual and reference positions evaluated in the same spatial frame. For whole-body systems such as mobile manipulators, the execution stack defines a declared output map or metric weighting that bounds the maximum physical swept-volume displacement in meters. The safety enforcer depends directly on this bound. Any spatial clearance reserved around a planned reference path must guarantee physical containment of the actual robot body.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The admitted setpoints and feedforward torques that the tracker turns into actuator effort are established in @sec-planning-handoff.\n:::\n\nProvided that the physical system begins within an initial qualified error region and its operating parameters (reference velocity, command acceleration, payload mass, external disturbances, and actuator temperature) stay within their validated operating envelope, the feedback controller guarantees the transient position-error bound:\n$$\\|\\CartesianPosition(\\TimeInstant) - \\ReferencePosition(\\TimeInstant)\\| \\le \\TrackingErrorBound \\quad \\forall \\TimeInstant \\ge 0.$$\nThis guarantee forms a software contract between the tracking layer and the safety enforcer. Rather than re-analyzing raw plant dynamics or reconstructing low-level controller state on every millisecond control cycle, the enforcer consumes this certified error bound as an invariant. However, this contract confines physical motion to the $\\TrackingErrorBound$ tube only while the operational conditions used to establish the certificate remain valid.\n\nThe magnitude of $\\TrackingErrorBound$ is a physical distance dictated by hardware properties: reflected rotor inertia ($\\GearReductionRatio^2 \\RotorInertia$), gearbox backlash, structural compliance, and actuator torque saturation. While a steady-state stiffness calculation balances constant external disturbances against closed-loop restoring force, steady-state metrics fail to capture transient overshoot, phase lag, or settling oscillations excited during aggressive maneuvers. Engineering teams therefore cannot rely on static linear approximations; they must support the tracking tube bound $\\TrackingErrorBound$ with a bounded plant and controller model, defined operating limits, and full-envelope dynamic qualification tests. Tests can expose an invalid bound; the tested trajectories alone do not prove containment for every admissible maneuver.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Reflected rotor inertia, $\\GearReductionRatio^2 \\RotorInertia$, is established in @sec-body-reflected-rotor-inertia.\n:::\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ THE BASE'S TRACKING BOUND</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: the tracking-bound paragraphs immediately below.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    state the inset the enforcer applies and what a doubled error costs.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    the base's tracking bound and the error if wear doubled it.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     AISLE.eps_track, the same field row 7 of the stopping budget uses.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_length, check</p>\n<p>AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class EnforcementTrackingBound:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>eps_track = AISLE.eps_track  # category: illustrative</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>eps_doubled = (2 * eps_track).to(millimeter)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(eps_track.to(millimeter).magnitude - 40.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"The base's tracking bound is 40 mm.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>eps_track_str = fmt_length(eps_track, unit=millimeter, precision=0) eps_doubled_str = fmt_length(eps_doubled, unit=millimeter, precision=0) ]```</p>\n<p>For the baseline warehouse mobile manipulator, the illustrative dynamic qualification target is $\\TrackingErrorBound =$ <code>[EnforcementTrackingBound.eps_track_str]</code> (illustrative; see the Reader Guide). The tracking controller sustains this bound only while three coupled operational constraints remain satisfied. First, reference trajectories must not demand accelerations exceeding drive torque limits. Second, external disturbance forces must remain below $\\DisturbanceForceBound$, the qualified bound on external disturbance force. Last, actuator coils must operate below their continuous thermal derating thresholds and DC bus voltage limits. A reference trajectory demanding acceleration beyond peak torque drives the actuators into saturation, causing the physical chassis to lag behind the command. Without aggressive anti-windup clamping, the integrator state accumulates severe windup, prolonging recovery long after the reference demand subsides. Outside this qualified operating envelope, the tracking certificate is void, and the system loses its guaranteed error bound.</p>\n<p>↩ Continuous thermal derating of motor windings is established in @sec-body-thermal-duty-cycles.</p>\n<p>Because the physical robot can deviate from its planned reference by up to $\\TrackingErrorBound$ in any direction, the safety architecture must subtract this uncertainty margin directly from the admissible workspace. Let $\\BarrierFunction$ measure the spatial clearance remaining around a reference. To guarantee that the physical robot maintains non-negative clearance, the enforcer applies an inset contraction: $$\\ContractedBarrierFunction(\\ReferencePosition) = \\BarrierFunction(\\ReferencePosition) - \\TrackingErrorBound \\ge 0.$$ A reference trajectory that maintains nominal clearance $\\NominalReferenceClearance$ from a warehouse rack upright guarantees the physical chassis only $\\NominalReferenceClearance - \\TrackingErrorBound$ of actual clearance. The safety enforcer therefore evaluates reference commands against a contracted boundary that adds <code>[EnforcementTrackingBound.eps_track_str]</code> to the nominal buffer. As detailed in @sec-enforcement-stopping-envelopes, this tracking inset compounds directly with stopping distance budgets, where localization uncertainty and transport delay consume additional clearance. Mechanical wear poses an insidious operational risk here. If worn drive bearings, tire degradation, or altered motor parameters double the true tracking error to <code>[EnforcementTrackingBound.eps_doubled_str]</code> without updating the enforcer's parameter registry, the physical base consumes an unbudgeted <code>[EnforcementTrackingBound.eps_track_str]</code> of clearance that the enforcer assumes remains available. An unmodeled tracking degradation silently undermines every higher-level safety margin.</p>\n<p>↩ Clearance insets that absorb spatial sensing error are established in @sec-perception-error-clearance.</p>\n<p>To maintain safety guarantees in production, the runtime monitoring stack must treat $\\TrackingErrorBound$ as an actively verified contract rather than a static design assumption. The underlying real-time middleware publishes $\\TrackingErrorBound$ alongside its validity envelope, while hardware monitors estimate the instantaneous tracking error $\\TrackingErrorDistance(\\TimeInstant) = \\|\\CartesianPosition(\\TimeInstant) - \\ReferencePosition(\\TimeInstant)\\|$ on every <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.tick_ms_str]</code> controller tick. Agreement with encoder-reported position is a runtime proxy, not independent evidence of physical localization; the localization allowance must contain drift that these channels cannot expose. If an observable disturbance or actuator thermal throttling causes the monitored $\\TrackingErrorDistance(\\TimeInstant)$ to exceed $\\TrackingErrorBound$, the tracking contract is broken. Upon detecting this violation, the safety enforcer revokes the admitted command and activates a state-matched emergency fallback maneuver. Maintaining physical containment during this transition requires that the fallback braking trajectory remains dynamically feasible even after the tracking bound is lost.</p>\n<p>A spatial stopping boundary must reserve margin for the tracking loop's qualified error bound. The runtime stopping envelope (@eq-enforce-stopping-envelope) incorporates this geometric inset alongside displacement accumulated during transport lag before admitting reference trajectories. When the safety barrier certificates of @sec-enforcement-safe-sets-conditional-permission are formulated in non-spatial coordinates (such as energy or momentum limits), the safety layer must map the tracking tube into a corresponding conservative bound on barrier variation, rather than subtracting a scalar distance. Evaluating barrier certificates against nominal trajectory geometry alone risks admitting references whose physical tracking tubes intersect obstacle boundaries.</p>\n<p>Control theory provides rich methodologies for synthesizing tracking controllers, state observers, and disturbance rejection algorithms [@slotine1991applied; @astrom2021feedback]. In embedded industrial robotics, systems frequently deploy a <strong>cascaded control hierarchy</strong>, where outer position loops feed intermediate velocity setpoints down to high-rate current loops. Alternatively, a <strong>tracking Model Predictive Controller (MPC)</strong> solves an online optimization problem over a receding horizon to generate optimal corrective control actions while respecting actuator bounds. From the perspective of the safety enforcer, the specific internal architecture of the tracking controller is abstracted away; the safety filter requires only that the chosen implementation certifies and guarantees a concrete tracking bound $\\TrackingErrorBound$ under specified operational conditions.</p>\n<p>A tracking controller may establish convergence to the reference path under its qualified model and operating conditions. That result supplies no collision-avoidance guarantee. A well-tuned tracker will follow an erroneous trajectory directly into a warehouse support column with high precision. Control Lyapunov functions mathematically formalize this asymptotic convergence toward reference targets; @sec-appendix-control-clf details their theoretical foundation and how optimization pipelines unify stabilizing Lyapunov objectives with the safety barrier certificates this chapter develops in @sec-enforcement-safe-sets-conditional-permission.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Control Lyapunov Function</strong>: A Lyapunov function certifies that an unforced system settles; a control Lyapunov function asks only that, at each state, some admissible input make it decrease. @artstein1983stabilization tied the existence of such a function to stabilizability by feedback, and @sontag1989universal gave an explicit formula that builds the stabilizing input from it. The control barrier function, defined later in this chapter, is its mirror image, keeping the state out of a region instead of pulling it toward a point, and when the two conflict the combined optimization relaxes convergence so that the barrier holds.</p>\n<p>This division of responsibilities cleanly separates trajectory tracking from safety enforcement. The low-level tracking controller focuses on driving the physical plant toward reference states, while the safety enforcer determines whether those references can be executed without violating invariant boundaries. The tracking error bound $\\TrackingErrorBound$ bridges these subsystems by quantifying the spatial divergence between admitted commands and physical execution. Because environmental disturbances, unmodeled payload shifts, or thermal current derating can rapidly widen this error tube, safety architectures must continuously verify physical performance against contracted assumptions. The downstream stopping envelope accounts for this tracking tube alongside sensor latency, braking distance, and localization drift, ensuring that the physical robot remains safely buffered from surrounding hazards.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h2\">Stopping Envelopes</h2>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ THE FINISHED STOPPING BUDGET OF THE WAREHOUSE MOBILE MANIPULATOR\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: row 7 and aisle-speed prose below, @tbl-enforcement-stopping-budget.\n# │ Goal:    add the tracking inset (row 7), then choose the aisle speed that the\n# │          finished budget allows.\n# │ Show:    each row&#x27;s term, value, running total, and remainder at the drive\n# │          limit; the same terms at the aisle speed; the ceiling; the time rows;\n# │          the missed-tick cost reused by the cycle list; the walking-person\n# │          ceiling (moved from @sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion); the\n# │          base stall at the aisle speed (distance added, overrun past D_clear);\n# │          the Delta_da allocation charged against the timing allowance and\n# │          the reserve it leaves.\n# │ How:     the Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStop* budgets (StoppingBudgetModel\n# │          with the S-curve jerk bound AISLE.j_brake, the tracking inset, and\n# │          the walking person) give the totals, spares, and ceilings;\n# │          WarehouseHostLeaseStall prices the stall; RM/AISLE fields split the\n# │          delay travel into rows 3-5; calc_max_permitted_velocity with\n# │          tau + a/(2j) is the closed-form cross-check above a^2/j;\n# │          RM.intent_inference_latency, the computed prefill-plus-decode\n# │          latency (time row).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import standard_gravity\nfrom mlsysim.physics.robotics import (\n    calc_c2_stop_suffix,\n    calc_scurve_stop_distance,\n    calc_scurve_stop_time,\n    calc_max_permitted_velocity,\n    calc_free_space_expiry,\n)\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_int, fmt_length, fmt_velocity, fmt_latency, fmt_acceleration, fmt_jerk, fmt_magnitude, fmt_math, check\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\nDRIVE_STOP = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopAtDriveLimit\nAISLE_STOP = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopAtAisleSpeed\n\nclass EnforcementStoppingBudget:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    v_drive = RM.base.max_velocity  # category: illustrative (drive limit)\n    v_aisle = AISLE.v_aisle  # category: chosen\n    a_brake = AISLE.a_brake  # category: illustrative (credible, loaded, dry floor)\n    d_clear = AISLE.d_clear  # category: illustrative\n    delta_loc = AISLE.delta_loc  # category: illustrative\n    delta_margin = AISLE.delta_margin  # category: chosen\n    eps_track = AISLE.eps_track  # category: illustrative\n    t_act = AISLE.t_brake_onset  # category: illustrative\n    t_lease = AISLE.t_lease  # category: chosen\n    t_tick = AISLE.t_tick  # category: chosen\n    t_bus = AISLE.t_bus  # category: illustrative\n    t_age = AISLE.perception_age  # category: derived\n    tau_delay = AISLE.tau_delay  # category: derived (excludes Delta_da)\n    delta_da = AISLE.t_dispatch_to_admission.to(\n        millisecond\n    )  # category: illustrative (allocation of @sec-placement-dispatch-admission, not yet measured)\n    v_human = AISLE.v_human_approach  # category: datasheet (ISO 13855 K)\n    mu_inspected = AISLE.mu_inspected_floor  # category: illustrative\n    t_intent = RM.intent_inference_latency  # category: derived (deployed INT4 prefill plus decode, @sec-brain-memory-wall)\n    # Jerk bound: the peak jerk of the reference C2 polynomial at the drive limit (@sec-planning-c2-stopping-suffix)\n    j_brake = AISLE.j_brake  # category: derived\n    t_stall = (Scenarios.WarehouseHostLeaseStall.added_delay + t_lease).to(millisecond)  # category: illustrative (proposer stall before any brake)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    drive = DRIVE_STOP.result\n    aisle = AISLE_STOP.result\n    lease_path = (t_lease + t_tick + t_bus).to(millisecond)\n    overhead = drive.overhead.to(millimeter)\n    v_plateau = drive.jerk_plateau_speed.to(meter / second)\n    ramp_delay = drive.ramp_delay.to(millisecond)\n\n    # Rows 1-7 at the drive limit, in book order\n    brake_drive = drive.braking_travel.to(millimeter)\n    onset_drive = (v_drive * t_act).to(millimeter)\n    lease_drive = (v_drive * lease_path).to(millimeter)\n    age_drive = (v_drive * t_age).to(millimeter)\n    shape_drive = drive.jerk_ramp_travel.to(millimeter)\n    inset = drive.tracking_inset.to(millimeter)\n    run1 = brake_drive\n    run2 = run1 + overhead\n    run3 = run2 + onset_drive\n    run4 = run3 + lease_drive\n    run5 = run4 + age_drive\n    run6 = run5 + shape_drive\n    run7 = run6 + inset\n    left = [\n        (d_clear - run1).to(millimeter),\n        (d_clear - run2).to(millimeter),\n        (d_clear - run3).to(millimeter),\n        (d_clear - run4).to(millimeter),\n        (d_clear - run5).to(millimeter),\n        (d_clear - run6).to(millimeter),\n        (d_clear - run7).to(millimeter),\n    ]\n\n    # The same terms at the aisle speed\n    brake_aisle = aisle.braking_travel.to(millimeter)\n    onset_aisle = (v_aisle * t_act).to(millimeter)\n    lease_aisle = (v_aisle * lease_path).to(millimeter)\n    age_aisle = (v_aisle * t_age).to(millimeter)\n    shape_aisle = aisle.jerk_ramp_travel.to(millimeter)\n    total_aisle = aisle.required_distance.to(millimeter)\n    spare = aisle.spare.to(millimeter)\n    # Base stall: a last proposal that keeps authority for the stall instead of\n    # the lease adds the extra delay&#x27;s travel to the finished budget.\n    stall_added = Scenarios.WarehouseHostLeaseStall.result.added_delay_travel.to(millimeter)\n    stall_breach = (total_aisle + stall_added - d_clear).to(millimeter)\n\n    # Speed is the one free term: the subtotal ceiling before dispatch allocation\n    ceiling = aisle.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)\n    # Above a^2/j the S-curve is constant braking at a_brake with the delay lengthened by a/(2j)\n    ceiling_closed = calc_max_permitted_velocity(\n        d_clear, tau_delay + ramp_delay, a_brake, loc_margin=delta_loc, physical_margin=delta_margin + eps_track\n    ).to(meter / second)\n    total_check = drive.required_distance.to(millimeter)\n    # Operands of @eq-enforce-speed-ceiling for the napkin-math notebook\n    tau_eff = (tau_delay + ramp_delay).to(millisecond)\n    d_avail = (d_clear - delta_loc - delta_margin - eps_track).to(millimeter)\n    a_tau = (a_brake * tau_eff).to(meter / second)\n    a_tau_sq = (a_tau**2).to(meter**2 / second**2)\n    two_a_d = (2 * a_brake * d_avail).to(meter**2 / second**2)\n    root_term = ((a_tau_sq + two_a_d) ** 0.5).to(meter / second)\n    ceiling_hand = (root_term - a_tau).to(meter / second)\n\n    # Time rows (no distance added)\n    # Free-space expiry abreast of the occluded rack end (@eq-mem-free-space-expiry):\n    # the free depth is the hull-to-rack side clearance.\n    belief_life = calc_free_space_expiry(AISLE.side_clearance, delta_loc, delta_margin, v_human).to(millisecond)\n    missed_tick = (v_aisle * t_tick).to(millimeter)\n    t_latest = aisle.delay_spare.to(millisecond)\n    capture_to_admission_max = (t_age + t_latest).to(millisecond)\n    capture_to_onset_max = (tau_delay + t_latest).to(millisecond)\n    # Delta_da is not a term of tau_delay; the timing allowance pays for it.\n    travel_da = (v_aisle * delta_da).to(millimeter)\n    reserve_t = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopWithDispatch.result.delay_spare.to(millisecond)\n    reserve_d = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopWithDispatch.result.spare.to(millimeter)\n    capture_to_onset = AISLE.t_capture_to_brake_onset.to(millisecond)  # rows 1-3 of @tbl-04-event-timing\n    mu_min = (a_brake / standard_gravity).to(&quot;dimensionless&quot;).magnitude\n    a_inspected = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopInspectedFloor.a_brake.to(meter / second**2)\n    # The floor caps the deceleration; the jerk bound belongs to the load and is unchanged.\n    ceiling_inspected = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopInspectedFloor.result.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)\n\n    # Walking person: v tau + d_stop(v) + v_h (tau + T_stop(v)) + overhead = d_clear.\n    # The S-curve&#x27;s distance and time are not one polynomial in v, so MLSysIM solves it numerically.\n    overhead_all = AISLE.fixed_overhead  # category: derived (delta_loc + delta_margin + eps_track)\n    v_walk = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopWalkingPerson.result.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)\n    d_walk = (\n        v_walk * tau_delay\n        + calc_scurve_stop_distance(v_walk, a_brake, j_brake)\n        + v_human * (tau_delay + calc_scurve_stop_time(v_walk, a_brake, j_brake))\n        + overhead_all\n    ).to(meter)\n    # Complete scenario roots charge dispatch travel once; moving humans also\n    # close the gap during that interval. The inspected case remains an\n    # optimistic all-weight traction screen, not a qualified braking bound.\n    tau_complete = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopWithDispatch.delay\n    ceiling_complete = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopWithDispatch.result.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)\n    ceiling_inspected_complete = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopInspectedFloorWithDispatch.result.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)\n    v_walk_complete = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopWalkingPersonWithDispatch.result.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)\n    complete_walk_distance = (\n        v_walk_complete * tau_complete\n        + calc_scurve_stop_distance(v_walk_complete, a_brake, j_brake)\n        + v_human * (tau_complete + calc_scurve_stop_time(v_walk_complete, a_brake, j_brake))\n        + overhead_all\n    ).to(meter)\n    # Why the walking-person ceiling is so low: below a^2/j the jerk-limited stop never\n    # reaches a_brake and lasts 2 sqrt(v/j), far longer than v/a, and the person walks\n    # for the whole stop.\n    t_stop_walk = calc_scurve_stop_time(v_walk, a_brake, j_brake).to(millisecond)\n    t_stop_walk_const = (v_walk / a_brake).to(millisecond)\n    brake_walk = calc_scurve_stop_distance(v_walk, a_brake, j_brake).to(millimeter)\n    closure_walk = (v_human * t_stop_walk).to(millimeter)\n    # A reference polynomial rescaled to stop from v_walk at the full a_brake would need this jerk\n    jerk_rescaled_walk = calc_c2_stop_suffix(v_walk, a_brake)[&quot;peak_jerk&quot;].to(meter / second**3)\n    # The same walking-person budget under constant-deceleration braking (no jerk bound):\n    # v^2/2a + (tau + v_h/a) v + v_h tau + overhead - d_clear = 0, positive root\n    _qa = 1 / (2 * a_brake)\n    _qb = tau_delay + v_human / a_brake\n    _qc = v_human * tau_delay + overhead_all - d_clear\n    v_walk_const = ((-_qb + (_qb**2 - 4 * _qa * _qc) ** 0.5) / (2 * _qa)).to(meter / second)\n    # Latency sensitivity of the static ceiling: 10 ms more pre-brake delay\n    ceiling_plus10 = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopTenMsSlower.result.speed_ceiling.to(meter / second)\n    ceiling_drop_10ms = (ceiling - ceiling_plus10).to(millimeter / second)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(run1.magnitude - 562.5) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;Row 1 running total is 562.5 mm.&quot;)\n    check(abs(run2.magnitude - 712.5) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;Row 2 running total is 712.5 mm.&quot;)\n    check(abs(run3.magnitude - 742.5) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;Row 3 running total is 742.5 mm.&quot;)\n    check(abs(run4.magnitude - 835.5) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;Row 4 running total is 835.5 mm.&quot;)\n    check(abs(run5.magnitude - 912.9) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;Row 5 running total is 912.9 mm.&quot;)\n    check(abs(j_brake.magnitude - 64.0 / 9.0) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;The jerk bound is the reference polynomial&#x27;s 7.11 m/s^3 peak.&quot;)\n    check(v_aisle &gt;= v_plateau and v_drive &gt;= v_plateau, &quot;Both budget speeds reach the deceleration bound (trapezoidal regime).&quot;)\n    check(abs(run6.magnitude - 1123.8375) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;Row 6 running total is 1123.84 mm.&quot;)\n    check(abs(run7.magnitude - 1163.8375) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;Row 7 running total is 1163.84 mm.&quot;)\n    check(abs(total_check.magnitude - run7.magnitude) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;Row 7 total must equal @eq-enforce-stopping-envelope at the drive limit.&quot;)\n    check(abs(left[6].magnitude + 63.8375) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;Row 7 runs 63.84 mm past the clear distance.&quot;)\n    check(abs(lease_path.magnitude - 62.0) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Lease path is 62 ms.&quot;)\n    check(abs(tau_delay.magnitude - 133.6) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;tau_delay is 133.6 ms.&quot;)\n    check(abs(ceiling.magnitude - ceiling_closed.magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Numerical ceiling must match the closed form above a^2/j.&quot;)\n    check(ceiling &gt;= v_plateau, &quot;The ceiling lies in the trapezoidal regime.&quot;)\n    check(abs(ceiling.magnitude - 1.4367) &lt; 5e-5, &quot;Ceiling is 1.44 m/s.&quot;)\n    check(abs(ceiling_hand.magnitude - ceiling.magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;The notebook&#x27;s hand arithmetic must reproduce the ceiling.&quot;)\n    check(abs(d_avail.magnitude - 910.0) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Clearance left after the fixed allowances is 910 mm.&quot;)\n    check(v_aisle &lt; ceiling, &quot;The chosen aisle speed must sit under the ceiling.&quot;)\n    check(abs(total_aisle.magnitude - 968.9925) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;Budget at 1.3 m/s is 968.99 mm.&quot;)\n    check(abs(spare.magnitude - 131.0075) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;Spare at 1.3 m/s is 131.01 mm.&quot;)\n    check(abs(stall_added.magnitude - 182.0) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;A 200 ms stall adds 182 mm at 1.3 m/s.&quot;)\n    check(stall_added &gt; spare, &quot;The stall&#x27;s added travel exceeds the whole spare.&quot;)\n    check(abs(stall_breach.magnitude - 50.9925) &lt; 5e-3, &quot;The stall runs 51.0 mm past the clear distance.&quot;)\n    # 2026-09-28 (M1/M2): the deployed intent model is INT4; its full computed inference replaces the FP16 weight sweep.\n    check(abs(t_intent.to(millisecond).magnitude - 335.61) &lt; 5e-3, f&quot;Intent inference latency drifted: {t_intent}&quot;)\n    check(t_intent &gt; t_lease, &quot;The intent inference cannot renew a chunk lease.&quot;)\n    check(belief_life.magnitude == 0.0, &quot;Abreast of the occluded rack end the free-space claim expires at capture.&quot;)\n    check(abs(missed_tick.magnitude - 1.3) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;One missed tick costs 1.3 mm at 1.3 m/s.&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_latest.magnitude - 100.775) &lt; 5e-3, &quot;The unallocated timing allowance is 100.8 ms at 1.3 m/s.&quot;)\n    check(reserve_t.magnitude &gt; 0 and reserve_d.magnitude &gt; 0, &quot;The Delta_da allocation must leave positive reserve.&quot;)\n    check(abs((tau_delay + delta_da - capture_to_onset).to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Capture to onset = tau_delay + Delta_da.&quot;)\n    check(capture_to_onset &lt; capture_to_onset_max, &quot;The allocated capture-to-onset time must fall inside the latest onset.&quot;)\n    check(\n        abs((capture_to_onset_max - capture_to_admission_max - t_lease - t_tick - t_bus - t_act).to(millisecond).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9,\n        &quot;Admission-to-onset must contain the lease, tick, bus, and drive onset exactly once.&quot;,\n    )\n    check(abs(mu_min - 0.204) &lt; 5e-4, &quot;Friction premise is 0.204.&quot;)\n    check(abs(ceiling_inspected.magnitude - 1.2309) &lt; 5e-5, &quot;Inspected-floor ceiling is 1.23 m/s.&quot;)\n    check(abs(v_walk.magnitude - 0.2599) &lt; 5e-5, f&quot;Walking-person ceiling should be 0.260 m/s, got {v_walk}.&quot;)\n    check(v_walk &lt; v_plateau, &quot;The walking-person stop never reaches the deceleration bound.&quot;)\n    check(abs((d_walk - d_clear).to(millimeter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;At the walking-person ceiling the budget must fill the clear distance exactly.&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_stop_walk.magnitude - 382.38) &lt; 5e-2, &quot;The jerk-limited stop from the walking-person ceiling lasts about 382 ms.&quot;)\n    check(t_stop_walk &gt; 2.9 * t_stop_walk_const, &quot;Below a^2/j the jerk-limited stop lasts roughly three times a constant-deceleration stop.&quot;)\n    check(closure_walk &gt; 10 * brake_walk, &quot;The person closes more than ten times the base&#x27;s own braking travel.&quot;)\n    check(jerk_rescaled_walk &gt; 5 * j_brake, &quot;Reaching a_brake from the walking-person ceiling would need several times the jerk bound.&quot;)\n    check(abs(v_walk_const.magnitude - 0.6371) &lt; 5e-4, &quot;Without the jerk bound the walking-person ceiling would be 0.64 m/s.&quot;)\n    check(ceiling_drop_10ms.magnitude &gt; 0, &quot;More pre-brake delay must lower the ceiling.&quot;)\n    check(\n        ceiling_complete &lt; ceiling and ceiling_inspected_complete &lt; ceiling_inspected and v_walk_complete &lt; v_walk,\n        &quot;Positive dispatch allocation must lower each scenario root.&quot;,\n    )\n    check(v_aisle &lt; ceiling_complete, &quot;The dry aisle candidate must fit after dispatch allocation.&quot;)\n    check(\n        abs((complete_walk_distance - d_clear).to(millimeter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-6,\n        &quot;The complete walking root charges closure throughout dispatch and stop.&quot;,\n    )\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    v_drive_str = fmt_velocity(v_drive, precision=1)\n    v_aisle_str = fmt_velocity(v_aisle, precision=1)\n    ceiling_str = fmt_velocity(ceiling, precision=2)\n    ceiling_complete_str = fmt_velocity(ceiling_complete, precision=2)\n    ceiling_inspected_complete_str = fmt_velocity(ceiling_inspected_complete, precision=2)\n    v_walk_complete_str = fmt_velocity(v_walk_complete, precision=2)\n    ceiling_inspected_str = fmt_velocity(ceiling_inspected, precision=2)\n    v_walk_str = fmt_velocity(v_walk, precision=2)\n    v_plateau_str = fmt_velocity(v_plateau, precision=2)\n    v_human_str = fmt_velocity(v_human, precision=1)\n    t_stop_walk_str = fmt_latency(round(t_stop_walk.magnitude) * millisecond, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_stop_walk_const_str = fmt_latency(round(t_stop_walk_const.magnitude) * millisecond, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    brake_walk_str = fmt_length(round(brake_walk.magnitude) * millimeter, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    closure_walk_str = fmt_length(round(closure_walk.magnitude) * millimeter, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    jerk_rescaled_walk_str = fmt_jerk(round(jerk_rescaled_walk.magnitude) * meter / second**3, precision=0)\n    v_walk_const_str = fmt_velocity(v_walk_const, precision=2)\n    ceiling_drop_10ms_str = fmt_int(ceiling_drop_10ms.magnitude)\n    d_clear_str = fmt_length(d_clear, unit=meter, precision=2)\n    a_brake_str = fmt_acceleration(a_brake, precision=0)\n    j_brake_str = fmt_jerk(j_brake, precision=2)\n    ramp_delay_str = fmt_latency(ramp_delay, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    tau_eff_str = fmt_latency(tau_eff, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    d_avail_str = fmt_length(d_avail, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    d_clear_mm_str = fmt_length(d_clear, unit=millimeter, precision=0, commas=True)\n    overhead_all_str = fmt_length(AISLE.fixed_overhead, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    delta_loc_str = fmt_length(delta_loc, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    delta_margin_str = fmt_length(delta_margin, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    a_tau_str = fmt(a_tau.magnitude, precision=4)\n    a_tau_sq_str = fmt(a_tau_sq.magnitude, precision=4)\n    two_a_d_str = fmt(two_a_d.magnitude, precision=2)\n    root_term_str = fmt(root_term.magnitude, precision=4)\n    tau_delay_str = fmt_latency(tau_delay, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    lease_path_str = fmt_latency(lease_path, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_age_str = fmt_latency(t_age, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    t_intent_str = fmt_latency(round(t_intent.to(millisecond).magnitude) * millisecond, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    belief_life_str = fmt_latency(belief_life, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    missed_tick_str = fmt_length(missed_tick, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    t_latest_str = fmt_latency(t_latest, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    capture_to_admission_max_str = fmt_latency(capture_to_admission_max, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    capture_to_onset_max_str = fmt_latency(capture_to_onset_max, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    delta_da_str = fmt_latency(delta_da, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    travel_da_str = fmt_length(travel_da, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    reserve_t_str = fmt_latency(reserve_t, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    reserve_d_str = fmt_length(reserve_d, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    capture_to_onset_str = fmt_latency(capture_to_onset, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    mu_min_str = fmt(mu_min, precision=3)\n    mu_inspected_str = fmt(mu_inspected, precision=2)\n    inset_str = fmt_length(inset, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    run6_str = fmt_length(run6, unit=millimeter, precision=2)\n    run7_str = fmt_length(run7, unit=millimeter, precision=2)\n    over7_str = fmt_length(-left[6], unit=millimeter, precision=2)\n    total_aisle_str = fmt_length(total_aisle, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    spare_str = fmt_length(spare, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    t_stall_str = fmt_latency(t_stall, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    stall_added_str = fmt_length(stall_added, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    stall_breach_str = fmt_length(stall_breach, unit=millimeter, precision=2)\n    # Table cells (the column headers carry mm)\n    brake_drive_value_str = fmt_magnitude(brake_drive, millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n    overhead_value_str = fmt_magnitude(overhead, millimeter, precision=0, commas=True)\n    onset_drive_value_str = fmt_magnitude(onset_drive, millimeter, precision=0, commas=True)\n    lease_drive_value_str = fmt_magnitude(lease_drive, millimeter, precision=0, commas=True)\n    age_drive_value_str = fmt_magnitude(age_drive, millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n    shape_drive_value_str = fmt_magnitude(shape_drive, millimeter, precision=2, commas=True)\n    inset_value_str = fmt_magnitude(inset, millimeter, precision=0, commas=True)\n    run1_value_str = fmt_magnitude(run1, millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n    run2_value_str = fmt_magnitude(run2, millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n    run3_value_str = fmt_magnitude(run3, millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n    run4_value_str = fmt_magnitude(run4, millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n    run5_value_str = fmt_magnitude(run5, millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n    run6_value_str = fmt_magnitude(run6, millimeter, precision=2, commas=True)\n    run7_value_str = fmt_magnitude(run7, millimeter, precision=2, commas=True)\n    left1_value_str = fmt_magnitude(left[0], millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n    left2_value_str = fmt_magnitude(left[1], millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n    left3_value_str = fmt_magnitude(left[2], millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n    left4_value_str = fmt_magnitude(left[3], millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n    left5_value_str = fmt_magnitude(left[4], millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n    left6_math = fmt_math(fmt_magnitude(left[5], millimeter, precision=2, commas=True))\n    left7_math = fmt_math(fmt_magnitude(left[6], millimeter, precision=2, commas=True))\n    brake_aisle_value_str = fmt_magnitude(brake_aisle, millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n    onset_aisle_value_str = fmt_magnitude(onset_aisle, millimeter, precision=0, commas=True)\n    lease_aisle_value_str = fmt_magnitude(lease_aisle, millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n    age_aisle_value_str = fmt_magnitude(age_aisle, millimeter, precision=2, commas=True)\n    shape_aisle_value_str = fmt_magnitude(shape_aisle, millimeter, precision=2, commas=True)\n    total_aisle_value_str = fmt_magnitude(total_aisle, millimeter, precision=2, commas=True)\n    spare_value_str = fmt_magnitude(spare, millimeter, precision=2, commas=True)\n    travel_da_value_str = fmt_magnitude(travel_da, millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n    reserve_d_value_str = fmt_magnitude(reserve_d, millimeter, precision=1, commas=True)\n]```\n\nIf the warehouse mobile manipulator approaches a rack end at its `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_drive_str]` mechanical drive limit when a coworker steps into the aisle, the delay before braking begins and the jerk-limited deceleration curve carry the base past the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.d_clear_str]` clearance even before tracking error is counted. That rack-end crossing is the scene in which nearly every chapter since @sec-body has priced one stopping term: the body supplied braking and its fixed allowances, the Nervous System the lease path, perception the observation age, and planning the jerk-limited stop suffix that exceeded the rack-end clearance at the drive limit (@sec-planning-base-stopping-budget). The tracking bound of @sec-enforcement-handoff is the last spatial term. Every one of these terms serves the test that the first law, irreversibility (principle \\ref{pri-vol4-irreversibility}), sets, $\\StoppingDistance \\le \\ClearDistance$. The enforcer applies that test not as one calculation but as a set of states.\n\n::: {#dfn-enforcement-stopping-envelope .callout-definition title=&quot;Stopping envelope&quot;}\n\n***Stopping envelope*** is the set of plant states, parameterized by clearance and velocity along each degree of freedom, from which the resident emergency braking response, initiated after accounting for all worst-case delays, halts motion before reaching an obstacle boundary while preserving all localization, protective, and tracking margins.\n\n1.  **Significance**: The envelope translates the abstract irreversibility requirement $\\StoppingDistance \\le \\ClearDistance$ into an actionable runtime velocity ceiling. When approaching a rack end, the warehouse mobile manipulator&#x27;s envelope restricts forward velocity, with the stated dispatch allocation, to at most `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.ceiling_complete_str]`, substantially below its mechanical drive limit of `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_drive_str]`.\n2.  **Distinction**: Unlike a static geometric keep-out zone that depends solely on position, the stopping envelope is a dynamic construct governed by speed, system latencies, and braking authority. A given physical clearance safely admits a slow approach while strictly forbidding a high-speed transit.\n3.  **Common pitfall**: Treating the stopping envelope as a static, precomputed boundary. In practice, carried payload mass, surface traction coefficients, and motor thermal limits continuously reshape this boundary; calculating safety margins from ideal datasheet decelerations severely overestimates stopping authority when the machine is heavily loaded.\n\n:::\n\nThe stopping envelope is the machine&#x27;s recoverable set, the states from which the resident stop still succeeds. @Sec-planning-inevitable-collision-states fixes the same boundary in time, as the commitment deadline past which momentum can carry the machine into an inevitable collision state. Velocity enters the recoverable set twice. Delay travel grows linearly with speed and braking travel quadratically, so a clearance that admits a slow approach forbids a fast one at the same position, and the enforcer must keep the machine inside the envelope on every tick, before a fault or an unmodeled disturbance can carry it out. The running calculation uses representative engineering limits; deployment requires each term to be qualified on the actual hardware.\n\nThe pre-brake delay $\\PrebrakeDelay$ of @sec-body-stopping-distance-prebrake already contains the enforcer&#x27;s permission cycle, budgeted alongside the lease communication path in @sec-nervous-lease-sizing. One interval stays out of the spatial sum: the dispatch-to-admission delay $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$, which @tbl-04-event-timing places between proposal dispatch and admission. The budget charges it instead against the timing allowance that the remaining clearance leaves (@sec-enforcement-composite-budget). What the spatial sum still lacks is the tracking error between the commanded reference and the actual plant.\n\nThe enforcer accounts for that error with the tracking bound $\\TrackingErrorBound$, offsetting the baseline stopping distance of @eq-body-stopping-distance. It can admit a candidate reference trajectory only if the plant, which may lag that reference by up to $\\TrackingErrorBound$, can still brake to a standstill within the available free distance $\\ClearDistance$. Combining physical clearance, latency, and velocity yields the stopping subtotal before dispatch-to-admission travel is charged:\n$$\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\PrebrakeDelay + \\ResidentStopDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection) + \\LocalizationBound + \\ProtectiveClearanceMargin + \\TrackingErrorBound \\le \\ClearDistance$$ {#eq-enforce-stopping-envelope}\nHere, $\\ResidentStopDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection)$ represents the physical distance traversed during the braking maneuver from an initial speed $\\SpeedAtDetection$. For idealized constant-deceleration braking, this term simplifies to $\\SpeedAtDetection^2 / (2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration)$. Under realistic jerk-limited trajectory profiles, $\\ResidentStopDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection)$ is governed by @eq-plan-c2-stopping-suffix, which incorporates the additional displacement accrued during finite jerk transitions. The term $\\LocalizationBound$ denotes the localization uncertainty bound, while $\\ProtectiveClearanceMargin$ provides an explicit protective safety cushion. The left-hand side sums the baseline stopping distance $\\StoppingDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection)$ from @eq-body-stopping-distance with the tracking inset $\\TrackingErrorBound$.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The full stopping-distance model, tracking inset included, is developed in @sec-appendix-systems-stopping.\n:::\n\nFor complete static-obstacle admission, the delay term uses $\\PrebrakeDelay + \\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ in place of $\\PrebrakeDelay$; the resident stop and fixed allowances are unchanged. This inequality defines a conditional stopping subtotal in state space. Within this set, the accounted reaction latency and braking profile fit within the measured obstacle clearance; complete admission must also charge dispatch-to-admission travel. Operating outside this boundary violates the stopping reservation under the qualified model assumptions, though conservative engineering margins do not imply that an instantaneous collision is certain under all conceivable emergency maneuvers. Per-tick enforcement must maintain this stopping reservation over the entire control epoch until the next validated command can take effect, rather than merely verifying instantaneous feasibility at the sample instant.\n\nWhenever a proposed trajectory requires a stopping distance in @eq-enforce-stopping-envelope that exceeds the measured clearance along the current heading, the enforcer rejects the command. Because the robot moves directionally, the stopping envelope is anisotropic. Heading dictates which obstacles threaten forward travel and consume braking clearance, while localization uncertainty and the robot&#x27;s swept geometric volume demand clearance margins around the entire perimeter.\n\n### Sizing the composite stopping budget {#sec-enforcement-composite-budget}\n\nOmitting any single interval during which the base keeps moving before or during a stop, or evaluating those intervals under mismatched operating conditions, admits transit speeds that overrun the physical boundary when all delays coincide. The composite stopping budget therefore reserves clearance for every interval in the arrest sequence: sensor transduction lag, compute scheduling delays, actuator engagement, the deceleration profile, and tracking divergence, each evaluated under the same worst-case operating conditions.\n\nFor the warehouse mobile manipulator, the tracking bound of `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.inset_str]` is the final spatial term. At the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_drive_str]` drive limit, the matched stop suffix had already pushed the cumulative requirement to `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.run6_str]`, past the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.d_clear_str]` rack-end clearance; that is the deficit @sec-planning-base-stopping-budget left open. The tracking inset carries the total to `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.run7_str]`, an overshoot of `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.over7_str]` beyond the physical boundary.\n\nThe $\\DifferentiabilityClass^2$ stop suffix replaces idealized constant deceleration ($\\SpeedAtDetection^2 / 2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$) with the jerk-limited S-curve of @sec-planning-c2-stopping-suffix, governed by the peak jerk limit $\\JerkLimit =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.j_brake_str]`. Planning stipulated that bound from the reference $\\DifferentiabilityClass^2$ polynomial rather than measuring it; the S-curve is the stop the MCU actually runs, and the polynomial supplies only its jerk bound. Because both operating velocities reach the peak deceleration threshold, @eq-plan-c2-stopping-suffix models this maneuver by adding the displacement of half a jerk ramp:\n$$\\ResidentStopDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection) = \\frac{\\SpeedAtDetection^2}{2 \\CredibleBrakingDeceleration} + \\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\frac{\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration}{2 \\JerkLimit}.$$\nBecause this smoothly shaped stop consumes more clearance than discontinuous full-torque braking, the admissible transit speed must be read from the finished budget rather than from the constant-deceleration estimate.\n\nVelocity remains the single operational variable the control architecture can dynamically modulate. Above the characteristic velocity threshold $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration^2/\\JerkLimit =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_plateau_str]`, the finite jerk ramps function equivalently to an added delay of $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration/(2\\JerkLimit)$ prior to braking at constant deceleration $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$. Under these conditions, the pre-dispatch **speed ceiling** keeps the form of @eq-body-velocity-ceiling, the positive root of the stopping inequality at equality. The jerk ramps lengthen the pre-brake delay to an effective delay, and the tracking bound comes off the available braking distance:\n$$\\SpeedBound = -\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration{}\\EffectiveBrakingDelay + \\sqrt{(\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration{}\\EffectiveBrakingDelay)^2 + 2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration{}\\InsetAvailableBrakingDistance}, \\qquad \\EffectiveBrakingDelay = \\PrebrakeDelay + \\frac{\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration}{2\\JerkLimit}, \\qquad \\InsetAvailableBrakingDistance = \\ClearDistance - \\LocalizationBound - \\ProtectiveClearanceMargin - \\TrackingErrorBound$$ {#eq-enforce-speed-ceiling}\nBelow $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration^2/\\JerkLimit$, the deceleration trajectory never reaches the peak limit $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$, rendering stopping distance non-quadratic in velocity; in this low-speed regime, MLSysIM evaluates @eq-enforce-stopping-envelope via numerical root-finding. Notebook \\ref{nbk-enforcement-speed-ceiling} computes this ceiling for the mobile manipulator approaching the aisle rack end.\n\n::: {#nbk-enforcement-speed-ceiling .callout-notebook title=&quot;The ceiling the budget sets&quot;}\n\n**Problem**: The warehouse mobile manipulator approaches the rack end with rows 1 to 7 of @tbl-enforcement-stopping-budget accounted, before dispatch-to-admission travel. What is the highest speed at which the jerk-limited stop still fits the clear distance, and how much clearance does a candidate aisle speed of `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_aisle_str]` leave?\n\n**Variables**:\n\n* $\\ClearDistance =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.d_clear_str]` (Illustrative; clear distance at the rack end).\n* $\\LocalizationBound + \\ProtectiveClearanceMargin =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.delta_loc_str]` $+$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.delta_margin_str]` (Illustrative localization bound; chosen protective clearance), and $\\TrackingErrorBound =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.inset_str]` (Illustrative; the qualification target of @sec-enforcement-handoff).\n* $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.a_brake_str]` (Illustrative; credible loaded deceleration on a dry floor) and $\\JerkLimit =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.j_brake_str]` (Derived; the peak jerk of the reference $\\DifferentiabilityClass^2$ polynomial).\n* $\\PrebrakeDelay =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.tau_delay_str]` (Derived; observation age plus lease path plus brake onset, excluding $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$).\n* Drive limit `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_drive_str]` (Illustrative).\n\n**Math**: The fixed allowances leave $\\InsetAvailableBrakingDistance =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.d_clear_mm_str]` $-$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.overhead_all_str]` $=$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.d_avail_str]`. The jerk ramps lengthen the delay to $\\EffectiveBrakingDelay =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.tau_delay_str]` $+$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.ramp_delay_str]` $=$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.tau_eff_str]`, so $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration{}\\EffectiveBrakingDelay =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.a_tau_str]` m/s. Under the square root of @eq-enforce-speed-ceiling, $(\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration{}\\EffectiveBrakingDelay)^2 =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.a_tau_sq_str]` and $2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration{}\\InsetAvailableBrakingDistance =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.two_a_d_str]` (both in m²/s²), whose sum has root `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.root_term_str]` m/s; subtracting `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.a_tau_str]` m/s gives the ceiling. At `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_aisle_str]` the terms of @tbl-enforcement-stopping-budget sum to `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.total_aisle_str]`, and the remainder divided by the speed is the timing allowance.\n\n**Result**: The pre-dispatch ceiling is $\\SpeedBound =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.ceiling_str]`, below the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_drive_str]` drive limit. The candidate aisle speed leaves `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.spare_str]` of spare, equivalent to `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.t_latest_str]` of unallocated delay.\n\n**Systems insight**: The irreversibility requirement $\\StoppingDistance \\le \\ClearDistance$, rather than motor electrical capability, dictates the operating speed. Running at the nominal drive limit violates the verified spatial budget, and the speed ceiling shifts strictly through the parameters governing the quadratic root and effective latency $\\EffectiveBrakingDelay$: available clearance, qualified braking deceleration, fixed margins, and end-to-end transport lag. Any latency introduced by downstream processing directly consumes the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.t_latest_str]` timing allowance, which cannot be arbitrarily replenished.\n\n:::\n\nThe candidate transit speed, `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_aisle_str]`, sits below that ceiling, and its `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.spare_str]` margin is the only clearance left to absorb unmodeled pipeline delays or to serve as a reserve. That margin is narrow relative to the failure modes it must absorb. If chunk lease enforcement were entrusted to a non-real-time host operating system, an upstream planning stall lasting `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.t_stall_str]` instead of the nominal `[EnforcementLeaseStop.lease_ms_str]` lease would inject `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.stall_added_str]` of unbraked displacement, causing the base to overrun the rack clearance by `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.stall_breach_str]`. This hazard shows why lease termination must be autonomously enforced by dedicated microcontroller hardware directly gating motor power (@sec-enforcement-authority-refuse).\n\nThe observation-age term ends at dispatch, and the chunk lease begins at admission, so the interval between them, $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$, belongs to neither. The complete operational delay therefore expands to $\\AdmissionInclusivePrebrakeDelay = \\PrebrakeDelay + \\DispatchAdmissionInterval$, requiring an additional spatial reservation of $\\SpeedAtDetection\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$. This phase encompasses any remaining neural inference, safety envelope projection, and inter-process communication; pipelined or overlapping stages must be budgeted carefully to avoid double-counting. A newly arrived sensor observation updates this budget only when the hardware pipeline delivers and validates it prior to actuation. Laid out on one time axis (@fig-12-capture-to-onset), the observation age ends at dispatch, the dispatch-to-admission interval follows, and the lease, one tick, one bus cycle, and drive onset bring the base to brake onset. A travel row beneath converts each interval into millimeters at the candidate aisle speed.\n\nAt this candidate speed, dividing the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.spare_str]` spare distance by the speed yields `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.t_latest_str]` of unallocated timing allowance. Added to the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.t_age_str]` observation age, that allowance places the latest admission after capture at `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.capture_to_admission_max_str]`. Added to the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.tau_delay_str]` pre-brake delay, it places the latest brake onset after capture at `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.capture_to_onset_max_str]`. The dispatch-to-admission interval spends part of that allowance and leaves whatever remains as the reserve; because operating right at these boundaries spends all available clearance, the figures are qualification constraints rather than performance claims. The budget carries forward the allocation that the placement chapter makes for this interval (@sec-placement-dispatch-admission), $\\DispatchAdmissionInterval =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.delta_da_str]` for the partitioned compute pipeline. That allocation consumes `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.travel_da_str]` of the spatial margin, leaves a cushion of `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.reserve_t_str]` (`[EnforcementStoppingBudget.reserve_d_str]` of distance), and puts total latency from capture to brake onset at `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.capture_to_onset_str]`. Because that allocation is an engineering budget rather than a measurement, an instrumented campaign with hardware timestamps must confirm it before the vehicle can be certified at this speed. Read row by row (@tbl-enforcement-stopping-budget, @fig-enforcement-stopping-budget), the terms through observation age fit inside the clear distance at the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_drive_str]` drive limit, whereas the jerk-limited stop (row 6) carries the total past the line and the tracking inset (row 7) widens the breach. At the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_aisle_str]` aisle speed, those same terms stop `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.spare_str]` short of the line and still leave a `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.reserve_d_str]` reserve once dispatch-to-admission travel is charged.\n\n| Row | Added in                             | Term                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |                                        At drive limit (mm) |                                  Running total (mm) |                        Left of $\\ClearDistance$ (mm) |                                        At aisle speed (mm) |\n|----:|:-------------------------------------|:------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------:|----------------------------------------------------:|-----------------------------------------------------:|-----------------------------------------------------------:|\n|   1 | @sec-body-kinetic-momentum           | Braking, $\\SpeedAtDetection^2/2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$                                                                                                                                                                  | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.brake_drive_value_str]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.run1_value_str]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.left1_value_str]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.brake_aisle_value_str]` |\n|   2 | @sec-body-kinetic-momentum           | Overhead, $\\LocalizationBound+\\ProtectiveClearanceMargin$                                                                                                                                                                     |    `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.overhead_value_str]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.run2_value_str]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.left2_value_str]` |    `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.overhead_value_str]` |\n|   3 | @sec-body-kinetic-momentum           | Brake onset, $\\SpeedAtDetection\\ActuatorResponseTime$                                                                                                                                                                         | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.onset_drive_value_str]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.run3_value_str]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.left3_value_str]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.onset_aisle_value_str]` |\n|   — | @sec-brain-two-proposers             | Time only: the chunk policy can renew a lease; the intent model&#x27;s `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.t_intent_str]` inference cannot                                                                                          |                                                          — |                                                   — |                                                    — |                                                          — |\n|   4 | @sec-nervous-lease-sizing            | Lease path, $\\SpeedAtDetection(\\ChunkLease+\\PermissionLoopPeriod+\\CommandTransportTime)$ over `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.lease_path_str]`                                                                             | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.lease_drive_value_str]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.run4_value_str]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.left4_value_str]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.lease_aisle_value_str]` |\n|   5 | @sec-perception-latency-waterfall    | Observation age, $\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\ObservationAge$ over `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.t_age_str]`                                                                                                                     |   `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.age_drive_value_str]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.run5_value_str]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.left5_value_str]` |   `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.age_aisle_value_str]` |\n|   — | @sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion | Time only: abreast of an occluded rack end the erosion law of @eq-mem-free-space-expiry leaves no free depth, so the free-space claim lasts `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.belief_life_str]` and memory cannot certify it |                                                          — |                                                   — |                                                    — |                                                          — |\n|   6 | @sec-planning-base-stopping-budget   | Jerk-limited $\\DifferentiabilityClass^2$ stop, the jerk-ramp travel $\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration/2\\JerkLimit$ added to $\\SpeedAtDetection^2/2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$                                | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.shape_drive_value_str]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.run6_value_str]` |      `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.left6_math]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.shape_aisle_value_str]` |\n|   7 | @sec-enforcement-stopping-envelopes  | Tracking inset, $\\TrackingErrorBound$                                                                                                                                                                                         |       `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.inset_value_str]` | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.run7_value_str]` |      `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.left7_math]` |       `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.inset_value_str]` |\n|     |                                      | **Total**                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |        `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.run7_value_str]` |                                                     |                                                      | `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.total_aisle_value_str]` |\n|     |                                      | **Left of $\\ClearDistance$**                                                                                                                                                                                                  |            `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.left7_math]` |                                                     |                                                      |       `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.spare_value_str]` |\n|   8 | @sec-placement-dispatch-admission    | Dispatch-to-admission travel, $\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$ over `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.delta_da_str]`, charged against the spare at the aisle speed only                                       |                                                          — |                                                   — |                                                    — |   `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.travel_da_value_str]` |\n|     |                                      | **Reserve after row 8**                                                                                                                                                                                                       |                                                          — |                                                     |                                                      |   `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.reserve_d_value_str]` |\n\n: **The warehouse mobile manipulator&#x27;s stopping budget**: Each numbered row details the distance contributed by a physical subsystem or software stage, modeled for a loaded chassis on a dry industrial floor at the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_drive_str]` drive limit vs. the candidate `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_aisle_str]` aisle speed; the running total and clearance remainder are evaluated at the drive limit against the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.d_clear_str]` obstacle clearance. Row 6 accounts for the additional displacement imposed by jerk-limited $\\DifferentiabilityClass^2$ deceleration profiles relative to ideal constant deceleration. Time-only rows contribute latency rather than physical distance. The totals cover rows 1 to 7; row 8, the dispatch-to-admission travel $\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\DispatchAdmissionInterval$, is charged against the spare at the aisle speed only, and the last line is the reserve it leaves. The second column names the section that prices each term, so every partial total stated in earlier chapters traces to a row of this table. {#tbl-enforcement-stopping-budget tbl-colwidths=&quot;[5,17,38,10,10,10,10]&quot;}\n\n::: {#fig-enforcement-stopping-budget fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**The stopping budget, row by row**: Each bar illustrates the cumulative distance required as each successive physical and timing term is incorporated into the budget, evaluated at the vehicle drive limit under loaded conditions on dry concrete. While kinematic braking, fixed margins, actuator onset, lease execution, and perception age collectively remain within the available clearance, the jerk-limited deceleration profile causes the total to exceed the boundary, and the closed-loop tracking inset exacerbates this overrun. The final bar displays the accounted subtotal at the candidate aisle speed, where the remaining clearance must absorb the dispatch-to-admission interval before providing a net safety reserve.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Stacked bar chart of stopping distance in millimeters. Seven bars at the drive limit grow from braking alone to the full budget, adding overhead, brake onset, lease path, observation age, the matched stop suffix, and the tracking inset; the sixth and seventh bars cross a dashed horizontal line marking the clear distance at the rack end. An eighth bar at the aisle speed, built from the same terms, ends below the line.&quot;}\n\n\n\n:::\n\n::: {#fig-12-capture-to-onset data-explore=&quot;original-v4-12-capture-to-onset&quot; data-explore-key=&quot;vol4:fig-12-capture-to-onset&quot; fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**From capture to brake onset, in time and in travel**: The warehouse mobile manipulator&#x27;s permission path at the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_aisle_str]` aisle speed, drawn to scale from the exposure midpoint of the frame that first shows the hazard. The observation age of `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.t_age_str]` ends at dispatch; the hatched dispatch-to-admission interval is the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.delta_da_str]` allocation that placement makes, a budget that loaded measurement must still confirm; the lease, one tick, one bus cycle, and drive onset follow admission. The row beneath reads the same axis as travel, so the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.t_latest_str]` timing allowance and the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.spare_str]` spare are one interval. Admission later than `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.capture_to_admission_max_str]`, or onset later than `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.capture_to_onset_max_str]`, spends clearance the budget does not have. The jerk-limited stop that follows onset runs past the right edge.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Timing strip with five lanes labeled camera, perception, proposal, permission, and drive over a time axis in milliseconds. Exposure and readout bars start the camera lane, an observation-age bar ends at a dispatch marker, a hatched dispatch-to-admission bar ends at an admission marker, a lease bar ends at lease expiry followed by a short tick and bus bar, and a drive-onset bar ends at brake onset, after which a solid stop bar runs off the right edge. A bracket over the strip marks the timing allowance from dispatch to a dashed latest-admission line; a second dashed line marks the latest onset. A travel row beneath converts the axis to millimeters and brackets the same interval as the spare distance.&quot;}\n\n\n\n:::\n\nThis baseline stopping model evaluates a stationary obstacle situated at distance $\\ClearDistance$, such as a dropped warehouse tote or a pedestrian who has stepped out from an aisle rack and stopped. Under this assumption, the hazard remains fixed from the instant braking begins. If, instead, a pedestrian continues walking toward the vehicle throughout the reaction and braking phases, the obstacle actively closes the safety gap from the opposing direction. Before dispatch allocation, an ongoing pedestrian approach restricts the mobile base to a subtotal ceiling of just `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_walk_str]` at this rack end.\n\nThis drastic velocity reduction occurs because an active hazard penalizes the stopping budget by the *duration* of the stop rather than merely its displacement. Assuming an ISO 13855 approach velocity $\\HumanWalkingSpeedBound =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_human_str]` across the entire reaction and stopping interval $\\PrebrakeDelay + \\StoppingDuration(\\SpeedAtDetection)$, the spatial budget acquires an additional closure penalty $\\HumanWalkingSpeedBound\\,[\\PrebrakeDelay + \\StoppingDuration(\\SpeedAtDetection)]$ absent from static calculations. When operating below the velocity threshold $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration^2/\\JerkLimit =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_plateau_str]`, the jerk-limited braking profile cannot achieve peak deceleration $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$; it ramps deceleration up and immediately down in a triangular profile lasting $2\\sqrt{\\SpeedAtDetection/\\JerkLimit}$. This duration diminishes far more slowly than the linear scaling $\\SpeedAtDetection/\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$ as velocity decreases. Initiating a jerk-limited stop from `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_walk_str]` requires `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.t_stop_walk_str]`, compared to only `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.t_stop_walk_const_str]` for idealized step deceleration. While the mobile base travels only `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.brake_walk_str]` during braking, the pedestrian advances `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.closure_walk_str]` toward the robot over the same window. Achieving peak deceleration from such low speeds would demand a jerk of `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.jerk_rescaled_walk_str]`, several times the stipulated bound the load was assumed to tolerate, whereas unconstrained step deceleration would permit `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_walk_const_str]`. That contrast arises because the jerk bound costs differently in the two regimes. Above $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration^2/\\JerkLimit$ it adds a fixed delay of $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration/(2\\JerkLimit)$ whose travel grows with speed and forms the row-6 term pushing the drive limit past the clear distance. Below that threshold it stretches the stop&#x27;s duration. Against a person who keeps walking, duration is what the budget pays for, so reducing operating speed yields diminishing safety returns.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The pedestrian intrusion distance, evaluated for a constant-deceleration stop at the drive limit, is established in @sec-memory-belief-through-occlusion.\n:::\n\nThrottling nominal aisle velocity to the walking-person ceiling would severely degrade warehouse transport throughput for an event that facility layout and procedural crossing rules can prevent. The baseline operational design therefore mandates enforced physical or optical crossing restrictions at aisle ends, while the residual-claims register in @sec-frontier-residual-claims-register tracks the governing premise that pedestrian incursions into the stopping zone are procedurally prevented. Operating at the candidate aisle velocity remains contingent on empirical validation of these access restrictions. The derivation bounds stopping against a static obstruction; protecting against an unconstrained approaching pedestrian requires certified physical interlocks or external safety scanners.\n\nThree operating regimes therefore set three ceilings (@fig-12-stopping-vs-speed). On a dry floor, fixed allowances, linear delay travel $\\SpeedAtDetection\\PrebrakeDelay$, quadratic braking travel $\\SpeedAtDetection^2/(2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration)$, and jerk-ramp travel sum to a total that meets the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.d_clear_str]` clear distance at `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.ceiling_str]`. Even an optimistic all-weight traction screen requires a floor friction coefficient of at least `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.mu_min_str]`, because $\\TractionLimitedDeceleration = \\min(\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration, \\CoulombFrictionCoefficient \\Gravity)$ assumes all weight contributes braking traction. The running machine also requires qualified driven-wheel load share, torque, and stability (@sec-body-kinetic-momentum). On a sealed floor inspected to $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.mu_inspected_str]`, the ceiling falls to `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.ceiling_inspected_str]`. A pedestrian walking toward the base at `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_human_str]` charges the stop by its duration and contracts the ceiling to `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_walk_str]`. Because these three roots are subtotal ceilings before dispatch allocation, charging the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.delta_da_str]` allocation lowers each of them. The dry-floor root falls to `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.ceiling_complete_str]` by adding the base&#x27;s travel through dispatch. The optimistic inspected-floor root falls to `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.ceiling_inspected_complete_str]` in the same way. The walking-person root falls to `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_walk_complete_str]` by adding both the base&#x27;s travel and the person&#x27;s closure across that interval. Because operating at any maximum root spends all clearance and leaves zero reserve, the remaining reserve must be recomputed for the selected speed, floor, and hazard motion. The `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.reserve_t_str]` timing reserve belongs specifically to the dry, static-obstacle `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_aisle_str]` candidate, and an authority transition cannot claim its original `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.t_latest_str]` margin a second time.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The authority transitions that must fit inside this remaining reserve are developed in @sec-intervention-authority-transitions.\n:::\n\n::: {#fig-12-stopping-vs-speed fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Stopping subtotals against speed, and the three pre-dispatch ceilings**: The stacked bands build the dry-floor stop from the fixed allowances, the travel during the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.tau_delay_str]` pre-brake delay, constant-deceleration braking, and the extra travel of the jerk ramps. Delay travel grows linearly with speed and braking travel quadratically, so the total meets the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.d_clear_str]` clear distance at `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.ceiling_str]`. The dashed curve applies the optimistic all-weight traction screen for a floor inspected to $\\CoulombFrictionCoefficient =$ `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.mu_inspected_str]`, moving the ceiling to `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.ceiling_inspected_str]`. The dotted curve adds the closure of a person walking toward the base through the delay and the stop, which charges the stop by its duration and gives a pre-dispatch ceiling of `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_walk_str]`. All three curves omit dispatch-to-admission travel; complete scenario ceilings are lower.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Plot of stopping distance in millimeters against speed in meters per second. Four stacked bands, a flat band of fixed allowances, a linearly growing band of delay travel, a quadratic braking band, and a thin band of jerk-ramp travel, rise to a solid total curve. A dashed curve for a low-friction floor and a dotted curve for a walking person rise more steeply. A dashed horizontal line marks the clear distance, and three markers where the curves cross it are labeled with their speed ceilings.&quot;}\n\n\n\n:::\n\nUnlike those software-leased stopping ceilings, an independent protective safety scanner operates under its own hardware-to-actuation timing budget. When an obstacle breaches a safety-rated zone, the scanner&#x27;s internal optical sampling, fail-safe output switching, safety controller logic, and drive disconnect delays entirely bypass the perception dispatch and software lease infrastructure. However, the physical response must still reserve the same underlying deceleration distance, mechanical tracking error, localization tolerance, and protective safety cushion; only computational delays bypassed by the safety bus are eliminated from the sum. Industrial mobile bases deploy such protective fields under ISO 3691-4 [@iso3691part4] for driverless industrial trucks and ISO 13849-1 [@iso13849part1] for safety-related control circuits, using a safety-rated planar scanner of the kind in @fig-12-real-laser-scanner, which evaluates programmable warning and protective fields. The physical dimensions of this protective field must be calculated from the dedicated scanner path&#x27;s verified hardware timing budget, rather than inheriting the software lease and vision latencies of the neural planner. The safety-rated optical scanner is a component of a separately qualified protective function, distinct from the primary perception LiDAR that generates non-safety-rated point clouds for neural proposal generation. Its component rating does not certify the complete sensor-to-stop path. In the running architecture, that path must establish the detection coverage, response time, and physical stopping performance required for protection at rack intersections. @Sec-enforcement-certified-functions maps the software stopping budget onto this certified hardware baseline and shows that industrial vehicle testing standards validate pedestrian detection against the same static-obstacle assumption derived here.\n\n::: {#fig-12-real-laser-scanner fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Planar laser scanner**: A rotating mirror sweeps a time-of-flight beam across a horizontal fan, so the device reports range over one plane around the base. A safety-rated scanner of this form adds an early warning field and a protective field. The warning field requests earlier deceleration; the protective field&#x27;s safety output feeds a controller that selects a risk-assessed stop. Protective-field length must include scanner response, controller and drive delay, braking travel, and uncertainty; a moving base does not become safe merely because motor torque is removed. (Credit: [Björn Heller](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sick_laserscanner.jpg), Wikimedia Commons, [CC BY-SA 3.0 DE](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/de/deed.en).)&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Photograph of a blue industrial laser scanner on an office desk beside a keyboard and mouse. A boxy upper housing carries a small status window and the maker&#x27;s label; below it a black cylindrical window wraps around the base, behind which the beam sweeps.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\n### Phase portraits and speed ceilings {#sec-enforcement-phase-portraits}\n\nWhether enforced through the software lease path or a dedicated scanner field, the stopping budget cannot be reduced to a fixed scalar velocity limit once obstacle distance shrinks: a speed that is recoverable two meters from the rack end becomes an inevitable collision eighty centimeters closer. Drawn in the distance--velocity $(\\ClearDistance, \\SpeedAtDetection)$ phase plane (@fig-12-stopping-envelope-phase-plane), the plotted pre-dispatch envelope separates states whose accounted subtotal fits from states whose subtotal already exceeds clearance. Complete admission contracts that boundary further by charging dispatch travel. Braking alone traces the outer parabola. The inset boundary pulls the viable set, the recoverable set drawn in this plane, inward by the pre-brake travel $\\SpeedAtDetection\\PrebrakeDelay$, the jerk ramps of the matched stop, and the fixed localization, protective, and tracking allowances. At the rack-end clear distance the height of that plotted boundary is the subtotal speed ceiling, with the candidate aisle speed beneath it. The complete dry-floor ceiling is `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.ceiling_complete_str]`; the governor must use the fully charged boundary. Under the verified delay and deceleration bounds, the envelope is therefore a velocity ceiling that varies with clearance, and a state outside it violates the stopping reservation even if an emergency maneuver might avoid a collision in an edge case.\n\n::: {#fig-12-stopping-envelope-phase-plane fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Conditional stopping subtotal before dispatch**: For the warehouse mobile manipulator&#x27;s base, a boundary separates states with modeled braking clearance from states that have already exhausted it. The inset includes the pre-brake delay, the matched stop suffix&#x27;s jerk ramps, and the localization, protective, and tracking allowances. Clearance below the fixed allowances admits no approach speed, including zero. The curves use the accounted delay and stated braking envelope; unaccounted dispatch-to-admission time lowers the ceiling further.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Phase portrait with obstacle distance on the horizontal axis and approach speed on the vertical axis. A braking-only parabola and an inset stopping boundary show the cost of pre-brake delay, the matched stop suffix, and the fixed allowances; a marker at the rack-end clear distance shows the speed ceiling, with the chosen aisle speed just below it.&quot;}\n\n\n\n:::\n\nEvaluated on every enforcer tick, the envelope becomes a speed governor that runs in two stages, and their order matters:\n\n1. **Current-state feasibility check**: The enforcer first verifies whether the robot&#x27;s current operational state $(\\ClearDistance, \\SpeedAtDetection)$ remains strictly inside the envelope. It tests whether a full braking response from the current velocity $\\SpeedAtDetection$, with all transport lags, jerk transitions, and margin allowances, fits within the currently measured clearance $\\ClearDistance$. If this condition fails, no newly proposed command setpoint can restore the lost stopping reservation, because reducing the target acceleration cannot retroactively erase the kinetic energy or displacement accrued from the existing physical velocity. A violation at this step immediately selects `STOP`, the resident controlled stop that @sec-enforcement-fallback-ladder defines among the enforcer&#x27;s fallback responses.\n2. **Candidate command projection**: If and only if the current state passes validation, the enforcer samples the speed ceiling $\\SpeedBound(\\ClearDistance)$ from the fully charged counterpart of the pre-dispatch boundary shown in @fig-12-stopping-envelope-phase-plane. It then clamps the candidate velocity proposal $\\CandidateSpeedCommand$ to the admissible ceiling, admitting $\\AdmittedSpeedCommand = \\min\\bigl(\\CandidateSpeedCommand, \\max(0, \\SpeedBound)\\bigr)$. Rather than discarding an over-speed proposal entirely, this governor projects the command onto the boundary of the safe set.\n\nIf obstacle clearance shrinks, computational jitter expands end-to-end latency, or floor friction degrades, the envelope boundary shifts inward, throttling admissible velocity on the subsequent control cycle when qualified sensing or configuration evidence exposes the change. A reference clamp alone does not establish that actual velocity remains inside the envelope between checks. This one-dimensional velocity governor is the scalar prototype for multi-dimensional safety filters; in @sec-enforcement-safe-sets-conditional-permission, control barrier functions extend this exact principle to enforce forward invariance over full state spaces.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The clearance-parameterized velocity ceiling is established as a kinematic constraint in @sec-body-dynamic-velocity-ceilings.\n:::\n\nThe stopping envelope is the kinematic roofline of @sec-boundary-kinematic-roofline carried through the finished budget. Physical clearance ($\\ClearDistance$) sets a ceiling on transit speed for the qualified braking, margin, and timing bounds. A faster inference frame rate raises that ceiling only if it reduces a delay charged to the stopping budget. Model size and inference optimizations affect the ceiling through their share of the sensor-to-actuator delay $\\PrebrakeDelay$. On the warehouse mobile manipulator, every `[EnforcementLeaseStop.tick_ms_str]` of added pipeline delay consumes `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.missed_tick_str]` of spatial clearance at nominal aisle velocity, and an unbudgeted 10 ms latency increase depresses the allowable speed ceiling by approximately `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.ceiling_drop_10ms_str]` mm/s. Deploying an oversized vision-language model that increases latency therefore directly lowers maximum transit speed, whereas an inference improvement that does not change the scheduled delivery tick may leave the charged delay unchanged. The speed ceiling shifts materially only when altering the physical parameters of the plant: verified deceleration authority $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$, end-to-end transport lag $\\PrebrakeDelay$, tracking error bound $\\TrackingErrorBound$, and localization uncertainty $\\LocalizationBound$.\n\nNone of these governing parameters remain stationary during real-world operation. Manipulating a heavy payload in the end-effector increases arm inertia, diminishing joint deceleration limits within peak motor torque bounds. Industrial floor contaminants such as oil or water severely degrade tire traction beneath the assumed braking limit $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$, while aggressive operational cycles heat motor windings and drive inverters into thermal derating. A safety enforcer that treats $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$ as an immutable datasheet constant will dangerously underestimate stopping distance under heavy workloads. Runtime safety monitors must update braking bounds as observable, qualified payload and thermal states evolve. Instantaneous wheel-floor friction is not measured on this machine; an unobserved floor change invalidates the inspection premise and requires an operating restriction rather than automatic adaptation. In either case, relying on unvalidated, optimistic estimates invalidates the formal safety contract.\n\nDynamic adaptation remains effective only as long as state estimators remain uncorrupted and physical actuators retain control authority. If severe surface contamination reduces friction toward zero, or if sustained high-torque demands push drive inverters beyond their thermal trip thresholds, physical braking authority collapses. When required stopping torque exceeds what the physical hardware can exert against the environment, software can still detect the boundary violation, but it can no longer prevent an impact.\n\n## Authority Exhaustion and Emergency Fallback {#sec-enforcement-exhausted-authority}\n\nA loaded arm descending toward the packing station must counter gravity before any torque remains to slow its motion. **Actuator saturation** occurs when requested effort reaches the electrical, mechanical, or thermal limits of the physical body, which can cause downstream integral controllers to accumulate windup. The actuator cannot deliver the corrective control action $\\CertifiedControlAction$ at its commanded magnitude in any of several cases. Motor phase currents may hit inverter current-chopping limits ($\\InverterCurrentLimit$). The DC bus may sag under inductive switching transients ($\\PowerRailInductance\\,d\\ConductorCurrent/d\\TimeInstant$). Back-EMF at high rotor speeds may collapse voltage headroom ($\\DcRailVoltage - \\MotorBackEmfConstant \\RotorAngularSpeed \\to 0$). Winding temperatures may approach thermal derating thresholds ($\\WindingTemperature \\to \\WindingTemperatureLimit$). No mathematical safety boundary can be defended with control effort the physical body cannot generate.\n\n: **Integrator Windup**: A proportional-integral controller&#x27;s integral term continues summing tracking error while an actuator sits at its saturation limit, as standard feedback loops do not inherently know that command outputs are clipped. When tracking error reverses, this accumulated state must unwind before the actuator command can leave the saturation rail. That unwinding delay directly consumes safety clearance. While modern anti-windup schemes clamp or back-calculate integrator state during saturation [@astrom2021feedback], the safety enforcer must still budget whatever residual unwinding delay remains in the low-level hardware controller. \n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The electrical and transmission limits that bound delivered actuator effort are established in @sec-body-actuator-limits.\n:::\n\nWhen required braking effort exceeds physical actuator limits, no safety filter can enforce the promised stopping horizon. An emergency fallback invoked after control authority is lost can mitigate damage, but it cannot restore an invariant guarantee that physical dynamics have already broken. The runtime enforcer must therefore contract its forward reservation *before* actuator effort saturates, preserving a feasible braking margin under worst-case loading.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ AUTHORITY LOSS ON THE MACHINE: ARM BIAS TORQUE AND BASE WINDUP</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: the descending-arm and windup paragraphs immediately below;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          reused by calc-enforcement-kinetic-barrier.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    show that bias torque quarters the arm's braking and that windup</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          delay on the base overruns the spare.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    arm stop with and without bias; unbraked windup travel vs. spare.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Scenarios.LoadedArmJointGravityStop (RM.arm.max_torque and the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          stated joint inertia, lever, speed, and bias share);</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          Scenarios.WarehouseIntegratorUnwind (the finished budget at the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          aisle speed, S-curve stop included, with the unwind as added delay).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>import math from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_velocity, fmt_torque, fmt_inertia, fmt_percent, check</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle JOINT = Scenarios.LoadedArmJointGravityStop UNWIND = Scenarios.WarehouseIntegratorUnwind</p>\n<p>class EnforcementAuthorityLoss:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>torque_limit = JOINT.torque_limit  # category: datasheet (peak torque, axes 1-4) tcp_limit = RM.tcp_speed_limit  # category: chosen inertia = JOINT.inertia  # scenario assumption; category: illustrative (joint with payload) link_length = JOINT.lever  # scenario assumption; category: illustrative joint_speed = JOINT.joint_speed  # rad/s; scenario assumption; category: illustrative bias_fraction = JOINT.bias_fraction  # scenario assumption; share of peak torque holding the load on descent; category: illustrative windup_span = 200 * millisecond  # scenario assumption; category: illustrative unwind = UNWIND.added_delay  # scenario assumption; category: illustrative v_aisle = AISLE.v_aisle  # category: chosen</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>bias = JOINT.bias net_torque = JOINT.torque_descending alpha_nom = JOINT.alpha_free alpha_eff = JOINT.alpha_descending angle_nom = JOINT.stop_angle(alpha_nom) angle_eff = JOINT.stop_angle(alpha_eff) d_nom = JOINT.tool_stop_free d_eff = JOINT.tool_stop_descending overrun = JOINT.overrun tcp_speed = JOINT.tcp_speed windup_travel = UNWIND.result.added_delay_travel.to(millimeter) spare = UNWIND.result.spare.to(millimeter) windup_excess = (windup_travel - spare).to(millimeter)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(bias.magnitude - 65.25) &lt; 1e-9, \"Bias is 65.25 of 87 N·m.\") check(abs(alpha_nom.magnitude / alpha_eff.magnitude - 4.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"Bias quarters the braking deceleration.\") check(abs(d_eff.magnitude / d_nom.magnitude - 4.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"Bias quadruples the tool stopping travel.\") check(tcp_speed &lt;= tcp_limit, \"The descending tool point stays inside the free-space TCP limit.\") check(abs(windup_travel.magnitude - 195.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"Windup at 1.3 m/s adds 195 mm.\") check(abs(spare.magnitude - 131.0075) &lt; 1e-6, \"The finished budget leaves 131.01 mm spare at 1.3 m/s.\") check(windup_travel &gt; spare, \"Windup travel exceeds the spare.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>torque_limit_str = fmt_torque(torque_limit, precision=0) bias_str = fmt_torque(bias, precision=2) net_torque_str = fmt_torque(net_torque, precision=2) bias_pct_str = fmt_percent(bias_fraction, precision=0, style=\"prose\") inertia_str = fmt_inertia(inertia, precision=0) link_length_str = fmt_length(link_length, unit=meter, precision=2) joint_speed_str = fmt(joint_speed.magnitude, precision=1) tcp_speed_str = fmt_velocity(tcp_speed, precision=2) tcp_limit_str = fmt_velocity(tcp_limit, precision=0) alpha_nom_str = fmt(alpha_nom.magnitude, precision=0) alpha_eff_str = fmt(alpha_eff.magnitude, precision=2) angle_nom_str = fmt(angle_nom, precision=4) angle_eff_str = fmt(angle_eff, precision=4) angle_nom_deg_str = fmt(math.degrees(angle_nom), precision=2) angle_eff_deg_str = fmt(math.degrees(angle_eff), precision=2) d_nom_str = fmt_length(d_nom, unit=millimeter, precision=2) d_eff_str = fmt_length(d_eff, unit=millimeter, precision=2) overrun_str = fmt_length(overrun, unit=millimeter, precision=2) windup_span_str = fmt_latency(windup_span, unit=millisecond, precision=0) unwind_str = fmt_latency(unwind, unit=millisecond, precision=0) v_aisle_str = fmt_velocity(v_aisle, precision=1) windup_travel_str = fmt_length(windup_travel, unit=millimeter, precision=0) windup_excess_str = fmt_length(windup_excess, unit=millimeter, precision=2) spare_str = fmt_length(spare, unit=millimeter, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<p>A stopping envelope based on peak torque alone overstates braking authority when gravity drives the moving load toward a hazard. Under a steady-state bias torque $\\JointBiasTorque$, the effective deceleration available to arrest angular motion collapses: $$\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration = \\frac{\\JointTorqueLimit - \\JointBiasTorque}{\\EffectiveJointInertia}$$ {#eq-enforce-effective-deceleration} where $\\EffectiveJointInertia$ is mechanism inertia and $\\JointTorqueLimit$ is the peak dynamic torque limit. In linear translational systems with mass $\\Mass$ and bias force $\\ActuatorBiasEffort$, @eq-enforce-effective-deceleration takes the equivalent form $\\NetBrakingDeceleration = (\\MaximumActuatorEffort - \\lvert \\ActuatorBiasEffort\\rvert) / \\Mass$. For motion aligned with the bias, a payload consuming <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.bias_pct_str]</code> of peak opposing torque leaves only the remaining quarter for active braking, quadrupling the constant-deceleration stopping distance at that speed.</p>\n<p>Gravitational and dynamic biases are directionally asymmetric. When motion opposes gravity (such as during an upward arm swing), gravity assists motor braking, yielding $\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration = (\\JointTorqueLimit + \\JointBiasTorque) / \\EffectiveJointInertia$. Conversely, when motion aligns with the bias (such as a loaded arm descending toward a workstation), gravity directly opposes deceleration, collapsing braking headroom to $\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration = (\\JointTorqueLimit - \\JointBiasTorque) / \\EffectiveJointInertia$. Because safety certificates must guarantee forward invariance in the worst-case physical direction, the runtime enforcer must evaluate $\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration$ with directional sign awareness rather than treating braking authority as an isotropic scalar. Suppose the warehouse mobile manipulator carries a mug from the takeaway conveyor down toward a coworker at the packing station. Its tool center point (TCP) moves at <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.tcp_speed_str]</code>, within its <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.tcp_limit_str]</code> free-space limit (only the final approach to the coworker's hand runs at the reduced handover speed derived in @sec-enforcement-intentional-contact). Gravitational bias during this descent quadruples the tool's stopping distance (notebook \\ref{nbk-enforcement-gravity-braking-tax}).</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: The loaded joint descends toward the packing station at $\\JointAngularSpeed_0$. How far does the tool point travel before the joint stops when the whole peak torque brakes, and how far when gravity on the arm and mug holds part of it?</p>\n<p><strong>Variables</strong>:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>$\\JointAngularSpeed_0 =$ <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.joint_speed_str]</code> rad/s and $\\LeverArmLength =$ <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.link_length_str]</code> (Illustrative; the joint speed and effective link lever arm of the scenario, see the Reader Guide).</li>\n<li>$\\EffectiveJointInertia =$ <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.inertia_str]</code> (Illustrative; joint inertia with the mug).</li>\n<li>$\\JointTorqueLimit =$ <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.torque_limit_str]</code> (Datasheet; peak torque of the registered arm's axes 1 to 4).</li>\n<li>$\\JointBiasTorque =$ <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.bias_str]</code>, <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.bias_pct_str]</code> of peak torque (Illustrative; gravity on the arm and mug in the direction of motion).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: With no bias, $\\UnbiasedAngularBrakingDeceleration = \\JointTorqueLimit/\\EffectiveJointInertia =$ <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.alpha_nom_str]</code> rad/s², the stopping angle is $\\JointAngularSpeed_0^2/(2\\UnbiasedAngularBrakingDeceleration) =$ <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.angle_nom_str]</code> rad (<code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.angle_nom_deg_str]</code>$^\\circ$), and the tool point travels $\\LeverArmLength\\,\\FiniteChange\\JointAngle =$ <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.d_nom_str]</code>. On the descent, @eq-enforce-effective-deceleration leaves $\\JointTorqueLimit - \\JointBiasTorque =$ <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.net_torque_str]</code> for braking, so $\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration =$ <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.alpha_eff_str]</code> rad/s², the stopping angle is <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.angle_eff_str]</code> rad (<code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.angle_eff_deg_str]</code>$^\\circ$), and the tool travels <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.d_eff_str]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Result</strong>: The descending stop is four times the unbiased one, an unbudgeted overrun of <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.overrun_str]</code> for an enforcer that assumes the datasheet torque.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Stopping distance scales as $1/\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration$, so a bias that takes <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.bias_pct_str]</code> of the torque quadruples the braking term of $\\StoppingDistance$ at the same speed. The certificate must use the net authority in the worst direction, not the actuator's rating.</p>\n<p>The asymmetry is direction, not magnitude (@fig-12-arm-bias-braking). On the descent, gravity torque acts with the motion and leaves only <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.net_torque_str]</code> of the motor's peak for braking, while the same joint moving up recruits gravity and stops in less distance than the unbiased case. Whether the descending overrun reaches the workstation depends on the clearance left when braking begins, the margin the barrier certificate of @sec-enforcement-safe-sets-conditional-permission evaluates. The coworker's hand must never fall within the arm's biased stopping distance of <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.d_eff_str]</code> plus its blind travel during sensing and communication delay before the handover channel engages.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ DESCENDING ARM UNDER BRAKING: BIAS TORQUE AND DIRECTIONAL ASYMMETRY (FIGURE)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @fig-12-arm-bias-braking in @sec-enforcement-exhausted-authority,\n# │          after calc-enforcement-authority-loss.\n# │ Goal:    Show why gravity quarters braking on the way down and helps on the\n# │          way up, at the same peak torque.\n# │ Show:    (a) free-body sketch of the loaded joint; (b) braking torque left\n# │          and tool stopping travel for no bias, descending, and ascending.\n# │ How:     Every value reads EnforcementAuthorityLoss; ascending adds the\n# │          bias (eq-enforce-effective-deceleration with the opposite sign).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nimport math\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom matplotlib.patches import FancyArrowPatch, Arc\nfrom mlsysim import viz\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check\n\nL = EnforcementAuthorityLoss\n_nm = lambda q: q.to(newton * meter).magnitude\nt_max, t_bias = _nm(L.torque_limit), _nm(L.bias)\nJ = L.inertia.to(kilogram * meter**2).magnitude\nw0 = L.joint_speed.to(1 / second).magnitude\nell = L.link_length.to(meter).magnitude\ncases = [  # label, braking torque left, role\n    (&quot;no bias&quot;, t_max, &quot;#4A5568&quot;),\n    (&quot;descending\\n(with gravity)&quot;, t_max - t_bias, &quot;#A51C30&quot;),\n    (&quot;ascending\\n(against gravity)&quot;, t_max + t_bias, &quot;#1A4D3E&quot;),\n]\nalpha = [tq / J for _, tq, _ in cases]\nstop_mm = [1000 * ell * w0**2 / (2 * a) for a in alpha]\ncheck(abs(stop_mm[0] - L.d_nom.to(millimeter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;no-bias stop matches d_nom&quot;)\ncheck(abs(stop_mm[1] - L.d_eff.to(millimeter).magnitude) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;descending stop matches d_eff&quot;)\ncheck(abs(alpha[1] - L.alpha_eff.magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;descending deceleration matches alpha_eff&quot;)\n\nINK, SOFT = &quot;#1A202C&quot;, &quot;#4A5568&quot;\nviz.set_book_style()\nfig, (ax, bx) = plt.subplots(2, 1, figsize=(6.2, 6.6), gridspec_kw=dict(height_ratios=[1.2, 1], hspace=0.3))\n# (a) free-body sketch of the loaded joint, descending (clockwise motion)\nax.set_aspect(&quot;equal&quot;)\nax.axis(&quot;off&quot;)\nth = math.radians(-25)\nex, ey = math.cos(th), math.sin(th)\nax.plot([0, ex], [0, ey], color=&quot;#334155&quot;, lw=5, solid_capstyle=&quot;butt&quot;, zorder=2)\nax.add_patch(plt.Circle((0, 0), 0.07, fc=&quot;white&quot;, ec=&quot;#334155&quot;, lw=1.5, zorder=3))\nax.add_patch(plt.Rectangle((ex - 0.07, ey - 0.09), 0.14, 0.12, fc=&quot;#F8FAFC&quot;, ec=&quot;#334155&quot;, lw=1.2, zorder=3))\nax.annotate(&quot;&quot;, xy=(ex, ey - 0.5), xytext=(ex, ey - 0.1), arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=&quot;-|&gt;&quot;, color=SOFT, lw=1.3))\nax.text(ex + 0.06, ey - 0.42, &quot;mg&quot;, fontsize=9, color=SOFT, style=&quot;italic&quot;)\n\ndef arc(r, a0, a1, col, rad):\n    p0 = (r * math.cos(math.radians(a0)), r * math.sin(math.radians(a0)))\n    p1 = (r * math.cos(math.radians(a1)), r * math.sin(math.radians(a1)))\n    ax.add_patch(FancyArrowPatch(p0, p1, connectionstyle=f&quot;arc3,rad={rad}&quot;, arrowstyle=&quot;-|&gt;,head_length=5,head_width=3&quot;, color=col, lw=1.3))\n\narc(1.3, 22, -12, &quot;#A51C30&quot;, -0.2)  # gravity bias: clockwise, with the motion\narc(0.55, -45, 8, &quot;#1A4D3E&quot;, 0.2)  # motor braking: counterclockwise, against it\n# motion: clockwise, so the tool point moves down along the tangent\nax.annotate(&quot;&quot;, xy=(ex - 0.15 - 0.13, ey - 0.12 - 0.28), xytext=(ex - 0.15, ey - 0.12), arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=&quot;-|&gt;&quot;, color=INK, lw=1.3))\nax.text(1.36, 0.14, r&quot;$\\tau_{\\mathrm{bias}}$&quot;, fontsize=9, color=&quot;#A51C30&quot;)\nax.text(1.36, 0.0, &quot;%.2f N·m&quot; % t_bias, fontsize=9, color=&quot;#A51C30&quot;)\nax.text(1.36, -0.12, &quot;gravity&quot;, fontsize=9, color=&quot;#A51C30&quot;)\nax.text(0.5, 0.26, r&quot;$\\tau_{\\max}$ = %.0f N·m&quot; % t_max, fontsize=9, color=&quot;#1A4D3E&quot;)\nax.text(0.5, 0.14, &quot;motor, braking&quot;, fontsize=9, color=&quot;#1A4D3E&quot;)\nax.text(ex - 0.32, ey - 0.4, r&quot;$\\omega$ (descending)&quot;, fontsize=9, color=INK, ha=&quot;right&quot;)\nax.set_xlim(-0.2, 1.9)\nax.set_ylim(-1.0, 0.55)\nax.text(-0.2, 0.55, &quot;(a) Loaded joint, descending&quot;, fontsize=9, fontweight=&quot;bold&quot;, va=&quot;bottom&quot;)\n# (b) braking torque left and stopping travel\ny = np.arange(len(cases))[::-1]\nfor yi, (lab, tq, col), a, d in zip(y, cases, alpha, stop_mm):\n    bx.barh(yi, tq, height=0.55, color=col, alpha=0.9)\n    bx.text(tq + 3, yi + 0.05, f&quot;{tq:.2f} N·m  →  α = {a:.2f} rad/s²&quot;, fontsize=9, va=&quot;bottom&quot;, color=INK)\n    bx.text(tq + 3, yi - 0.05, f&quot;tool stops in {d:.2f} mm&quot;, fontsize=9, va=&quot;top&quot;, color=col)\nbx.set_yticks(y)\nbx.set_yticklabels([c[0] for c in cases], fontsize=9)\nbx.set_xlim(0, (t_max + t_bias) * 1.75)\nbx.set_xlabel(&quot;torque left for braking (N·m)&quot;, fontsize=9)\nbx.tick_params(axis=&quot;x&quot;, labelsize=9)\nfor s in (&quot;top&quot;, &quot;right&quot;):\n    bx.spines[s].set_visible(False)\nbx.grid(False)\nbx.set_title(&quot;(b) Same motor, three directions&quot;, fontsize=9, fontweight=&quot;bold&quot;, loc=&quot;left&quot;)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\nplt.close()\n]```\n\n:::\n\nAlongside external force biases like gravity, an unprotected integrator downstream of the safety enforcer can introduce severe latency during actuator saturation. If clipping does not halt integration, tracking error continues to accumulate in the controller state. When the safety system subsequently requests maximum braking, the command must pass through this wound-up state rather than bypassing it. The actuator command consequently remains pinned at its forward saturation limit while the integrator unwinds. Take an illustrative case in which tracking error accumulates for `[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.windup_span_str]` and the unwinding phase then delays net braking deceleration by $\\IntegratorUnwindDelay =$ `[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.unwind_str]`. The two durations are independent assumptions rather than one derived from the other; the unwind time depends on the integrator gain and on how sharply the error reverses, and only a test of the actual drive fixes it. At an aisle speed of `[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.v_aisle_str]`, this latency adds $\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\IntegratorUnwindDelay =$ `[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.windup_travel_str]` of unbraked travel. That overrun exceeds the system&#x27;s entire `[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.spare_str]` unallocated clearance buffer by `[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.windup_excess_str]`, even before dispatch-to-admission latency is charged. Classical techniques such as integrator clamping, back-calculation, and conditional integration prevent windup in the feedback loop [@astrom2006advanced; @franklin1998digital]. The enforcement architecture, however, must explicitly quantify any residual unwinding delay and budget the corresponding unbraked distance into the stopping horizon.\n\nActuator saturation is one of the few hardware limitations with an immediate, deterministic detector inside the permission path. On each `[EnforcementLeaseStop.tick_ms_str]` tick, the permission path compares the commanded effort $\\CommandedActuatorEffort$ against the realizable actuator envelope: $\\RealizedActuatorEffort = \\text{clip}(\\CommandedActuatorEffort, \\MinimumActuatorEffort, \\MaximumActuatorEffort)$. A nonzero residual $\\CommandedActuatorEffort - \\RealizedActuatorEffort \\neq 0$ or an active drive-stage current-limit flag directly reveals that the requested effort has been clipped. This comparison detects saturation at the command interface, however, rather than measuring delivered shaft torque or tire-ground traction. Qualified physical evidence (phase currents, DC bus voltage, thermal derating thresholds, and kinematic state telemetry) must support a conservative lower bound on available directional braking deceleration. An upper actuator-effort ceiling limits possible braking; it does not establish the braking that a stop may credit, and a clipping detector does not measure tire-floor traction.\n\nBecause degraded authority immediately lengthens stopping distance, the enforcer updates its braking envelope in real time. Before admitting the next trajectory segment, the filter recomputes the effective deceleration $\\NetBrakingDeceleration = (\\MaximumActuatorEffort - \\lvert \\ActuatorBiasEffort\\rvert) / \\Mass$. Allowable vehicle velocity scales down as authority contracts; if the recalculated stopping distance no longer fits within the confirmed clear distance, the enforcer must command immediate deceleration while sufficient braking headroom remains.\n\nAuthority exhaustion must also be communicated upstream to the planner. If a `[EnforcementLeaseStop.chunk_rate_hz_str]` $\\text{Hz}$ trajectory generator continues commanding aggressive accelerations while the low-level `[EnforcementLeaseStop.loop_rate_str]` loop reports saturation, the policy is planning trajectories for a virtual body that the physical robot cannot realize. The permission path treats saturation as an active constraint violation. It revokes the active trajectory lease and forces a replan initialized from the machine&#x27;s actual, authority-constrained dynamic state.\n\n::: {#psp-enforcement-actuator-authority .callout-perspective title=&quot;The actuator authority invariant&quot;}\nA control barrier function, as formulated in @sec-appendix-control-cbf, remains mathematically valid only while physical actuators retain sufficient current, voltage, and thermal headroom to deliver the computed corrective effort: $\\ActuatorEffort^* \\in [\\MinimumActuatorEffort, \\MaximumActuatorEffort]$. When steady-state gravity, friction, or payload inertia encroaches on this operating envelope, the runtime enforcer must dynamically contract the safe set before marginal control authority vanishes.\n:::\n\n::: {#chk-enforce-actuator-saturation-headroom .callout-checkpoint title=&quot;Stopping envelope and actuator headroom&quot;}\n\nConsider how the stopping envelope, and the actuator authority beneath it, bound permissible motion:\n\n- [ ] **Quadratic envelope scaling**: Why does the stopping budget grow faster than speed, so that the finished budget, rather than the drive limit, sets the aisle speed, and why can no planning policy bypass this kinematic floor?\n- [ ] **Transport lag vs. braking estimate**: At the `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_aisle_str]` aisle speed, what clearance is spent during $\\PrebrakeDelay$ ($\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\PrebrakeDelay$) and during constant-deceleration braking ($\\SpeedAtDetection^2/2\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$), and what is the speed below which the delay term dominates?\n- [ ] **Effective deceleration collapse**: What is the physical mechanism that causes stopping distance to quadruple when steady-state gravity consumes `[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.bias_pct_str]` of peak actuator torque?\n- [ ] **Dynamic safe set contraction**: Why must the permission path actively shrink the admissible state space when an actuator approaches thermal or electrical saturation, rather than waiting for tracking error to breach a hard limit?\n:::\n\n## Safe Sets as Conditional Permission {#sec-enforcement-safe-sets-conditional-permission}\n\nOnce gravitational bias torque is counted, the descending arm analyzed in @sec-enforcement-exhausted-authority requires `[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.d_eff_str]` of tool travel to brake to a complete stop, and this stopping distance scales quadratically with approach speed. A clearance margin that guarantees safety at one velocity fails completely at a higher velocity. The runtime safety enforcer therefore requires a joint predicate over position and velocity that it can evaluate synchronously on every tick of the control clock.\n\nWhile a stopping budget establishes a static physical margin, a control barrier function transforms that spatial margin into an instantaneous constraint on the next actuator command [@ames2014control; @ames2019control]. This formulation belongs to the broader family of **safety filters** that monitor candidate action proposals against certified fallbacks, intervening only when necessary [@hsu2024safetyfilter] so that the machine can execute aggressive tasks while guaranteeing sufficient braking authority to halt.\n\n: **Barrier Function**: In constrained optimization, an interior barrier term such as $-\\log(-\\InequalityConstraintFunction(\\OptimizationIterate))$ grows without bound near constraint boundaries, preventing numerical iterates from escaping the feasible set. Barrier certificates extended this mathematical principle to dynamical systems, proving that no trajectory starting in a safe set can ever reach an unsafe state [@prajna2004barrier]. Control barrier functions subsequently formulated this certificate as an explicit, affine condition on the instantaneous control input [@wieland2007constructive]. While an optimization algorithm&#x27;s safety guarantee holds because its numerical iterates are evaluated exactly in software, a physical plant&#x27;s safety guarantee holds only as long as actuator limits, dynamics, and real-time execution match the model assumed by the certificate. \n\nA **safe set** $\\ForwardInvariantSafeSet$ collects all system states for which a scalar certificate $\\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState)$ remains nonnegative. Its boundary represents zero margin, whereas its interior provides positive margin. For the manipulator arm, both position and velocity must enter this certificate because a physical clearance that safely accommodates a slow approach must reject a high-speed approach at the identical position:\n$$\\ForwardInvariantSafeSet = \\{\\MechanicalPlantState \\in \\PlantStateSpace \\mid \\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState) \\ge 0\\}, \\quad \\SafetyBoundaryManifold = \\{\\MechanicalPlantState \\in \\PlantStateSpace \\mid \\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState) = 0\\}, \\quad \\text{Int}(\\ForwardInvariantSafeSet) = \\{\\MechanicalPlantState \\in \\PlantStateSpace \\mid \\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState) &gt; 0\\}.$$\nEnsuring that trajectories remain inside $\\ForwardInvariantSafeSet$ is known as forward invariance, the physical counterpart to an **inductive loop invariant** in formal software verification:\n$$\\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState_0) \\ge 0 \\quad \\land \\quad \\BarrierFunctionRate(\\MechanicalPlantState, \\ControlInput) \\ge -\\ClassKFunction(\\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState)) \\quad \\implies \\quad \\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState_t) \\ge 0 \\quad \\forall \\TimeInstant \\ge 0.$$ {#eq-enforce-forward-invariance}\nThis implication holds in continuous time under valid plant models and actuator feasibility. Nagumo&#x27;s theorem [@blanchini1999set] provides the geometric boundary interpretation: the closed-loop vector field at the boundary $\\SafetyBoundaryManifold$ must point tangent to or back into the set. The comparison function $\\ClassKFunction(\\cdot)$, chosen as an extended class-$\\Glyph{\\mathcal{K}}$ function, strictly increases with safety margin and vanishes at zero. This allows rapid approach motions deep inside the interior of $\\ForwardInvariantSafeSet$ while enforcing a nondecreasing safety margin along the perimeter. In an embedded implementation, discrete-time sampling, state estimation noise, and torque delivery latency demand additional safety buffers before continuous-time invariance translates into physical reliability.\n\n: **Nagumo&#x27;s Theorem**: @nagumo1942lage established that, under conditions ensuring unique solutions, a closed set is forward-invariant if and only if the system vector field points into the set or remains tangent to its boundary at every boundary point; viability theory subsequently formalized and generalized this principle. Because Nagumo&#x27;s condition applies to continuous-time flows, a digital controller executing at periodic control ticks requires an inter-sample buffer, sized among the conditions forward invariance rests on (@sec-enforcement-invariance-conditions), to guarantee physical safety between discrete updates. \n\n::: {#dfn-enforcement-control-barrier-function .callout-definition title=&quot;Control barrier function&quot;}\n\nA ***control barrier function*** is a continuously differentiable scalar function $\\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState)$ of the plant state whose 0-superlevel set defines the safe set $\\ForwardInvariantSafeSet$, and whose directional derivative can be directly influenced by the control input. Restricting actuation to control inputs that satisfy $\\BarrierFunctionRate(\\MechanicalPlantState, \\ControlInput) \\ge -\\ClassKFunction(\\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState))$ guarantees that any trajectory initialized within $\\ForwardInvariantSafeSet$ remains inside $\\ForwardInvariantSafeSet$ for all time (forward invariance, @eq-enforce-forward-invariance), provided the plant model, actuator limits, and timing assumptions remain valid.\n\n1.  **Significance**: The function translates an empirical stopping margin into an admissible half-space constraint on the next control cycle. For the descending arm of @sec-enforcement-exhausted-authority, its zero level set sits exactly where remaining tool clearance equals the biased stopping distance of `[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.d_eff_str]`; closer than this boundary, no admissible torque command can keep the system within $\\ForwardInvariantSafeSet$.\n2.  **Distinction**: Whereas a Lyapunov function certifies asymptotic convergence toward an equilibrium state, a control barrier function certifies set invariance, proving that system trajectories never exit an admissible operating region. The barrier function provides the mathematical certificate; the least-change quadratic program filter of @sec-enforcement-minimal-intervention provides the runtime mechanism to enforce it.\n3.  **Common pitfall**: Formulating $\\BarrierFunction$ strictly as raw geometric distance. Because distance has relative degree two with respect to torque (commanded torque produces acceleration, which integrates to velocity, which in turn integrates to position), the control input has zero direct authority over $\\BarrierFunctionRate$ at the boundary. A filter monitoring position alone reacts only after physical momentum has made collision unavoidable. An effective barrier must incorporate approach velocity and net braking deceleration, as shown in @sec-enforcement-kinetic-barrier-certificates.\n\n:::\n\n### Kinetic barrier certificates and relative degree {#sec-enforcement-kinetic-barrier-certificates}\n\nOn the descending arm, a barrier function written purely in terms of remaining spatial distance $\\AngularClearance$ leaves commanded motor torque out of its first time derivative, so the safety filter remains blind to closing velocity until the arm reaches the boundary with momentum it can no longer arrest. Across the causal boundary, high-level software proposals meet continuous physical plant dynamics governed by a control-affine differential equation:\n$$\\PlantStateRate = \\ControlAffineDriftField(\\MechanicalPlantState) + \\ControlAffineInputField(\\MechanicalPlantState)\\ControlInput$$\nwhere $\\ControlAffineDriftField(\\MechanicalPlantState)$ captures unforced physical drift (including inertia, Coriolis forces, gravitational bias, and passive friction), $\\ControlAffineInputField(\\MechanicalPlantState)$ maps control inputs to generalized accelerations, and $\\ControlInput$ represents delivered joint torques. On this robot, embedded motor drives close the internal current and velocity loops, meaning the MCU does not write raw voltages to the coils; the per-tick cycle described in @sec-enforcement-loop-budget converts a torque-space decision into the setpoint and torque limit the drive accepts under the qualified delivered-effort realization contract developed there.\n\nEvaluating how a scalar barrier certificate $\\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState)$ evolves along state trajectories requires computing its directional time derivative. By the multivariable chain rule, the total time derivative $\\BarrierFunctionRate$ expands across the state velocity $\\PlantStateRate$:\n$$\\BarrierFunctionRate(\\MechanicalPlantState, \\ControlInput) = \\frac{d\\BarrierFunction}{d\\TimeInstant} = \\frac{\\partial \\BarrierFunction}{\\partial \\MechanicalPlantState} \\PlantStateRate = \\nabla \\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState) \\cdot \\PlantStateRate = \\underbrace{\\nabla \\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState) \\cdot \\ControlAffineDriftField(\\MechanicalPlantState)}_{\\LieDerivativeAlongDrift(\\MechanicalPlantState)} + \\underbrace{\\left(\\nabla \\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState) \\cdot \\ControlAffineInputField(\\MechanicalPlantState)\\right)}_{\\LieDerivativeAlongControl(\\MechanicalPlantState)} \\ControlInput$$\nNonlinear control theory designates $\\LieDerivativeAlongDrift$ and $\\LieDerivativeAlongControl$ as Lie derivatives. In modern computational systems, these directional derivatives are standard **Jacobian-vector products (JVPs)** supported natively by autodiff frameworks such as JAX and PyTorch:\n$$\\LieDerivativeAlongDrift(\\MechanicalPlantState) = \\nabla \\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState)^\\top \\ControlAffineDriftField(\\MechanicalPlantState) = \\text{JVP}(\\BarrierFunction, \\MechanicalPlantState, \\ControlAffineDriftField(\\MechanicalPlantState)), \\qquad \\LieDerivativeAlongControl(\\MechanicalPlantState) = \\nabla \\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState)^\\top \\ControlAffineInputField(\\MechanicalPlantState) = \\text{JVP}(\\BarrierFunction, \\MechanicalPlantState, \\ControlAffineInputField(\\MechanicalPlantState)).$$\nIn forward-mode automatic differentiation, a JVP evaluates the derivative along a single tangent vector. When the control matrix $\\ControlAffineInputField(\\MechanicalPlantState)$ contains multiple input columns $\\ControlAffineInputField_j(\\MechanicalPlantState)$, the notation $\\operatorname{JVP}(\\BarrierFunction, \\MechanicalPlantState, \\ControlAffineInputField)$ denotes the row vector of individual channel products $\\operatorname{JVP}(\\BarrierFunction, \\MechanicalPlantState, \\ControlAffineInputField_j)$. The drift term and the control-channel terms capture distinct physical dynamics:\n\n1. $\\LieDerivativeAlongDrift(\\MechanicalPlantState) = \\text{JVP}(\\BarrierFunction, \\MechanicalPlantState, \\ControlAffineDriftField(\\MechanicalPlantState))$ is the **system drift JVP**, measuring the rate at which safety margin changes due solely to momentum, gravitational load, and passive dynamics.\n2. $\\LieDerivativeAlongControl(\\MechanicalPlantState) = \\text{JVP}(\\BarrierFunction, \\MechanicalPlantState, \\ControlAffineInputField(\\MechanicalPlantState))$ is the **control authority JVP**, measuring the instantaneous sensitivity of the barrier rate of change to commanded actuator torque across the causal boundary.\n\n: **Lie Derivative** (the derivative along a flow): Named after Sophus Lie, whose continuous transformation groups generate the flows along which the derivative is taken, the Lie derivative in control theory is identical to the Jacobian-vector product in modern scientific computing. Because both describe the same directional derivative, the automatic differentiation frameworks used during neural policy training can directly evaluate drift and control authority for learned or tabulated barrier functions, whereas the embedded safety enforcer executes the analytical closed form. \n\nFormulating an effective barrier certificate requires avoiding the **relative degree trap**. In computational graph terms, relative degree is the graph distance, counted in time-integrator nodes, between the commanded control input $\\ControlInput$ and the barrier certificate $\\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState)$. If an engineer defines a safety certificate purely as geometric clearance, $\\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState) = \\ObstaclePosition - \\RobotPosition \\ge 0$, the commanded actuation is separated from position by two successive integrations:\n$$\\text{Actuation } \\ActuatorEffort \\xrightarrow{\\int \\frac{1}{\\Mass} d\\TimeInstant} \\text{Velocity } \\RobotApproachVelocity \\xrightarrow{\\int d\\TimeInstant} \\text{Position } \\RobotPosition$$\nThis two-stage integration cascade establishes relative degree $\\BarrierRelativeDegree = 2$. Taking the first time derivative of clearance yields the closing velocity, $\\BarrierFunctionRate = -\\RobotApproachVelocity$, which contains no control input whatsoever ($\\LieDerivativeAlongControl(\\MechanicalPlantState) = \\text{JVP}(\\BarrierFunction, \\MechanicalPlantState, \\ControlAffineInputField(\\MechanicalPlantState)) = \\ZeroVector$). Because the control authority JVP evaluates to zero, motor torque has zero instantaneous authority over the rate of barrier decay. At the boundary of the safe set ($\\BarrierFunction = 0$), enforcing forward invariance $\\BarrierFunctionRate \\ge -\\ClassKFunction(0) = 0$ requires $-\\RobotApproachVelocity \\ge 0 \\implies \\RobotApproachVelocity \\le 0$. Halting a moving system instantaneously would require infinite deceleration and unbounded torque ($\\ActuatorEffort = -\\infty$). Applying this first-derivative condition to position alone provides no torque constraint before the geometric boundary. Higher-order barrier formulations can retain a position constraint and differentiate until the input appears; this chapter instead incorporates the stopping distance explicitly.\n\nTo restore nonzero control authority ($\\LieDerivativeAlongControl \\neq \\ZeroVector$), the barrier certificate must explicitly incorporate kinetic energy and braking limits. For an approaching arm joint, let $\\AngularClearance = \\ToolClearanceDistance / \\LeverArmLength$ represent the remaining angular clearance, $\\JointAngularSpeed &gt; 0$ the approach speed, $\\EffectiveJointInertia \\JointAngularAcceleration = \\CommandedJointTorque + \\JointBiasTorque$ the joint dynamics, and $\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration$ a validated lower bound on net available angular braking deceleration. Adopting the linear class-$\\Glyph{\\mathcal{K}}$ function $\\ClassKFunction(\\BarrierFunction) = \\LinearBarrierDecayRate \\BarrierFunction$, where $\\LinearBarrierDecayRate &gt; 0$ is a constant approach-rate gain, the **kinetic barrier certificate** is:\n$$\\BarrierFunction(\\AngularClearance,\\JointAngularSpeed)=\\AngularClearance-\\frac{\\JointAngularSpeed^2}{2\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration}.$$ {#eq-enforce-kinetic-barrier}\nDifferentiating $\\BarrierFunction(\\AngularClearance,\\JointAngularSpeed)$ along the joint dynamics yields $\\BarrierFunctionRate = \\LieDerivativeAlongDrift + \\LieDerivativeAlongControl\\,\\CommandedJointTorque$, where $\\LieDerivativeAlongControl = -\\frac{\\JointAngularSpeed}{\\EffectiveJointInertia\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration} \\ne 0$ for any nonzero approach speed $\\JointAngularSpeed &gt; 0$. Because commanded torque $\\CommandedJointTorque$ appears directly in this first time derivative with a nonzero coefficient, the kinetic barrier achieves **relative degree one** ($\\BarrierRelativeDegree = 1$). Actuator commands now exert immediate control authority across the causal boundary. Enforcing the forward-invariance condition $\\BarrierFunctionRate + \\LinearBarrierDecayRate \\BarrierFunction \\ge 0$ isolates the admissible torque into an explicit affine half-space constraint:\n$$\\CommandedJointTorque\\le-\\JointBiasTorque+\\EffectiveJointInertia\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration\\left(\\frac{\\LinearBarrierDecayRate \\BarrierFunction}{\\JointAngularSpeed}-1\\right),\\qquad -\\JointTorqueLimit\\le\\CommandedJointTorque\\le\\JointTorqueLimit.$$ {#eq-enforce-kinetic-torque}\nIn this sign convention, positive angular velocity $\\JointAngularSpeed$, positive gravitational bias $\\JointBiasTorque$, and positive motor torque $\\CommandedJointTorque$ all act downward toward the workstation so that clearance shrinks at rate $\\AngularClearanceRate = -\\JointAngularSpeed$, and a negative upper bound on $\\CommandedJointTorque$ in @eq-enforce-kinetic-torque therefore sets a minimum magnitude on the upward braking torque $-\\CommandedJointTorque$ that the motor must apply against gravity. This condition defines the linear half-space $\\BarrierConstraintNormal(\\MechanicalPlantState)^\\top \\ControlInput \\le \\BarrierConstraintBound(\\MechanicalPlantState)$ solved by the control barrier function quadratic program (CBF-QP) filter developed next, with the half-space normal and scalar threshold determined directly by the Jacobian-vector products: $\\BarrierConstraintNormal(\\MechanicalPlantState)^\\top = -\\LieDerivativeAlongControl(\\MechanicalPlantState)$ and $\\BarrierConstraintBound(\\MechanicalPlantState) = \\LieDerivativeAlongDrift(\\MechanicalPlantState) + \\LinearBarrierDecayRate \\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState)$. At standstill ($\\JointAngularSpeed = 0$), the microcontroller firmware applies a separate static acceleration and position-hold check to prevent division by zero.\n\n: **Kinetic barrier directional derivative chain rule**: Evaluating $\\BarrierFunctionRate(\\AngularClearance, \\JointAngularSpeed) = \\frac{\\partial \\BarrierFunction}{\\partial \\AngularClearance}\\AngularClearanceRate + \\frac{\\partial \\BarrierFunction}{\\partial \\JointAngularSpeed}\\JointAngularAcceleration$ with $\\AngularClearanceRate = -\\JointAngularSpeed$ and $\\JointAngularAcceleration = (\\CommandedJointTorque + \\JointBiasTorque)/\\EffectiveJointInertia$ yields $\\BarrierFunctionRate = (-\\JointAngularSpeed - \\frac{\\JointAngularSpeed\\JointBiasTorque}{\\EffectiveJointInertia\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration}) - \\frac{\\JointAngularSpeed}{\\EffectiveJointInertia\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration}\\CommandedJointTorque = \\LieDerivativeAlongDrift(\\AngularClearance,\\JointAngularSpeed) + \\LieDerivativeAlongControl(\\AngularClearance,\\JointAngularSpeed)\\CommandedJointTorque$. Substituting into $\\BarrierFunctionRate + \\LinearBarrierDecayRate \\BarrierFunction \\ge 0$ and rearranging produces $\\frac{\\JointAngularSpeed(\\CommandedJointTorque + \\JointBiasTorque)}{\\EffectiveJointInertia\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration} \\le \\LinearBarrierDecayRate \\BarrierFunction - \\JointAngularSpeed$. Multiplying by the positive factor $\\frac{\\EffectiveJointInertia\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration}{\\JointAngularSpeed}$ and subtracting the bias yields @eq-enforce-kinetic-torque.\n\n: **Standstill velocity thresholding**: In microcontroller firmware, floating-point evaluation of $\\frac{\\LinearBarrierDecayRate \\BarrierFunction}{\\JointAngularSpeed}$ is explicitly guarded by a velocity threshold $\\StandstillSpeedThreshold$: when $|\\JointAngularSpeed| \\le \\StandstillSpeedThreshold$, the kinetic barrier is bypassed in favor of a static acceleration or position-hold bound, preventing floating-point division by zero and NaN exceptions in the sub-millisecond loop.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ KINETIC BARRIER ON THE MACHINE'S DESCENDING ARM JOINT</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: the loaded-arm paragraph immediately below (@eq-enforce-kinetic-torque).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    evaluate the torque ceiling of the kinetic barrier at three clearances.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    alpha, h at 110 mm, the h = 0 clearance, and the braking torque demanded.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Scenarios.LoadedArmJointGravityStop, the joint scenario of</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          EnforcementAuthorityLoss, with its barrier gain and clearances.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_length, fmt_torque, check</p>\n<p>JOINT = Scenarios.LoadedArmJointGravityStop</p>\n<p>class KineticBarrierArm:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>torque_limit = JOINT.torque_limit  # category: datasheet radius = JOINT.lever  # scenario assumption; category: illustrative gamma = JOINT.barrier_gain  # scenario assumption; category: chosen (barrier gain) clear_far, clear_near = JOINT.barrier_clearances  # scenario assumption; category: illustrative</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>alpha = JOINT.alpha_descending clear_zero = JOINT.tool_stop_descending angle_far = (clear_far / radius).to(\"dimensionless\") h_far = JOINT.barrier_margin(clear_far) ceiling_far = -JOINT.barrier_torque_demand(clear_far) ceiling_zero = -JOINT.barrier_torque_demand(clear_zero) ceiling_near = -JOINT.barrier_torque_demand(clear_near)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(alpha.magnitude - 7.25) &lt; 1e-9, \"Net braking headroom gives 7.25 rad/s^2.\") check(abs(ceiling_far.magnitude + 80.078125) &lt; 1e-9, \"At 110 mm the barrier demands at least 80.1 N·m of braking.\") check(abs(ceiling_zero.magnitude + torque_limit.magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"At h = 0 the barrier demands the full peak torque.\") check(-ceiling_near &gt; torque_limit, \"At 70 mm the demanded braking torque exceeds the joint's peak torque.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">margin_barrier_braking_demand_knee.svg and its fig-alt hand-type 80.1, 87, 89.1 N·m and 79.45, 70, 110 mm.</h1>\n<p>check(abs(-ceiling_near.magnitude - 89.1) &lt; 0.05, \"At 70 mm the barrier demands 89.1 N·m.\") check(abs(clear_zero.magnitude - 79.45) &lt; 0.01, \"The barrier's zero set sits at 79.45 mm of tool clearance.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>alpha_str = fmt(alpha.magnitude, precision=2) gamma_str = fmt(gamma.magnitude, precision=0) angle_far_str = fmt(angle_far.magnitude, precision=4) h_far_str = fmt(h_far, precision=4) clear_far_str = fmt_length(clear_far, unit=millimeter, precision=0) clear_zero_str = fmt_length(clear_zero, unit=millimeter, precision=2) clear_near_str = fmt_length(clear_near, unit=millimeter, precision=0) brake_far_str = fmt_torque(-ceiling_far, precision=1) brake_zero_str = fmt_torque(-ceiling_zero, precision=0) brake_near_str = fmt_torque(-ceiling_near, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<p>Applying this kinetic barrier to the descending-arm parameters from @sec-enforcement-exhausted-authority sets concrete torque ceilings across the approach. The moment of inertia is $\\EffectiveJointInertia =$ <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.inertia_str]</code>. The gravitational bias torque is $\\JointBiasTorque =$ <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.bias_str]</code>, directed toward the workstation. The actuator saturation limit is $|\\CommandedJointTorque| \\le$ <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.torque_limit_str]</code>, so the net angular braking deceleration is $\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration = (\\JointTorqueLimit - \\JointBiasTorque) / \\EffectiveJointInertia =$ <code>[KineticBarrierArm.alpha_str]</code> $\\text{rad/s}^2$. The link lever arm is $\\LeverArmLength =$ <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.link_length_str]</code>, the approach speed is $\\JointAngularSpeed =$ <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.joint_speed_str]</code> $\\text{rad/s}$, and the barrier gain is $\\LinearBarrierDecayRate =$ <code>[KineticBarrierArm.gamma_str]</code> $\\text{s}^{-1}$. These are stipulated joint-level dynamics that deployment must calibrate on the actual arm. At <code>[KineticBarrierArm.clear_far_str]</code> of tool clearance from the workstation surface, the angular clearance is $\\AngularClearance =$ <code>[KineticBarrierArm.angle_far_str]</code> $\\text{rad}$ and the barrier certificate evaluates to $\\BarrierFunction =$ <code>[KineticBarrierArm.h_far_str]</code> $\\text{rad}$, so substituting into @eq-enforce-kinetic-torque requires the joint to deliver at least <code>[KineticBarrierArm.brake_far_str]</code> of counteracting braking torque. Most of that demand is not deceleration at all, because the first <code>[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.bias_str]</code> merely cancels gravity to keep the joint from speeding up and only the remainder slows it, which is why a joint still outside its zero set already asks for most of its peak torque. At <code>[KineticBarrierArm.clear_zero_str]</code>, the system reaches the zero set ($\\BarrierFunction = 0$) where the barrier demands <code>[KineticBarrierArm.brake_zero_str]</code>, the motor's full peak torque capacity. If clearance degrades further to <code>[KineticBarrierArm.clear_near_str]</code>, the inequality demands <code>[KineticBarrierArm.brake_near_str]</code>, which exceeds peak actuator limits and places the state outside the control-admissible viable set. There, under these scalar dynamics, fixed bias, and instantaneous maximum-braking assumptions, no controller initiated at that point can prevent contact.</p>\n<p>Physical robotic hardware provides neither perfectly continuous state feedback nor instantaneous torque response. Stator current rise times, fieldbus jitter, and drivetrain mechanical compliance delay and soften corrective torque delivery. To guarantee safety under these real-world non-idealities, the runtime enforcer evaluates the barrier certificate against an inset boundary that explicitly accounts for the worst-case stopping deficit induced by hardware latency and compliant dynamics. This initiates corrective filtering earlier along the trajectory.</p>\n<p>This kinetic barrier construction follows directly from rigid-body braking mechanics. For the stipulated scalar approach with exact dynamics, fixed bias, instantaneous effort, and no omitted margins, its zero level set represents the exact boundary of states from which maximum allowable braking stops the joint before collision: the <strong>viability kernel</strong> [@aubin1991viability] of a double integrator subject to actuator saturation. Among collision-free states, the kernel's complement is the set of inevitable collision states defined in @sec-planning-inevitable-collision-states. With a conservative available lower bound instead of exact maximum braking, the same construction supplies a sufficient stopping set rather than the exact kernel. The resulting torque requirement remains compatible with the stipulated actuator limits. At $\\BarrierFunction = 0$, the inequality in @eq-enforce-kinetic-torque demands exactly $-\\JointTorqueLimit$, and for all $\\BarrierFunction &gt; 0$ the demand is strictly less restrictive, ensuring that at least one admissible control command exists everywhere within the safe set. Actuator feasibility across the entire set, not merely the calculus of the derivative, is what qualifies $\\BarrierFunction(\\AngularClearance, \\JointAngularSpeed)$ as a valid barrier certificate under input constraints.</p>\n<p>This identical principle governs autonomous base navigation. The mobile base certificate $\\MobileBaseBarrierFunction(\\ClearDistance, \\SpeedAtDetection) = \\ClearDistance - [\\SpeedAtDetection\\,\\PrebrakeDelay + \\ResidentStopDistance(\\SpeedAtDetection) + \\LocalizationBound + \\ProtectiveClearanceMargin + \\TrackingErrorBound]$ is nonnegative precisely when the stopping subtotal in @eq-enforce-stopping-envelope fits the clearance. The operational certificate must additionally subtract the dispatch-to-admission travel before granting permission. Both constructions reserve the motion needed to stop. A nonnegative stopping reservation alone does not establish forward invariance; the sampled implementation must also preserve that reservation under its qualified state, effort, and timing bounds. The single-joint arm derivation omits latency and localization margins temporarily only to isolate the mechanics of relative degree.</p>\n<p>When complex nonlinear dynamics prevent closed-form analytical solutions for the viability kernel, <strong>Hamilton-Jacobi (HJ) reachability</strong> computes the boundary numerically as the zero level set of an optimal control value function [@mitchell2005time; @bansal2017hamilton]. The resulting tabulated lookup surface serves as the runtime barrier certificate, with its spatial gradient defining the half-space normal for real-time filtering. Because state-space grid discretization scales exponentially with dimensionality, HJ reachability lookup tables are best suited for decoupled, low-dimensional subsystems such as a single revolute joint or the longitudinal tracking channel of a mobile base.</p>\n<p>↪ The Hamilton-Jacobi value function and its differential-game formulation are developed in @sec-appendix-control-hj-reachability.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Hamilton-Jacobi Reachability</strong>: Originating in the zero-sum differential game formulations of @isaacs1965differential, reachability analysis frames safety as a game between a control policy and a worst-case adversarial disturbance. The resulting safe set contains precisely those states from which the controller can guarantee safety against any bounded disturbance trajectory. Consequently, an HJ reachability table is inherently robust but conservative, and its safety guarantee holds only within the assumed disturbance envelope.</p>\n<p>A barrier certificate specifies which actuator commands preserve forward invariance, but it does not specify how the runtime safety path should handle an uncertified proposal. When a candidate command violates the half-space constraint, the enforcer must choose whether to project the command onto the boundary of admissible inputs, reject it in favor of a precomputed emergency braking trajectory, or maintain the previously certified setpoint. This intervention policy determines how mathematical barrier constraints translate into real-time authority over the motor drives.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h2\">Refusing Proposals and Enforcing State Invariants</h2>\n<p>A trajectory chunk arrives from the application processor on schedule, properly framed, and bearing a fresh evidence timestamp, yet one of its constituent joint setpoints would accelerate the arm directly into a steel rack upright. Nothing in the incoming packet payload marks that setpoint as erroneous. The permission path therefore grants zero executive authority to an incoming learned command until that proposal passes the configured state, input, freshness, and stopping-clearance checks. This is the third law, <strong>proposal is not permission</strong> (principle \\ref{pri-vol4-proposal-permission}), executed deterministically one <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.tick_ms_str]</code> tick at a time. Rather than assuming that a learned proposal satisfies stability criteria, actuator limits, or stopping guarantees, the permission path treats every incoming vector strictly as an advisory candidate.</p>\n<p>The resident controller and output selector translate this principle of refusal into deterministic execution. The resident controller maintains a fallback response qualified for the current machine state, while the selector determines whether to admit an incoming candidate, apply a feasible correction, or transfer authority directly to that resident response. Rejecting an incoming candidate does not instantaneously abort the command currently driving the plant. Instead, the active command continues running so long as its own evidence timestamp, lease deadline, tracking error bound, and stopping reservation remain valid.</p>\n<p>Under this architecture, the upstream neural policy never commands the physical plant directly. Its proposals reach the safety MCU through the board link and seqlock mailbox, with no addressable memory shared between the two compute domains. Each admitted chunk drives the plant only under a chunk lease, a hardware-enforced dead-man permission that lapses unless a fresh, validated proposal renews it. A trajectory chunk arrives as a horizon of $\\ActionChunkHorizon$ waypoints, and the admission filter inspects all $\\ActionChunkHorizon$ points against reach, velocity, and stopping-reservation constraints before permitting the first waypoint to execute. On each subsequent control tick, the enforcer re-evaluates the specific setpoint scheduled for execution against live sensor telemetry. If the enforcer projects a setpoint on a single tick, it does not rewrite the chunk's downstream waypoints. Instead, the safety MCU reports the intervention upstream, requiring the policy to replan from the actual, corrected state rather than resuming execution along a trajectory computed for a state the machine never reached.</p>\n<p>↩ The seqlock mailbox that hands proposals to the permission path is established in @sec-nervous-mailbox-exchanges.</p>\n<p>If the proposal remains fresh, the physical machine state resides within the qualified stopping region, and the tracking error satisfies $\\|\\TrackingErrorDistance(\\TimeInstant)\\| \\le \\TrackingErrorBound$, the output selector permits the proposal (or its minimally projected variant $\\CertifiedControlAction$) to drive the actuators. Conversely, if the chunk lease expires without renewal, tracking error breaches the tolerance bound, no admissible command satisfies instantaneous physical constraints, or the active command endangers physical boundaries beyond the corrective capacity of projection, the output selector revokes permission and unilaterally engages the baseline safety controller. The upstream neural policy is not consulted, cannot negotiate, and possesses no hardware mechanism to override the selector's decision.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Dead-Man Control</strong>: Early electric locomotives incorporated a spring-loaded dead-man handle that the operator had to depress continuously; releasing the handle (if the driver lost consciousness, for example) interrupted tractive power and engaged the mechanical brakes. System safety arose from the cessation of action rather than the transmission of an explicit fault message. The chunk lease applies the same principle in software. The host must actively renew its permission, ensuring that an upstream processor too damaged to command a stop is inherently incapable of renewing execution authority.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ THE LATCH APPROACH AS THE FILTERING CASE\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: the filter-vs.-veto paragraph immediately below; the record&#x27;s\n# │          approach-ceiling paragraph cites the same speeds.\n# │ Goal:    give the chapter&#x27;s first projection on the machine.\n# │ Show:    the chunk policy&#x27;s proposed approach and the target-ODD approach.\n# │ How:     AISLE latch fields (the same ones EnforcementRecordMachine loads).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_velocity, check\n\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass EnforcementLatchFilter:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    v_latch_high = AISLE.v_latch_high  # category: illustrative (policy&#x27;s proposed approach)\n    v_latch = AISLE.v_latch_approach  # category: chosen (target-ODD approach)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    speed_ratio = (v_latch_high / v_latch).to(&quot;dimensionless&quot;).magnitude\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(v_latch_high.to(meter / second).magnitude - 0.10) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;The policy proposes 0.10 m/s.&quot;)\n    check(abs(v_latch.to(meter / second).magnitude - 0.03) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;The target-ODD approach is 0.03 m/s.&quot;)\n    check(speed_ratio &gt; 1.0, &quot;The proposed approach is faster than the evidence covers.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    v_latch_high_str = fmt_velocity(v_latch_high, precision=2)\n    v_latch_str = fmt_velocity(v_latch, precision=2)\n]```\n\nWhen a proposal conflicts with physical constraints, the enforcer chooses between filtering and vetoing the command. Filtering applies when the proposal is structurally sound and a nearby admissible control action preserves the policy&#x27;s underlying intent. Consider the manipulator arm approaching a spring-latched cage door when the chunk policy proposes approaching the strike plate at `[EnforcementLatchFilter.v_latch_high_str]`. The door zone admits only the guarded approach speed of `[EnforcementLatchFilter.v_latch_str]` defined by the target operational design domain (ODD), the velocity envelope validated across empirical trials and demonstration datasets. The faster proposal therefore fails on both evidential and physical grounds. It fails on evidence, because no trial or demonstration sampled that speed. It also fails on physics, because the contact analysis of @sec-enforcement-intentional-contact shows that at that speed the force on the plate at brake onset nearly reaches the latch limit, leaving a stop that demands far harder arm deceleration than the guarded approach needs. The enforcer therefore preserves the approach trajectory&#x27;s target and spatial direction, projecting only its scalar speed down to the `[EnforcementLatchFilter.v_latch_str]` limit. The least-change quadratic program formulated in @sec-enforcement-minimal-intervention solves this projection in real time alongside the half-space constraints derived by a control barrier function.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The evaluation record&#x27;s target ODD boundary, sampled at the guarded approach speed, is established in @sec-evaluation-logs.\n:::\n\nFiltering must account for momentum alongside position, ensuring that the actuators retain sufficient control authority to bring the machine to a complete stop before available clearance vanishes (@sec-enforcement-safe-sets-conditional-permission). In the latch example, scalar speed projection preserves both the goal and the approach direction. In a coupled joint-torque filter, however, a correction can alter the end-effector trajectory through task space; such modification remains permissible only when the revised motion satisfies dynamic continuity constraints and remains task-admissible. Conversely, a structurally invalid proposal (a discontinuous jump in commanded position, an acceleration profile beyond peak motor torque, or a trajectory pointed directly into an immovable obstacle) indicates that perception or inference has entered an unmodeled failure mode. Projecting an ungrounded candidate onto the nearest admissible boundary would execute arbitrary motion unrelated to any coherent task intent. Under these conditions, the enforcer exercises an outright veto. The selector permits the active command to finish only if that command continues to satisfy its own validity checks; otherwise, it immediately transfers authority to a prequalified local fallback.\n\nBoth filtering and veto operate strictly at runtime; offline training cannot substitute for either mechanism. Penalty terms, barrier losses, and reward shaping bias the policy distribution during training, but they cannot mathematically bound what the deployed neural network will emit in edge cases. The **statistical safety fallacy** conflates a bound on expected violation cost with an absolute guarantee across every execution trace. A constrained reinforcement learning objective such as $\\mathbb{E}[\\text{cost}] \\le \\ExpectedCostLimit$ constrains only the ensemble mean, leaving open the possibility of catastrophic tail events during individual rollouts. Formal neural-network verification can establish provable output bounds over bounded input sets [@katz2017reluplex], and closed-loop verification can combine those bounds with plant models. Formal proofs nevertheless remain vulnerable to reality gaps: unmodeled friction, sensor latency, non-deterministic scheduling, or actuator saturation outside the proof&#x27;s operational assumptions will invalidate the guarantee. The permission path therefore verifies every candidate control vector at the hardware actuation boundary, regardless of the optimization objective used during training. When an incoming proposal is safe, the verification check is transparent, allowing learned sensorimotor competence to pass directly to the motor drives without alteration.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ Why training-time penalties cannot bound the actions a deployed policy emits is established in @sec-boundary-fallacies-pitfalls.\n:::\n\nWhen incoming proposals stall instead of arriving on schedule, the chunk lease, `[EnforcementLeaseStop.lease_ms_str]` on this machine, bounds upstream execution authority during a host stall or scheduling delay, provided the local timer, selector, sensing, and actuation paths survive. In addition to the lease, a dedicated `[EnforcementLeaseStop.heartbeat_rate_hz_str]` $\\text{Hz}$ host heartbeat monitors basic link viability with a `[EnforcementLeaseStop.heartbeat_timeout_ms_str]` silence timeout. The safety MCU executes its deterministic `[EnforcementLeaseStop.loop_rate_str]` control loop, servicing a local `[EnforcementLeaseStop.self_watchdog_ms_str]` hardware watchdog timer only after successfully completing an entire cycle; an independent hardware trip circuit supervises MCU power and clock integrity. If either the host heartbeat or the chunk lease expires, the safety MCU revokes proposal permissions and engages a deterministic, state-matched fallback from the responses that @sec-enforcement-fallback-ladder defines, assuming reserved clearance and actuator braking capacities remain sufficient. On the warehouse mobile manipulator, if a proposer renews its lease and subsequently stalls, mechanical braking begins within `[EnforcementLeaseStop.renewal_to_onset_ms_str]` of the last valid renewal. This lease-expiry scenario assumes the underlying heartbeat remains active even as valid proposals cease; a total host operating-system freeze trips the `[EnforcementLeaseStop.heartbeat_timeout_ms_str]` heartbeat timeout even earlier. If a worker steps out from behind a warehouse rack, the sensor capture latency of the camera frame that first revealed the obstacle adds directly to this renewal-to-onset delay, and @sec-enforcement-stopping-envelopes carries this cumulative latency directly into the admission check.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The lease, heartbeat, and self-watchdog timers, and why a heartbeat cannot renew a lease, are established in @sec-nervous-supervisory-timers.\n:::\n\nSevering upstream authority requires an unambiguously defined physical response at the lowest hardware interface. The zero-command floor defines the default physical state enforced when active software control terminates, but commanding &#x27;zero&#x27; produces drastically different dynamics depending on the actuator topology. Injecting zero current into a low-friction, direct-drive actuator allows a gravity-loaded manipulator arm to drop freely unless a mechanical brake or counterweight holds the link. Conversely, commanding zero velocity to a stiff position-controlled servo can demand infinite acceleration and trigger violent inertial transients. As detailed in @sec-body-unpowered-states, an unpowered drive drops into one of three hardware states: unconstrained coasting, phase-short dynamic braking, or mechanical locking via spring-applied brakes. The system architect must ensure that the commanded fallback matches both the instantaneous kinetic state and the physical capabilities of the surviving hardware.\n\nProposal rejection is a continuous, steady-state operating decision rather than an exceptional emergency trip. The enforcer must evaluate every invariant and execute its selected fallback deterministically even if the upstream proposer crashes completely, provided local sensing, braking authority, and stopping clearance remain intact. When those physical invariants hold, runtime filtering smoothly reconciles learned proposals with safety boundaries, preserving admissible task progress within the qualified physical envelope.\n\n## The Minimal Intervention Principle and Optimization Filters {#sec-enforcement-minimal-intervention}\n\nWhen the arm approaches the cage-door latch too quickly while still proposing a useful target and direction, rejecting every such proposal would discard useful learned behavior, whereas accepting it unchecked would exceed the permitted approach speed. The **minimal intervention principle** resolves this trade-off by passing an admissible proposal untouched and choosing the smallest feasible correction, one that satisfies physical constraints and finishes before the permission deadline, whenever a proposal crosses a boundary. The resulting **safety shield** preserves useful task progress within the physical limits. For the torque forbidden by @eq-enforce-kinetic-torque, the correction should remove only the constraint violation; replacing every near-boundary command with a stop would discard admissible learned behavior.\n\n: **Shielding**: In safe reinforcement learning, a shield sits between the learning agent and its environment and overrides only those actions that would violate a temporal-logic safety specification [@alshiekh2018safe]. The CBF-QP is a continuous-control shield. Its override is the smallest torque change rather than a substituted discrete action, and its specification is a stopping margin rather than a logic formula, which lets it keep the learned action&#x27;s direction instead of replacing it. \n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── LOAD: declared enforcer cycle budget ────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import Embodied, Scenarios from mlsysim.core.units import microsecond, millisecond from mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_latency, fmt_frequency, check</p>\n<p>CYCLE = Scenarios.WarehouseEnforcerCycleBudget</p>\n<p>class EnforcementCycleBudget: scenario = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle wcet = scenario.enforcer_wcet  # category: chosen (declared, not measured) deadline = scenario.enforcer_deadline  # category: chosen tick = scenario.t_tick  # category: chosen unloaded = scenario.enforcer_unloaded  # category: illustrative (unloaded observed cycle) acquisition = CYCLE.stage(\"state acquisition\")  # category: chosen (sub-budget; with the proposal check it covers the unloaded 40 us read in Ch13) proposal_lease = CYCLE.stage(\"proposal and lease check\")  # category: chosen (sub-budget) tracking = CYCLE.stage(\"tracking check\")  # category: chosen (sub-budget) qp = CYCLE.stage(\"QP solve\")  # category: chosen (sub-budget) write = CYCLE.stage(\"output write\")  # category: chosen (sub-budget) reserve = CYCLE.stage(\"reserve\")  # category: chosen (sub-budget) window = 500 * millisecond  # category: illustrative (rolling window of the intervention metric)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── EXECUTE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>total = CYCLE.total</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── GUARD ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs((total - wcet.to(microsecond)).magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"The stage sub-budgets must sum to the declared WCET.\") check(wcet &lt; deadline &lt; tick, \"The declared WCET must sit inside the deadline, and the deadline inside the tick.\") check(unloaded &lt; wcet, \"The unloaded observed cycle must sit below the declared WCET.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">margin_cbf_qp_cycle_budget_envelope.svg and its fig-alt hand-type 150, 250, 30, 400 and 135 us.</h1>\n<p>check( [round(x.to(microsecond).magnitude) for x in (qp, wcet, reserve, deadline, unloaded)] == [150, 250, 30, 400, 135], \"The cycle-budget margin figure's alt text states 150, 250, 30, 400 and 135 us.\", )</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── OUTPUT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>wcet_us_str = fmt(wcet.to(microsecond).magnitude, precision=0) deadline_us_str = fmt(deadline.to(microsecond).magnitude, precision=0) unloaded_us_str = fmt(unloaded.to(microsecond).magnitude, precision=0) acquisition_us_str = fmt(acquisition.magnitude, precision=0) proposal_lease_us_str = fmt(proposal_lease.magnitude, precision=0) tracking_us_str = fmt(tracking.magnitude, precision=0) qp_us_str = fmt(qp.magnitude, precision=0) write_us_str = fmt(write.magnitude, precision=0) reserve_us_str = fmt(reserve.magnitude, precision=0) tick_str = fmt_latency(tick.to(millisecond), unit=millisecond, precision=0) rate_str = fmt_frequency((1 / tick).to(\"Hz\")) window_ms_str = fmt_latency(window, unit=millisecond, precision=0) ]```</p>\n<p>For the already-derived torque half-space, the enforcer solves a constrained least-change problem, the <strong>CBF-QP safety filter</strong> [@ames2016control]. The tracker supplies the nominal effort; the barrier and actuator limits constrain the result. The combined program of @sec-appendix-control-clf, which optimizes convergence and safety together and relaxes convergence when they conflict, is not needed here. The tracker of @sec-enforcement-handoff already supplies the nominal effort, and the filter has only to keep it inside the barrier: $$\\min_{\\ControlInput \\in \\mathbb{R}^\\ControlInputDimension} \\frac{1}{2}\\|\\ControlInput - \\NominalControlCommand\\|^2 \\quad \\text{s.t.} \\quad \\BarrierConstraintNormal(\\MechanicalPlantState)^\\top \\ControlInput \\le \\BarrierConstraintBound(\\MechanicalPlantState), \\quad \\ActuatorTorqueLowerBound \\le \\ControlInput \\le \\ActuatorTorqueUpperBound$$ {#eq-enforce-cbf-qp} where the half-space normal and threshold are defined by the directional derivatives $\\BarrierConstraintNormal(\\MechanicalPlantState)^\\top = -\\LieDerivativeAlongControl(\\MechanicalPlantState)$ and $\\BarrierConstraintBound(\\MechanicalPlantState) = \\LieDerivativeAlongDrift(\\MechanicalPlantState) + \\ClassKFunction(\\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState))$ derived in @sec-enforcement-kinetic-barrier-certificates, matching the single-axis torque constraint of @eq-enforce-kinetic-torque, and $[\\ActuatorTorqueLowerBound, \\ActuatorTorqueUpperBound]$ represents the verified actuator torque envelope. The selected implementation allocates <code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.qp_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$ to this solve. Dedicated memory and a fixed iteration cap support that target; qualification must establish completion or a timely fallback under the admitted constraint set.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h3\">Closed-form quadratic programming and torque projection</h3>\n<p>Clamping each joint axis independently, or projecting a command onto a barrier and then clipping it to motor torque limits afterward, can leave the coupled stopping constraint violated while skewing the direction of motion across task space. A single active barrier admits a closed-form projection when the projected command also lies inside the actuator bounds; if a bound becomes active, the enforcer must solve for the intersection rather than project onto the barrier and clamp afterward.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\nfrom mlsysim import Scenarios\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, check\n\nPROJ = Scenarios.TwoJointTorqueProjection\n\nclass CBFQPProjection:\n    u_nom = PROJ.proposal  # N·m; category: illustrative (abstract proposal)\n    a_vec = PROJ.normal  # category: illustrative (abstract half-space normal)\n    b_scalar = PROJ.bound  # N·m; category: illustrative (abstract threshold)\n    u_clip = PROJ.clamp  # N·m; category: illustrative (per-axis clamp for contrast)\n    u_lim = PROJ.actuator_limit  # N·m; category: illustrative (symmetric per-joint actuator limit)\n\n    res = PROJ.projection\n    u_proj = res[&quot;u_projected&quot;]\n    lambda_star = res[&quot;lambda_star&quot;]\n    correction_norm = res[&quot;correction_norm&quot;]\n\n    a_dot_u_nom = PROJ.proposal_value\n    breach = PROJ.breach\n    a_dot_u_clip = PROJ.clamp_value\n    clip_penetration = PROJ.clamp_penetration\n\n    theta_nom_deg = PROJ.angle_deg(u_nom)\n    theta_clip_deg = PROJ.angle_deg(u_clip)\n    skew_deg = theta_clip_deg - theta_nom_deg\n\n    check(abs(u_proj[0]) &lt;= u_lim and abs(u_proj[1]) &lt;= u_lim, &quot;The projected command stays within the joint limits.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT (Format &amp; Export) ────────────────────────────────────────\n    u_nom_1_str = fmt(u_nom[0], precision=1)\n    u_nom_2_str = fmt(u_nom[1], precision=0)\n    a_1_str = fmt(a_vec[0], precision=0)\n    a_2_str = fmt(a_vec[1], precision=0)\n    b_str = fmt(b_scalar, precision=0)\n    u_lim_str = fmt(u_lim, precision=0)\n\n    a_dot_u_nom_str = fmt(a_dot_u_nom, precision=0)\n    breach_str = fmt(breach, precision=0)\n    lambda_star_str = fmt(lambda_star, precision=0)\n    u_proj_1_str = fmt(u_proj[0], precision=1)\n    u_proj_2_str = fmt(u_proj[1], precision=0)\n    correction_norm_str = fmt(correction_norm, precision=3)\n\n    u_clip_1_str = fmt(u_clip[0], precision=0)\n    u_clip_2_str = fmt(u_clip[1], precision=0)\n    a_dot_u_clip_str = fmt(a_dot_u_clip, precision=0)\n    clip_penetration_str = fmt(clip_penetration, precision=0)\n    theta_nom_deg_str = fmt(theta_nom_deg, precision=1)\n    theta_clip_deg_str = fmt(theta_clip_deg, precision=1)\n    skew_deg_str = fmt(skew_deg, precision=1)\n]```\n\nFor a nonzero constraint normal and inactive actuator bounds, the single barrier constraint $\\BarrierConstraintNormal^\\top \\ControlInput \\le \\BarrierConstraintBound$ in @eq-enforce-cbf-qp admits the closed-form projection derived in @sec-appendix-control-cbf-qp:\n$$\\CertifiedControlAction = \\NominalControlCommand - \\BarrierProjectionMultiplier^* \\BarrierConstraintNormal, \\qquad \\BarrierProjectionMultiplier^* = \\max\\left(0, \\frac{\\BarrierConstraintNormal^\\top \\NominalControlCommand - \\BarrierConstraintBound}{\\|\\BarrierConstraintNormal\\|^2}\\right)$$ {#eq-enforce-cbf-projection}\nwhere the optimal dual multiplier $\\BarrierProjectionMultiplier^* \\ge 0$ is zero whenever the nominal proposal is safe ($\\BarrierConstraintNormal^\\top \\NominalControlCommand \\le \\BarrierConstraintBound$), leaving $\\CertifiedControlAction = \\NominalControlCommand$. It is strictly positive when the proposal breaches the boundary ($\\BarrierConstraintNormal^\\top \\NominalControlCommand &gt; \\BarrierConstraintBound$). In that case it scales the minimal intervention vector $\\FiniteChange \\ControlInput = -\\BarrierProjectionMultiplier^* \\BarrierConstraintNormal$, which projects $\\CertifiedControlAction$ orthogonally onto the safe half-space boundary $\\BarrierConstraintNormal^\\top \\ControlInput = \\BarrierConstraintBound$.\n\nOn a two-axis manipulator with illustrative values, suppose the tracker following setpoints from an unconstrained neural policy computes nominal joint torques $\\NominalControlCommand = [$ `[CBFQPProjection.u_nom_1_str]`, `[CBFQPProjection.u_nom_2_str]` $]^\\top\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$ against a validated safety half-space $\\BarrierConstraintNormal^\\top \\ControlInput \\le \\BarrierConstraintBound$. Its parameters are $\\BarrierConstraintNormal = [$ `[CBFQPProjection.a_1_str]`, `[CBFQPProjection.a_2_str]` $]^\\top$ and $\\BarrierConstraintBound =$ `[CBFQPProjection.b_str]` $\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}$. The per-joint actuator limits are ±`[CBFQPProjection.u_lim_str]` $\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}$. Because the candidate gives $\\BarrierConstraintNormal^\\top \\NominalControlCommand =$ `[CBFQPProjection.a_dot_u_nom_str]` $\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}$ and breaches the boundary by `[CBFQPProjection.breach_str]` $\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}$, @eq-enforce-cbf-projection yields $\\BarrierProjectionMultiplier^* =$ `[CBFQPProjection.lambda_star_str]` and projects the command to $\\CertifiedControlAction = [$ `[CBFQPProjection.u_proj_1_str]`, `[CBFQPProjection.u_proj_2_str]` $]^\\top\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$. This meets the half-space exactly while keeping both joints within their limits at an intervention norm of `[CBFQPProjection.correction_norm_str]` $\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}$. By contrast, an independent per-axis clamp that ignores the coupling and cuts only the joint with the larger normal component holds Joint 1 to `[CBFQPProjection.u_clip_1_str]` $\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}$ while passing Joint 2 at `[CBFQPProjection.u_clip_2_str]` $\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}$. That clamp gives $\\BarrierConstraintNormal^\\top \\ClampedControlCommand =$ `[CBFQPProjection.a_dot_u_clip_str]` $\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}$, still `[CBFQPProjection.clip_penetration_str]` $\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}$ outside the constraint, while skewing the torque vector from `[CBFQPProjection.theta_nom_deg_str]`$^\\circ$ to `[CBFQPProjection.theta_clip_deg_str]`$^\\circ$. The per-axis clamp both violates the coupled constraint and distorts the command. Orthogonal projection satisfies the constraint with the smallest possible torque change, though it too rotates the torque vector, so the resulting end-effector path still requires checking against the coupled plant model.\n\nThis geometric behavior exposes the distinction between **torque-space and task-space minimal intervention**: an orthogonal projection $\\CertifiedControlAction = \\NominalControlCommand + \\FiniteChange \\ControlInput$ minimizes joint effort deviation $\\|\\ControlInput - \\NominalControlCommand\\|^2$, which redistributes multi-axis joint torques and rotates the net Cartesian wrench. While a feasible projection can preserve the safe set $\\ForwardInvariantSafeSet$ under the stated model and timing assumptions, it deflects the end-effector trajectory and may arrest task progress. If the upstream neural policy continues emitting unprojected nominal setpoints without awareness of the intervention, the robot can chatter or stall at the boundary. The downstream enforcer must therefore stream the intervention vector $\\FiniteChange \\ControlInput$ back to the application processor as a telemetry signal, notifying the high-level planner to re-plan around the newly active physical constraint.\n\nThe same correction has a picture in state space and one in torque space (@fig-12-cbf-safety-filter). In state space, a trajectory that would breach the velocity-dependent boundary $\\SafetyBoundaryManifold$ is turned along it; in the torque plane, the projection places $\\CertifiedControlAction$ on the half-space boundary $\\BarrierConstraintNormal^\\top \\ControlInput = \\BarrierConstraintBound$, while the per-axis clamp leaves $\\ClampedControlCommand$ outside it and skews its direction.\n\n::: {#fig-12-cbf-safety-filter fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Kinetic barrier and torque projection**: Panel (a) schematically shows a velocity-dependent viable boundary inset from geometric clearance; the raw distance alone has relative degree two for torque. Panel (b) illustrates Euclidean projection onto a supplied torque half-space. Panel (b) is schematic and not drawn to the worked example&#x27;s values; that example&#x27;s $[2,1]$ normal and $5\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$ threshold are abstract arithmetic inputs, not coefficients derived from the arm example.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Two panels: a schematic inset stopping boundary in state space, and a two-axis torque plane in which a nominal command is projected onto a feasible half-space while a per-axis clamped command remains outside it.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\nFilter choice joins a mathematical condition to a measured execution budget and the machine&#x27;s available braking authority. As summarized in @tbl-12-safety-filter-architectures, architectural choices differ in their handling of relative degree, solver worst-case execution time (WCET), forward invariance guarantees, and directional fidelity. Independent clamping can miss coupled constraints; a CBF-QP can enforce a derived half-space if it is feasible and finishes within the verified cycle. Predictive filters [@wabersich2021predictive] trade a longer horizon for configuration-dependent compute cost.\n\nThe rows of @tbl-12-safety-filter-architectures belong to one unified family rather than rival techniques [@hsu2024safetyfilter]: each filter monitors a candidate against a **fallback policy** known to keep the machine safe and intervenes whenever the candidate would leave the set from which that fallback still works, differing only in how the intervention occurs. Whereas a **least-restrictive** filter passes the proposal untouched up to the boundary and then switches abruptly to the fallback (which is simple to implement but chatters along the boundary), a smooth filter such as the CBF-QP blends in a correction before the boundary is reached. This chapter&#x27;s enforcer combines both mechanisms: admitting a trajectory only with a matched stop suffix and holding a state-matched fallback for every reachable state forms a **backup-set filter** that checks safety by rolling the fallback forward from the proposed state [@gurriet2018online; @chen2021backup]. The per-tick CBF-QP acts as the smooth local filter layered on top of it, and HJ reachability [@fisac2019general] supplies the value function when a precomputed surface replaces a closed-form barrier.\n</code></pre>\n<p>\\Needspace{26\\baselineskip}</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>\n| Filter                         | Input and condition                                                                    | What it can establish                                                   | Main validation task                                                                                                          |\n|:-------------------------------|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:------------------------------------------------------------------------|:------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Independent torque clamp       | Each axis checked separately                                                           | Hardware amplitude limit, not coupled stopping clearance                | Test coupled wrench and stopping behavior                                                                                     |\n| First-order CBF-QP             | Barrier whose first derivative contains the input                                      | Conditional invariance from a viable initial state                      | Validate model, feasible torque set, and cycle bound                                                                          |\n| Kinetic / higher-order barrier | Position constraint with speed and braking authority included                          | Conditional stopping margin before geometric contact                    | Bound bias torque, delay, estimate error, and discretization                                                                  |\n| Energy tank / passivity filter | State $\\EnergyTankLevel$, power balance $\\EnergyTankPower$, force $\\ContactForceLimit$ | Conditional energy bound; force requires a separately qualified channel | Bound force-sensor bias, calibrate virtual damping matrix $\\VirtualDampingMatrix$, and set reserve floor $\\EnergyTankReserve$ |\n| HJ value-function filter       | Precomputed value function over a gridded state space                                  | Containment for the modeled region                                      | Verify grid coverage, interpolation error, and disturbance bounds                                                             |\n| Predictive safety filter       | Finite-horizon trajectory and terminal set                                             | Recursive feasibility only under terminal and timing assumptions        | Bound full solve time and fallback on timeout                                                                                 |\n\n: **Safety-filter decisions**: A filter&#x27;s physical claim depends on the chosen barrier and validated plant, not on the algorithm name. Nominal solve measurements are not worst-case deadlines. The energy-tank row is developed for intentional contact in @sec-enforcement-intentional-contact. {#tbl-12-safety-filter-architectures tbl-colwidths=&quot;[20,26,28,26]&quot;}\n\nOperating near an active safe-set boundary can produce chattering. If a learned policy repeatedly proposes an unsafe command, sampled enforcement may switch the active constraint across successive `[EnforcementLeaseStop.tick_ms_str]` ticks. Depending on the plant and controller, rapid torque changes can excite a structural mode or increase motor heating and transmission wear. A tested boundary layer or hysteresis can reduce switching, but its added tracking error must fit inside the validated stopping margin.\n\nThe intervention vector $\\FiniteChange \\ControlInput = \\CertifiedControlAction - \\NominalControlCommand$ also measures upstream policy health. A rising intervention frequency or magnitude can indicate proposal drift, a changed task, or a changed plant. Normal filtering can also preserve successful whole-machine service while reducing unassisted proposer autonomy; the log must distinguish these outcomes rather than count every correction as a task failure. Summed over an illustrative rolling window of $\\InterventionWindowDuration =$ `[EnforcementCycleBudget.window_ms_str]` as $\\sum_{k} \\|\\CertifiedControlAction_k - {\\NominalControlCommand}_k\\| \\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$, the metric lets the permission path flag divergence for logging or operator notification while the per-tick limits still protect the plant.\n\n### Intentional contact and in-contact energy barriers {#sec-enforcement-intentional-contact}\n\nOnce the manipulator arm reaches the cage-door strike plate ($\\AngularClearance = 0$), the spatial kinetic barrier (@eq-enforce-kinetic-barrier) reduces to $\\BarrierFunction(0, \\JointAngularSpeed) = -\\JointAngularSpeed^2 / (2\\NetAngularBrakingDeceleration) \\ge 0$, which admits only zero speed ($\\JointAngularSpeed = 0$) and freezes the gripper before it can depress the latch. A spatial kinetic barrier operates under the premise that the robot&#x27;s objective is to avoid collision ($\\AngularClearance &gt; 0$), whereas manipulation, grasping, and assembly require intentional physical contact ($\\AngularClearance = 0$). While the spatial barrier can admit arrival at contact with zero approach speed, it does not govern the subsequent force-controlled interaction where geometric separation no longer supplies the relevant margin. Once the warehouse mobile manipulator&#x27;s gripper meets the strike plate at the guarded approach, the arm must push against the latch spring, and the margin that matters is the energy and force it delivers there.\n\nEnforcing safety during intentional contact requires transitioning from a **spatial clearance barrier** to an **energetic passivity barrier**. When physical clearance is zero ($\\ClearDistance = 0$), safety cannot mean zero velocity; it means bounding mechanical energy exchange and normal contact force $\\NormalContactForce$. A controller in physical contact interacts with an unmodeled, potentially stiff environment. If tracking errors or neural policy oscillations pump unmeasured energy into the interface, the closed-loop system undergoes contact chattering, limit cycles, or high-force impact spikes that damage both the workpiece and the robot&#x27;s transmission.\n\nTo bound active energy exchange, the enforcer augments the controller state with an **energy tank** $\\EnergyTankLevel(\\TimeInstant)$ [@ferraguti2013tank; @hogan1985impedance] grounded in passivity. A passive system can return no more energy than its initial stored energy plus the energy it absorbs, so a power-preserving interconnection with a passive environment retains a passive energy balance (while a stability claim additionally requires the appropriate dissipation and state-observability conditions). The tank enforces that property by bookkeeping, acting as a software tally of work rather than a physical store of energy. Every joule that virtual damping removes from the arm&#x27;s motion is credited to the tank, and the controller may spend actively only what the tank holds, namely its accumulated credits plus a fixed initial reserve. In this contact formulation, $\\ActiveControlTorque$ denotes the active torque component excluding damping, so the total controller torque is $\\ActiveControlTorque-\\VirtualDampingMatrix\\GeneralizedVelocities$. The damping term can credit the tank only to the extent that the actuator actually realizes the corresponding dissipative work, and using total torque in the debit while also crediting damping would count the same dissipation twice. With those components separated, the modeled balance is:\n$$\\EnergyTankPower = \\GeneralizedVelocities^\\top \\VirtualDampingMatrix\\,\\GeneralizedVelocities - \\ActiveControlTorque^\\top \\GeneralizedVelocities$$ {#eq-enforce-energy-tank}\nwhere $\\GeneralizedVelocities^\\top \\VirtualDampingMatrix\\,\\GeneralizedVelocities \\ge 0$ is the dissipative power credited under qualified torque tracking and $\\ActiveControlTorque^\\top \\GeneralizedVelocities$ is active mechanical power. Saturation, delay, or tracking error requires conservative bounds on realized work rather than credit for an unrealized damping request. The enforcer then defines the energy-tank barrier certificate of @eq-enforce-energy-cbf:\n$$\\EnergyTankBarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState, \\EnergyTankLevel) = \\EnergyTankLevel - \\EnergyTankReserve \\ge 0$$ {#eq-enforce-energy-cbf}\nwhere $\\EnergyTankReserve &gt; 0$ is a reserved baseline. Combined with a hard contact-force limit $\\ContactForceBarrierFunction(\\MeasuredContactForce) = \\ContactForceLimit - \\|\\MeasuredContactForce\\| \\ge 0$, the enforcer establishes the in-contact viable set $\\InContactViableSet$. While $\\EnergyTankLevel &gt; \\EnergyTankReserve$ and $\\|\\MeasuredContactForce\\| &lt; \\ContactForceLimit$, candidate neural actions are admitted to perform manipulation. If the task consumes the allocated energy budget ($\\EnergyTankLevel \\to \\EnergyTankReserve$) or contact force approaches $\\ContactForceLimit$, the barrier filter clamps active energy injection ($\\ActiveControlTorque^\\top \\GeneralizedVelocities \\le 0$), restricting active work under the modeled power balance. This energy constraint does not by itself bound contact force, because a passive interaction with a stiff surface can still produce a large force. The force channel needs its own sensing-error, response-delay, and actuator bounds before the combined constraints can justify continued contact.\n\n: **Dissipativity**: @willems1972dissipative generalized circuit passivity to any dynamical system with a storage function, an energy-like quantity that can grow no faster than the power supplied to the system. Interconnected passive systems inherit that bound, which is why contact with an unknown but passive environment can be kept stable without a model of the environment. The energy tank is a storage function that the controller keeps explicitly in software, so the bound holds only as far as the tank&#x27;s bookkeeping matches the work the actuators actually do. \n\nFor a contact command, integrating the work balance in @eq-enforce-energy-tank over one control interval $\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$ supplies the energy allowance for the same least-change decision. Suppose the current tank level is ${\\EnergyTankLevel}_0\\ge \\EnergyTankReserve$, a conservative lower bound on damping power actually realized is $\\RealizedDampingPower$, and a bounded contact-direction speed $\\ContactDirectionSpeed&gt;0$ is held over this simplified interval. The spendable work is then ${\\EnergyTankLevel}_0-\\EnergyTankReserve+\\RealizedDampingPower\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$. Because a proposed constant force $\\ProposedContactForce \\ge 0$ debits $\\ProposedContactForce \\ContactDirectionSpeed \\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant$, the energy allowance caps force at $\\EnergyLimitedContactForce=({\\EnergyTankLevel}_0-\\EnergyTankReserve+\\RealizedDampingPower\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)/(\\ContactDirectionSpeed\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant)$. Letting $\\ContactForceCap$ denote the smaller of the deliverable force and the contact-force ceiling after sensing and delay allowances, the admitted command under these stipulated scalar conditions is $\\AdmittedContactForce = \\min(\\ProposedContactForce, \\EnergyLimitedContactForce, \\ContactForceCap)$, whose debit leaves ${\\EnergyTankLevel}_1={\\EnergyTankLevel}_0+\\RealizedDampingPower\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant-\\AdmittedContactForce \\ContactDirectionSpeed\\FiniteChange \\TimeInstant\\ge \\EnergyTankReserve$ while keeping force below the independent cap. The controller can therefore continue useful contact with a reduced force instead of abandoning the task. At the reserve with no credited damping, however, no positive active work is admitted. Zero speed requires a separate force check rather than division by speed, and delayed or uncertain power requires conservative work bounds over the interval.\n\nThe force channel&#x27;s own bounds can be computed for the running machine&#x27;s two contacts: the cage door, which the arm pushes on purpose, and the coworker&#x27;s hand, which it must never press too hard. Each calculation turns a force limit into a speed that the enforcement record carries as a channel ceiling, and each rests on premises that only a measurement can settle. They are derived here, where the channel is enforced; the release decision consumes the results as premises.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ Contact force and speed ceilings are enforced as release premises in @sec-release-question.\n:::\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ CONTACT FORCE AT THE DOOR AND AT THE HANDOVER</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: @sec-enforcement-intentional-contact (latch and handover paragraphs,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @eq-release-trip-indentation, @eq-release-coupled-indentation,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @eq-release-unbraked-force), @fig-10-latch-force-budget.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    derive once, where the channel is enforced, the latch force at</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          brake onset and the stop it requires, and the handover contact</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          force and speed ceilings; release cites these as premises.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    latch force-rise rate, force at onset, remaining compression, arm</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          deceleration and stop time at the guarded and unsupported</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          approaches; hand-arm reduced mass, transient force at the TCP limit</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          and at the handover speed, clamped force, both speed ceilings.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     calc_latch_contact_stopping_profile (AISLE latch fields);</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          calc_reduced_mass, calc_transient_impact_force,</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          calc_transient_contact_speed_limit with the ISO/TS 15066 hand body</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          model (AISLE.m_contact_human, AISLE.k_contact_human).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import <em> from mlsysim.core.units import </em> from mlsysim.physics.robotics import ( calc_latch_contact_stopping_profile, calc_reduced_mass, calc_transient_impact_force, calc_transient_contact_speed_limit, ) from mlsysim.fmt import ( fmt_velocity, fmt_force, fmt_force_rate, fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_acceleration, fmt_mass, fmt_stiffness, fmt_multiple, fmt_sci_qty, fmt, fmt_int, check, )</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class EnforcementContactForce:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v_latch = AISLE.v_latch_approach  # category: chosen (guarded approach) v_latch_high = AISLE.v_latch_high  # category: illustrative (policy's proposed approach) k_latch = AISLE.k_latch  # category: illustrative f_trip = AISLE.f_latch_tripwire  # category: illustrative t_contact = AISLE.t_contact_response  # category: illustrative (contact loop) f_lim = AISLE.f_latch_limit  # category: illustrative m_eff = RM.arm_effective_contact_mass  # category: derived m_hand = AISLE.m_contact_human  # category: datasheet (ISO/TS 15066 hand body model) k_hand = AISLE.k_contact_human  # category: datasheet (ISO/TS 15066 hand body model) f_contact = AISLE.f_contact_criterion  # category: chosen v_handover = AISLE.v_handover  # category: chosen (handover approach under accept_item) tcp_limit = RM.tcp_speed_limit  # category: chosen (free-space TCP limit)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>stop_lo = calc_latch_contact_stopping_profile(v_latch, scenario=AISLE) stop_hi = calc_latch_contact_stopping_profile(v_latch_high, scenario=AISLE) df_dt = stop_lo[\"df_dt\"] f_cmd = stop_lo[\"f_at_brake_onset\"] x_rem = stop_lo[\"x_remaining\"] a_arm = stop_lo[\"a_arm_needed\"] t_arm_stop = stop_lo[\"t_arm_stop\"] f_cmd_hi = stop_hi[\"f_at_brake_onset\"] a_arm_hi = stop_hi[\"a_arm_needed\"] m_red = calc_reduced_mass(m_eff, m_hand).to(kilogram) f_at_tcp_limit = calc_transient_impact_force(tcp_limit, m_eff, k_hand, human_mass=m_hand).to(newton) f_ratio = (f_at_tcp_limit / f_contact).to(\"dimensionless\").magnitude f_handover = calc_transient_impact_force(v_handover, m_eff, k_hand, human_mass=m_hand).to(newton) f_handover_clamped = calc_transient_impact_force(v_handover, m_eff, k_hand).to(newton) v_ceiling = calc_transient_contact_speed_limit(f_contact, m_eff, k_hand, human_mass=m_hand).to(meter / second) v_ceiling_clamped = calc_transient_contact_speed_limit(f_contact, m_eff, k_hand).to(meter / second)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">Notebook operands: sqrt(k_H * mu) for the free and the clamped hand (N per m/s)</h1>\n<p>imp_free = ((k_hand <em> m_red) </em><em> 0.5).to(newton / (meter / second)) imp_clamped = ((k_hand </em> m_eff) ** 0.5).to(newton / (meter / second)) clamp_ratio = (imp_clamped / imp_free).to(\"dimensionless\").magnitude</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(f_cmd.to(newton).magnitude - 39.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"Latch force at brake onset, guarded approach, is 39 N.\") check(abs(x_rem.to(millimeter).magnitude - 0.1525) &lt; 5e-5, \"Remaining latch compression is 0.15 mm.\") check(abs(a_arm.to(meter / second<strong>2).magnitude - 2.95) &lt; 5e-3, \"The guarded stop needs about 3 m/s^2 (2.95).\") check(abs(t_arm_stop.to(millisecond).magnitude - 10.0) &lt; 0.5, \"The guarded stop takes about 10 ms.\") check(abs(f_cmd_hi.to(newton).magnitude - 95.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"Latch force at brake onset, unsupported approach, is 95 N.\") check(abs(a_arm_hi.to(meter / second</strong>2).magnitude - 400.0) &lt; 1e-6, \"The unsupported approach needs 400 m/s^2.\") check(f_cmd &lt; f_lim and f_cmd_hi &lt; f_lim, \"Both approaches reach brake onset below the latch limit.\") check(abs(m_eff.to(kilogram).magnitude - 12.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"Arm effective contact mass is 12.0 kg.\") check(abs(m_red.magnitude - 0.5714) &lt; 5e-5, \"Hand-arm reduced mass is 0.571 kg.\") check(abs(f_at_tcp_limit.magnitude - 207.02) &lt; 0.01, \"Transient force at the TCP limit is 207 N.\") check(abs(f_handover.magnitude - 20.70) &lt; 0.01, \"Transient handover force is 20.7 N.\") check(abs(f_handover_clamped.magnitude - 94.87) &lt; 0.01, \"Clamped handover force is 94.9 N.\") check(f_handover &lt; f_contact &lt; f_handover_clamped, \"Transient passes and clamped fails the criterion.\") check(abs(v_ceiling.magnitude - 0.2415) &lt; 5e-5, \"Free-hand ceiling is 0.24 m/s.\") check(abs(v_ceiling_clamped.magnitude - 0.0527) &lt; 5e-5, \"Clamped-hand ceiling is 0.053 m/s.\") check(v_ceiling_clamped &lt; v_handover &lt; v_ceiling, \"The handover cap sits between the clamped and free-hand ceilings.\") check(abs((v_handover <em> imp_free).to(newton).magnitude - f_handover.magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"Notebook: v </em> sqrt(k mu) reproduces the free-hand force.\") check( abs((f_contact / imp_clamped).to(meter / second).magnitude - v_ceiling_clamped.magnitude) &lt; 1e-9, \"Notebook: F_h / sqrt(k m_eff) reproduces the clamped ceiling.\", ) check(4.0 &lt; clamp_ratio &lt; 5.0, \"Clamping multiplies the force by between four and five.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">margin_handover_clamp_force_envelope.svg fig-alt hand-types 20.7 N, 94.9 N and the 50 N criterion.</h1>\n<p>check(abs(f_contact.to(newton).magnitude - 50.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"The handover force criterion is 50 N.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v_latch_str = fmt_velocity(v_latch, unit=meter / second, precision=2) v_latch_high_str = fmt_velocity(v_latch_high, unit=meter / second, precision=2) k_latch_str = fmt_sci_qty(k_latch, newton / meter, precision=1) f_trip_str = fmt_force(f_trip, precision=0) t_contact_str = fmt_latency(t_contact, unit=millisecond, precision=0) df_dt_nms_str = fmt_force_rate(df_dt, unit=newton / millisecond, precision=0) f_cmd_str = fmt_force(f_cmd, precision=0) f_cmd_high_str = fmt_force(f_cmd_hi, precision=0) f_lim_str = fmt_force(f_lim, precision=0) x_remaining_str = fmt_length(round(x_rem.to(millimeter).magnitude, 2) <em> millimeter, unit=millimeter, precision=2) a_arm_str = fmt_acceleration(round(a_arm.to(meter / second</em><em>2).magnitude) </em> meter / second<strong>2, unit=meter / second</strong>2, precision=0) a_arm_high_str = fmt_acceleration(a_arm_hi, unit=meter / second*<em>2, precision=0) t_arm_stop_str = fmt_latency(round(t_arm_stop.to(millisecond).magnitude) </em> millisecond, unit=millisecond, precision=0) m_eff_str = fmt_mass(m_eff, unit=kilogram, precision=0) m_hand_str = fmt_mass(m_hand, unit=kilogram, precision=1) k_hand_str = fmt_stiffness(k_hand, unit=newton / millimeter, precision=0) m_red_str = fmt_mass(m_red, unit=kilogram, precision=2) tcp_limit_str = fmt_velocity(tcp_limit, unit=meter / second, precision=0) f_tcp_str = fmt_force(round(f_at_tcp_limit.magnitude) <em> newton, precision=0) f_ratio_mult_str = fmt_multiple(f_ratio, precision=1) f_contact_str = fmt_force(f_contact, precision=0) v_handover_str = fmt_velocity(v_handover, unit=meter / second, precision=2) f_handover_str = fmt_force(f_handover, precision=1) f_handover_clamped_str = fmt_force(f_handover_clamped, precision=1) v_ceiling_str = fmt_velocity(round(v_ceiling.magnitude, 2) </em> meter / second, unit=meter / second, precision=2) v_ceiling_clamped_str = fmt_velocity(round(v_ceiling_clamped.magnitude, 3) * meter / second, unit=meter / second, precision=3) k_hand_nm_str = fmt(k_hand.to(newton / meter).magnitude, precision=0, commas=True) m_red_val_str = fmt(m_red.magnitude, precision=3) m_eff_val_str = fmt(m_eff.to(kilogram).magnitude, precision=0) m_hand_val_str = fmt(m_hand.to(kilogram).magnitude, precision=1) v_handover_val_str = fmt(v_handover.to(meter / second).magnitude, precision=2) f_contact_val_str = fmt(f_contact.to(newton).magnitude, precision=0) imp_free_str = fmt_int(round(imp_free.magnitude)) imp_clamped_str = fmt_int(round(imp_clamped.magnitude)) clamp_ratio_str = fmt(clamp_ratio, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<p>At the door the quantity that matters is how fast force rises. Against a strike plate of stiffness $\\LatchPlateStiffness$, an arm approaching at $\\LatchApproachSpeed$ raises the contact force at $d\\NormalContactForce/d\\TimeInstant = \\LatchPlateStiffness\\,\\LatchApproachSpeed$, which at the guarded <code>[EnforcementContactForce.v_latch_str]</code> against the <code>[EnforcementContactForce.k_latch_str]</code> plate is <code>[EnforcementContactForce.df_dt_nms_str]</code>. The contact tripwire fires at $\\ContactTripwireForce =$ <code>[EnforcementContactForce.f_trip_str]</code>, but the contact loop needs $\\ContactLoopResponseTime =$ <code>[EnforcementContactForce.t_contact_str]</code> for detection, command delivery, and torque rise, and the plate keeps compressing by $\\LatchApproachSpeed \\ContactLoopResponseTime$ before deceleration begins. The force at physical braking onset is therefore $$\\BrakingOnsetContactForce = \\ContactTripwireForce + \\LatchPlateStiffness\\,\\LatchApproachSpeed\\,\\ContactLoopResponseTime$$ {#eq-release-trip-indentation} which evaluates to <code>[EnforcementContactForce.f_cmd_str]</code>, well inside the <code>[EnforcementContactForce.f_lim_str]</code> latch limit. The limit still binds the stop that follows, because the plate goes on compressing while the arm sheds its remaining kinetic energy: $$\\MaximumPlateIndentation = \\frac{\\BrakingOnsetContactForce}{\\LatchPlateStiffness} + \\frac{\\LatchApproachSpeed^2}{2\\ArmStoppingDeceleration} \\le \\frac{\\LatchForceLimit}{\\LatchPlateStiffness}$$ {#eq-release-coupled-indentation} Against time since first contact (@fig-10-latch-force-budget), force climbs through the tripwire and across the loop-response window to <code>[EnforcementContactForce.f_cmd_str]</code> at brake onset; the commanded stop then bends the curve over to finish at the <code>[EnforcementContactForce.f_lim_str]</code> latch limit, while an unbraked run crosses the limit within a few milliseconds. The braking term ignores the plate's spring work, which would help decelerate the arm, so the bound is conservative. Below the limit the plate can compress only <code>[EnforcementContactForce.x_remaining_str]</code> further, and stopping within that distance requires about <code>[EnforcementContactForce.a_arm_str]</code>, an illustrative stop of about <code>[EnforcementContactForce.t_arm_stop_str]</code>.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ LATCH CONTACT FORCE AGAINST TIME (FIGURE)\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: @fig-10-latch-force-budget\n# │\n# │ Goal:    show why the stop must be sized from the force already reached at\n# │          brake command, not from the tripwire.\n# │ Show:    rise at k*v to the 15 N tripwire, 2 ms response to 39 N, the sized\n# │          arm stop peaking at the 100 N limit; the unbraked rise for contrast.\n# │ How:     reads calc-enforcement-contact-force (EnforcementContactForce);\n# │          guards that the drawn profile hits 39 N at command and 100 N at rest.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom mlsysim import viz\nfrom mlsysim.core.units import millisecond, newton, meter, second\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import check\n\n# ┌── 1. LOAD (values from calc-enforcement-contact-force) ────────────────────\nK = EnforcementContactForce.k_latch.to(newton / meter).magnitude  # N/m\nV = EnforcementContactForce.v_latch.to(meter / second).magnitude  # m/s\nF_TRIP = EnforcementContactForce.f_trip.to(newton).magnitude\nF_LIM = EnforcementContactForce.f_lim.to(newton).magnitude\nT_RESP = EnforcementContactForce.t_contact.to(second).magnitude\nF_CMD = EnforcementContactForce.f_cmd.to(newton).magnitude\nA_ARM = EnforcementContactForce.a_arm.to(meter / second**2).magnitude\nT_STOP = EnforcementContactForce.t_arm_stop.to(second).magnitude\n\n# ┌── 2. EXECUTE: force from first contact through the arm stop ──────────────\nt_trip = F_TRIP / (K * V)\nt_cmd = t_trip + T_RESP\ntau = np.linspace(0.0, T_STOP, 200)\nf_brake = F_CMD + K * (V * tau - 0.5 * A_ARM * tau**2)\nt_rise = np.linspace(0.0, t_cmd, 50)\nf_rise = K * V * t_rise\ncheck(abs(f_rise[-1] - F_CMD) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;latch figure: rise reaches the brake-command force&quot;)\ncheck(abs(f_brake[-1] - F_LIM) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;latch figure: the sized stop peaks exactly at the latch limit&quot;)\nms = 1000.0\nt_end = (t_cmd + T_STOP) * ms\nt_nb = np.linspace(t_cmd, t_end, 50)\nf_nb = K * V * t_nb / ms\n\n# ┌── 3. PLOT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nR = viz.VOL4_ROLES\nviz.set_book_style()\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.2, 3.6))\nax.grid(False)\nfor s in (&quot;top&quot;, &quot;right&quot;):\n    ax.spines[s].set_visible(False)\nY_TOP = 1.45 * F_LIM\nax.axhspan(F_LIM, Y_TOP, color=R[&quot;consequence&quot;], alpha=0.07, lw=0)\nax.axhline(F_LIM, color=R[&quot;consequence&quot;], lw=1.2, ls=(0, (5, 3)))\nax.text(t_end * 1.02, F_LIM + 3, f&quot;latch limit {F_LIM:.0f} N&quot;, ha=&quot;right&quot;, va=&quot;bottom&quot;, fontsize=9, color=R[&quot;consequence&quot;])\nax.axhline(F_TRIP, color=R[&quot;neutral&quot;], lw=0.8, ls=(0, (1, 2)))\nax.text(t_end * 1.02, F_TRIP + 2, f&quot;tripwire {F_TRIP:.0f} N&quot;, ha=&quot;right&quot;, va=&quot;bottom&quot;, fontsize=9, color=R[&quot;neutral&quot;])\nax.axvspan(t_trip * ms, t_cmd * ms, color=&quot;#D97706&quot;, alpha=0.14, lw=0)\nax.text((t_trip + t_cmd) / 2 * ms, Y_TOP * 0.93, f&quot;response\\n{T_RESP * ms:.0f} ms&quot;, ha=&quot;center&quot;, va=&quot;top&quot;, fontsize=9, color=&quot;#B45309&quot;)\nax.plot(t_nb, f_nb, color=R[&quot;consequence&quot;], lw=1.4, ls=(0, (5, 3)), clip_on=True)\nax.text(t_nb[18], min(f_nb[18], Y_TOP) + 2, &quot;no brake&quot;, ha=&quot;right&quot;, va=&quot;bottom&quot;, fontsize=9, color=R[&quot;consequence&quot;])\nax.plot(t_rise * ms, f_rise, color=R[&quot;structural&quot;], lw=2.0)\nax.plot((t_cmd + tau) * ms, f_brake, color=R[&quot;structural&quot;], lw=2.0)\nax.plot([t_cmd * ms], [F_CMD], &quot;o&quot;, ms=5, color=R[&quot;structural&quot;])\nax.annotate(\n    f&quot;brake command {F_CMD:.0f} N&quot;,\n    xy=(t_cmd * ms, F_CMD),\n    xytext=(t_cmd * ms + 1.0, F_CMD - 16),\n    fontsize=9,\n    color=R[&quot;structural&quot;],\n    va=&quot;top&quot;,\n    arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle=&quot;-&quot;, color=R[&quot;structural&quot;], lw=0.7),\n)\nax.text(\n    (t_cmd + 0.58 * T_STOP) * ms,\n    F_CMD + 22,\n    f&quot;arm stop ≈ {A_ARM:.0f} m/s², ≈ {T_STOP * ms:.0f} ms&quot;,\n    ha=&quot;center&quot;,\n    va=&quot;top&quot;,\n    fontsize=9,\n    color=R[&quot;structural&quot;],\n)\nax.set_xlim(0, t_end * 1.03)\nax.set_ylim(0, Y_TOP)\nax.set_xlabel(&quot;time since first contact (ms)&quot;, fontsize=9)\nax.set_ylabel(&quot;contact force (N)&quot;, fontsize=9)\nax.tick_params(labelsize=9)\nfig.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n]```\n\n:::\n\nThe same two equations show why the approach speed is an enforced channel rather than a preference. At the chunk policy&#x27;s proposed `[EnforcementContactForce.v_latch_high_str]` (@sec-enforcement-authority-refuse), @eq-release-trip-indentation already gives `[EnforcementContactForce.f_cmd_high_str]` at braking onset, and @eq-release-coupled-indentation then demands an arm deceleration of `[EnforcementContactForce.a_arm_high_str]`. The faster approach spends the response interval on force that the stop can no longer absorb. This is the physical half of the refusal in @sec-enforcement-authority-refuse; the evidential half is that no trial sampled this speed. Both results treat the arm as rigid and the plate as the whole contact stiffness. Gripper, wrist-sensor, and joint compliance in series lower the effective stiffness and slow the rise, while a geared joint reflects rotor inertia into the moving mass. Whether this arm achieves the required deceleration within a fraction of a millimeter depends on joint torque, drive rise time, and structural stiffness that only arm stopping trials establish. A compliant element placed in series on purpose turns that softening into passive protection. Springs in series add their compliances, so the combined stiffness falls below that of the softer element and the force rises more slowly, a protection that holds even when the enforcer cannot stop in time. The stiffness of that element is therefore part of the contact margin, and @sec-frontier-passive-compliance-containment sizes it for this latch.\n\nThe coworker&#x27;s hand is the opposite case, a contact the task tolerates only up to a bounded peak force. The force depends first on which contact occurs. In a *transient* contact the hand is free and recoils, so both bodies move and the collision acts through their reduced mass $\\ReducedImpactMass$. In a *clamped* (quasi-static) contact the hand is trapped against a rack upright or the robot&#x27;s own body, cannot recoil, and receives the full robot-side effective mass at the tool center point, $\\EffectiveImpactMass =$ `[EnforcementContactForce.m_eff_str]`. The biomechanical body model of ISO/TS 15066:2016, whose collaborative-application requirements ISO 10218-2:2025 now incorporates (@sec-enforcement-certified-functions), assigns the hand and fingers an effective mass $\\BodyRegionMass$ of `[EnforcementContactForce.m_hand_str]` and an effective spring constant $\\HertzianContactStiffness$ of `[EnforcementContactForce.k_hand_str]` [@isots15066; @iso10218part2; @kirschner2022iso15066]. This worked example estimates the robot-side mass as half the arm&#x27;s moving mass plus its payload capacity; it does not compute the configuration- and direction-dependent mass reflected through the contact Jacobian. In an unbraked elastic contact the peak force is\n$$\\UnbrakedImpactForce = \\ContactApproachSpeed\\,\\sqrt{\\HertzianContactStiffness\\,\\ReducedImpactMass}, \\qquad \\ReducedImpactMass = \\left(\\frac{1}{\\BodyRegionMass} + \\frac{1}{\\EffectiveImpactMass}\\right)^{-1}$$ {#eq-release-unbraked-force}\nThis is the spring-mass bound of @eq-training-impact-peak, which equated a moving mass&#x27;s kinetic energy to the peak elastic energy of the contact, with one change: two bodies move. Momentum conservation keeps the pair&#x27;s common (center-of-mass) motion unchanged through the contact, so only the kinetic energy of their relative motion, $\\frac{1}{2}\\ReducedImpactMass \\ContactApproachSpeed^2$ for a closing speed $\\ContactApproachSpeed$, can compress the hand, and the reduced mass takes the place of the single moving mass. For this hand and arm, $\\ReducedImpactMass$ is `[EnforcementContactForce.m_red_str]`, because the light hand dominates the sum; a clamped hand is the limit $\\BodyRegionMass \\to \\infty$, in which $\\ReducedImpactMass$ becomes $\\EffectiveImpactMass$. Solved for speed at a force criterion $\\HandForceCriterion$, the equation gives the channel ceiling $\\ContactApproachSpeed = \\HandForceCriterion/\\sqrt{\\HertzianContactStiffness\\,\\ReducedImpactMass}$. The peak follows the assumed masses and stiffness; the model establishes neither an impact duration nor a pressure or injury limit.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The single-mass spring-impact bound that this reduced-mass form extends is established in @sec-training-contact-exploit.\n:::\n\nAt the arm&#x27;s `[EnforcementContactForce.tcp_limit_str]` free-space limit even the transient model predicts `[EnforcementContactForce.f_tcp_str]`, or `[EnforcementContactForce.f_ratio_mult_str]` the `[EnforcementContactForce.f_contact_str]` criterion, so the arm cannot enter an unseparated contact at that speed. At the handover speed the two contact cases bound the approach very differently (notebook \\ref{nbk-enforcement-clamped-hand-penalty}).\n\n::: {#nbk-enforcement-clamped-hand-penalty .callout-notebook title=&quot;The clamped-hand penalty&quot;}\n\n**Problem**: The arm approaches the coworker&#x27;s hand at the handover speed. What peak force does @eq-release-unbraked-force predict for a free hand that recoils and for a hand clamped against a rack upright, and what approach speed does the force criterion allow in each case?\n\n**Variables**:\n\n* $\\EffectiveImpactMass =$ `[EnforcementContactForce.m_eff_str]` (Derived; the simplified half-arm-mass plus payload-capacity estimate of the contact model).\n* $\\BodyRegionMass =$ `[EnforcementContactForce.m_hand_str]` and $\\HertzianContactStiffness =$ `[EnforcementContactForce.k_hand_str]` (Datasheet; the ISO/TS 15066 hand body model).\n* $\\ContactApproachSpeed =$ `[EnforcementContactForce.v_handover_str]` (Chosen; the handover approach while the coworker holds the `accept_item` grant that @sec-intervention-human-authority defines).\n* $\\HandForceCriterion =$ `[EnforcementContactForce.f_contact_str]` (Chosen; the handover force criterion).\n\n**Math**: For a free hand, $\\ReducedImpactMass =$ (1/`[EnforcementContactForce.m_hand_val_str]` + 1/`[EnforcementContactForce.m_eff_val_str]`)$^{-1} =$ `[EnforcementContactForce.m_red_val_str]` kg, and with $\\HertzianContactStiffness =$ `[EnforcementContactForce.k_hand_nm_str]` N/m the stiffness term is $\\sqrt{\\HertzianContactStiffness\\ReducedImpactMass} =$ `[EnforcementContactForce.imp_free_str]` N per m/s. The peak force is $\\ContactApproachSpeed\\sqrt{\\HertzianContactStiffness\\ReducedImpactMass} =$ `[EnforcementContactForce.v_handover_val_str]` $\\times$ `[EnforcementContactForce.imp_free_str]` $=$ `[EnforcementContactForce.f_handover_str]`, and the criterion admits $\\HandForceCriterion/\\sqrt{\\HertzianContactStiffness\\ReducedImpactMass} =$ `[EnforcementContactForce.f_contact_val_str]` $/$ `[EnforcementContactForce.imp_free_str]`, or `[EnforcementContactForce.v_ceiling_str]`. A clamped hand cannot recoil, so $\\ReducedImpactMass$ becomes $\\EffectiveImpactMass$ and $\\sqrt{\\HertzianContactStiffness \\EffectiveImpactMass} =$ `[EnforcementContactForce.imp_clamped_str]` N per m/s. The same approach then delivers `[EnforcementContactForce.v_handover_val_str]` $\\times$ `[EnforcementContactForce.imp_clamped_str]` $=$ `[EnforcementContactForce.f_handover_clamped_str]`, and the criterion admits only `[EnforcementContactForce.f_contact_val_str]` $/$ `[EnforcementContactForce.imp_clamped_str]`, or `[EnforcementContactForce.v_ceiling_clamped_str]`.\n\n**Result**: The free hand sees `[EnforcementContactForce.f_handover_str]`, inside the criterion; the clamped hand sees `[EnforcementContactForce.f_handover_clamped_str]`, which fails it.\n\n**Systems insight**: Clamping multiplies the force at any speed by $\\sqrt{\\EffectiveImpactMass/\\ReducedImpactMass} =$ `[EnforcementContactForce.clamp_ratio_str]`, because the whole arm-side mass, not the light hand, sets the collision. A speed ceiling derived for a free hand is therefore a geometric premise as much as a kinematic one.\n\n:::\n\n\n\nThe handover channel is therefore a transient-contact ceiling. It holds only while the handover geometry excludes clamping, with the handover point in free space and no rack upright, tote edge, or link of the arm within reach of the hand along the approach. Both contacts end the same way. The arithmetic is exact; what it cannot supply is the premise it rests on, whether an achievable arm deceleration, a contact stiffness, or a clamp-free geometry. The enforcement record keeps each ceiling beside that premise, and the same discipline governs every barrier in this chapter.\n\n### The conditions forward invariance rests on {#sec-enforcement-invariance-conditions}\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ INTER-SAMPLE DRIFT UNDER ZERO-ORDER HOLD</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: the discretization condition below and the ZOH fallacy.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    size the inter-sample buffer v T + a T^2 / 2 on the base.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    drive limit, independent approach-acceleration upper bound, buffer.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Scenarios.WarehouseBaseHoldDrift (RM.base.max_velocity, the</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          stipulated approach upper bound, AISLE.t_tick).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_length, fmt_velocity, fmt_acceleration, check</p>\n<p>HOLD = Scenarios.WarehouseBaseHoldDrift</p>\n<p>class EnforcementZOHBuffer:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v_max = HOLD.speed  # category: illustrative (drive limit) a_approach_upper = ( HOLD.acceleration )  # scenario assumption; category: illustrative (independent approach-acceleration upper bound, not credited braking) t_ctrl = HOLD.period  # category: chosen</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>d_zoh = HOLD.drift.to(millimeter)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(d_zoh.magnitude - 1.501) &lt; 1e-9, \"The ZOH buffer at the drive limit is 1.501 mm.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v_str = fmt_velocity(v_max, precision=1) a_str = fmt_acceleration(a_approach_upper, precision=0) dzoh_str = fmt_length(d_zoh, unit=millimeter, precision=3) ]```</p>\n<p>Unmodeled friction loss, actuator saturation, solver overruns, or inter-sample drift under a held torque command can each push a physical plant across a barrier boundary even when the continuous-time inequality is satisfied at the sample instant. The containment claim provided by both spatial and energetic barrier calculations is therefore strictly conditional. A forward-invariance claim requires a feasible barrier and the following bounded conditions; the list is necessary for this design, not an exhaustive if-and-only-if test:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Model Fidelity:</strong> The nominal plant dynamics $\\ControlAffineDriftField(\\MechanicalPlantState)$ and $\\ControlAffineInputField(\\MechanicalPlantState)$ must capture the true system dynamics within a validated parameter error bound.</li>\n<li><strong>Disturbance Bounds:</strong> External forces and unmodeled friction acting on the mass must remain strictly within the modeled disturbance envelope $|\\ExternalDisturbanceForce(\\TimeInstant)| \\le \\DisturbanceForceBound$.</li>\n<li><strong>Actuation Authority:</strong> Physical actuators must possess sufficient voltage, current, and thermal headroom to realize the commanded corrective force within a qualified delivered-effort error and response-delay envelope, as well as without saturation ($\\ActuatorEffort^* \\in [\\MinimumActuatorEffort, \\MaximumActuatorEffort]$).</li>\n<li><strong>Solver Determinism:</strong> The optimization solver or projection algorithm must terminate strictly within its allocated execution budget ($\\FilterSolveTime \\le \\FilterSolveBudget &lt; \\PermissionLoopPeriod$).</li>\n<li><strong>Discretization Invariance:</strong> The discrete sampling interval $\\PermissionLoopPeriod$ must be sufficiently small that inter-sample state drift does not breach the continuous-time invariance boundary.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The approach-acceleration upper bound is a separate premise from the lower bound on available braking, and equality of their illustrative magnitudes does not identify their roles. Each of the five conditions maps to a dedicated engineering budget: model fidelity and disturbance bounds come from system identification and bench load testing, actuation authority from dynamometer curves and thermal limits, solver determinism from static worst-case execution time analysis on the MCU, and discretization from the loop period itself. Because the drive holds the last admitted command between samples (a zero-order hold), the plant drifts for one full period before the next check can act on it. The drift covers at most $\\FiniteChange \\ZohHoldTravel = \\BodySpeed \\PermissionLoopPeriod + \\frac{1}{2} \\ProfileAccelerationBound \\PermissionLoopPeriod^2$ at speed $\\BodySpeed$, where the worst case is the highest speed the base can reach rather than the ceiling $\\SpeedBound$ that admission enforces. At the <code>[EnforcementZOHBuffer.v_str]</code> drive limit, an independently stipulated <code>[EnforcementZOHBuffer.a_str]</code> upper bound on approach acceleration, and $\\PermissionLoopPeriod =$ <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.tick_ms_str]</code>, that drift is <code>[EnforcementZOHBuffer.dzoh_str]</code>, a small fraction of $\\ProtectiveClearanceMargin$. Tightening a raw-clearance check by this displacement preserves geometric separation over the hold interval under the stated motion bounds, whereas a kinetic or energy barrier requires a bound on its own evolution throughout the interval.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Zero-Order Hold</strong>: The order counts the derivatives the hold carries forward. A zero-order hold keeps the command's value constant until the next sample, while a first-order hold would also extrapolate its slope. For one control tick the plant therefore integrates a torque the controller can no longer change, which is the source of the drift buffer $\\BodySpeed \\PermissionLoopPeriod + \\frac{1}{2}\\ProfileAccelerationBound \\PermissionLoopPeriod^2$; @sec-appendix-control-zoh develops the hold's delay.</p>\n<p>A condition that no detector watches is an assumption, so the permission path attaches one to each. A <strong>disturbance observer</strong> compares predicted with measured acceleration on every cycle. Inverter status registers and current shunts report actuator saturation. A hardware watchdog and cycle counter time the solver against its deadline. Timestamp counters on the sensor bus catch jitter and missed samples. When a detector invalidates a premise, the enforcer cannot restore permission by substituting optimistic parameters. It selects a response whose sensing, effort, and clearance premises survive, as developed in @sec-enforcement-fallback-ladder. Both the free-space projection and contact decision must finish before the physical margin reserved for computation is spent.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h3\">Execution timing in the sub-millisecond loop</h3>\n<p>A correct barrier calculation delivered after the permission deadline cannot protect the margin reserved for that deadline. The whole cycle must therefore have a bounded execution time [@wilhelm2008worst], including state acquisition and the final drive transfer. A real-time operating system can reduce scheduling delays, but its name does not establish a bound on shared-memory or peripheral interference. The permission path's implementation must be qualified under the competing workloads that can delay it, so the declared cycle allocation is a timing requirement carried into loaded placement tests rather than a measured trace or a platform-independent solver guarantee.</p>\n<p>In this heterogeneous design, the shield executes inside a <code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.rate_str]</code> timer interrupt routine on the MCU. An unloaded cycle of acquisition, validation, and a typical active-set solve completes in about <code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.unloaded_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$, an illustrative estimate rather than a bound. The candidate path instead declares a worst-case execution-time requirement of <code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.wcet_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$ for the whole cycle, divided among six stages, and the whole sequence must finish inside the <code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.deadline_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$ deadline:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Interrupt Entry &amp; State Acquisition (<code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.acquisition_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$):</strong> The hardware timer triggers the interrupt service routine (ISR), and firmware latches the wheel and joint encoder registers and the latest inertial measurement unit (IMU) sample.</li>\n<li><strong>Proposal &amp; Lease Verification (<code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.proposal_lease_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$):</strong> The MCU makes one bounded read of its local mailbox, which the link receiver fills outside this routine, validates the checksum, and confirms sequence order before checking evidence age, the active chunk lease, and the host heartbeat timer. In the evidence-age check, the evidence's age at dispatch is the difference between the source frame's evidence timestamp and the host dispatch timestamp in the proposal header (@sec-nervous-supervisory-timers). That age may not exceed <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.age_threshold_str]</code>, which combines the <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.t_age_str]</code> observation age charged in row 5 of @tbl-enforcement-stopping-budget with <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.eps_sync_str]</code> of clock uncertainty (@sec-perception-observation-contract). Passing that test qualifies the evidence only at dispatch, so the MCU also reconstructs the evidence's current age from its source epoch and elapsed time and checks it against the evidence's own validity deadline and the remaining physical margin. Inference, admission, waiting, and transfer after dispatch must already be reserved once in that margin through the dispatch-to-admission allocation inside the timing allowance. Otherwise fresh evidence is required before activation, because a new proposal timestamp cannot renew old evidence. The MCU then verifies that elapsed time since valid admission or renewal has not exceeded the <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.lease_ms_str]</code> active chunk lease and reads the host heartbeat timer, which marks the host silent once no heartbeat has arrived within the <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.heartbeat_timeout_ms_str]</code> timeout.</li>\n<li><strong>Tracking Contract Verification (<code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.tracking_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$):</strong> Firmware evaluates instantaneous tracking error norm $\\|\\TrackingErrorDistance(\\TimeInstant)\\| = \\|\\CartesianPosition(\\TimeInstant) - \\ReferencePosition(\\TimeInstant)\\|$ against the qualified bound $\\TrackingErrorBound$.</li>\n<li><strong>CBF-QP Safety Shield Solve (<code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.qp_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$):</strong> An active-set QP solver running in pre-allocated static SRAM, the MCU's tightly coupled data memory, evaluates active barrier inequalities and computes $\\CertifiedControlAction = \\arg\\min_{\\ControlInput \\in \\mathbb{R}^\\ControlInputDimension} \\frac{1}{2}\\|\\ControlInput - \\NominalControlCommand\\|^2$. A fixed iteration cap limits the work count; meeting the allocation also requires bounded per-iteration arithmetic, memory access, interrupt interference, and output service, including ill-conditioned constraint geometry. Dynamic memory allocation is forbidden on this path so that heap service is excluded from that timing obligation. In this two-processor design no memory is shared with the host, so camera direct memory access (DMA) streams cannot reach the memory this cycle uses. The couplings that remain are the link receiver, whose DMA writes into the MCU's own memory, and any shared supply rail or clock; @sec-placement-hardware-allocation measures that residual interference and tests whether the isolation holds under load.</li>\n<li><strong>Output Selection &amp; Admitted Command Staging (<code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.write_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$):</strong> If the QP is feasible and constraints hold, the enforcer emits the <strong>admitted command</strong>. The drives close the velocity and current loops. Because industrial servo drives running in position mode choose torque direction internally from position tracking error while an unsigned torque limit clips only the magnitude of each joint's effort symmetrically, a position setpoint plus an unsigned torque limit does not by itself realize the signed braking effort or coupled multi-axis half-space the QP requires. This interface needs a qualified mapping from the staged command to delivered effort, with bounded realization error and response delay. The filter tightens each commanded half-space by the largest possible projection of that effort error along its normal, so every effort the drive may realize still satisfies the delivered-effort constraint. Near full braking, that allowance may remove feasibility and require earlier intervention. The drive configuration and qualification evidence must establish these bounds; this example supplies no measured error or delay. During the response interval, a previously held command must remain valid and the intersample/response allowance must cover its effect, charging each delay once. If the position/limit interface cannot satisfy this contract, the design needs a qualified effort interface or a stricter qualified operating envelope and fallback. The next position setpoint and per-axis torque limit travel in the next EtherCAT frame only under that realization contract. Drive-reported torque on the following tick can detect a breach but cannot retroactively certify the preceding interval; an invalid replacement leaves previously admitted setpoints in force only while their own evidence, lease, state, and stopping reservation remain valid.</li>\n<li><strong>Completion &amp; Reserve (<code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.reserve_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$):</strong> The MCU checks the register write and services its own watchdog only after the cycle succeeds. The reserve absorbs variation across Stages 1 through 5; a cycle that has not completed by the deadline requires a qualified preempting timer or an independent mechanism to select the resident fallback before the next tick. The overrunning ISR cannot be assumed to detect and recover from its own stall. Only with that bounded transfer does a missed tick cost the base <code>[EnforcementStoppingBudget.missed_tick_str]</code> of travel at the aisle speed. @Sec-placement-testing-separation tests whether the declared WCET holds under load, so that ticks are not missed.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>↪ The schedulability analysis that a declared WCET and deadline feed is developed in @sec-appendix-systems-timers.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Active-Set QP</strong>: An active-set solver guesses which constraints hold with equality, solves the resulting linear system, and adds or drops one constraint per iteration. Dual variants such as that of @goldfarb1983numerically start from the unconstrained optimum, here the nominal command itself, and add only violated constraints, so a proposal that already satisfies every barrier costs almost nothing. The iteration count is bounded by the combinations of the constraint set rather than by a clock, so a fixed iteration cap bounds steps while the time per step must still be measured under interference.</p>\n<p>Stage 5 is also where refusal takes effect. Any of several conditions triggers it. The chunk lease may have expired, the host heartbeat may have fallen silent, the shared drive bus may report undervoltage, tracking error $\\|\\TrackingErrorDistance(\\TimeInstant)\\|$ may exceed $\\TrackingErrorBound$, actuator saturation may render the feasible control set empty ($\\SafeControlSet = \\emptyset$), or an operating-contract detector such as the disturbance observer or the solver deadline may trip. In any of these cases, the output selector withholds the admitted command, revokes policy authority, and selects the state-matched stop. At standstill, lease or heartbeat expiry selects HOLD, the powered standstill hold that @sec-enforcement-fallback-ladder defines, only while powered support remains available. Undervoltage instead requires a qualified transition to mechanical restraint before that support expires. In motion, STOP under undervoltage remains conditional on drive survival.</p>\n<p>The barrier fixes which commands preserve the stop; the filter and its cycle decide whether the chosen command arrives in time.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Relative degree</strong>: Why does a barrier on raw distance give torque no control authority at the boundary, and what does the kinetic barrier of @eq-enforce-kinetic-barrier add to restore it?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Projection vs. clamp</strong>: Why can clamping each joint separately leave a coupled half-space violated, while @eq-enforce-cbf-projection satisfies it with the smallest torque change?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Projection estimate</strong>: Treat the latch approach as a one-dimensional speed command whose half-space is the <code>[EnforcementLatchFilter.v_latch_str]</code> target-ODD ceiling. What are $\\BarrierProjectionMultiplier^*$ and the admitted speed when the chunk policy proposes <code>[EnforcementLatchFilter.v_latch_high_str]</code>?</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Timing premise</strong>: Which of the five forward-invariance conditions do the declared <code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.wcet_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$ WCET and the <code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.deadline_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$ deadline defend, and what must select the fallback when a cycle overruns?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every stage of this cycle assumes that the solve returns a feasible command. When saturation empties the admissible torque set, the lease lapses, or the cycle overruns its deadline, projection has nothing to return, and the permission path must already hold a response it can execute without the proposer. Executing that check deterministically while surviving proposer faults requires placing the enforcer where the host processor cannot preempt or bypass it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h2\">Admission, Cyclic Checking, and Monitor Independence</h2>\n<p>A planned waypoint can clear a warehouse rack upright in the planner's geometric check while the physical manipulator overshoots into the steel during execution, and an instantaneous motor torque well within electrical limits can accelerate the arm past the point where its remaining stopping reservation can arrest motion. Relying on trajectory admission alone misses in-flight tracking overshoot and saturation, whereas checking only single-step torque commands without a verified stopping continuation lets the arm enter an inevitable collision state before the local filter reacts. Robust safety enforcement therefore demands both layers (the per-tick cycle of @sec-enforcement-loop-budget on a dedicated MCU and full-plan admission before motion begins). That raises three structural questions: why neither layer can replace the other, where the cyclic checker sits relative to the command stream, and how independent of the proposer the MCU must actually be.</p>\n<p>Full-plan admission judges the candidate trajectory, its seams, its swept clearance, and its verified stopping continuation before the machine commits to motion. The expensive geometry behind that judgment (queries of global occupancy octrees, signed distance fields, or mesh collision models) cannot run inside a <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.tick_ms_str]</code> control interrupt merely because the MCU owns final actuation privilege. It therefore runs on the host as a separately scheduled, bounded job, and its result reaches the MCU as the trajectory record. The MCU's own admission check then verifies that record against bounded, qualified local evidence (the resident stop suffix, its entry state, and the commitment deadline) without traversing the global map. The hard real-time cyclic path evaluates current joint state, remaining lease validity, and available actuator effort on every tick before selecting and dispatching the next physical command.</p>\n<p>An in-line checker places its worst-case execution time directly inside the control-loop deadline, consuming a portion of the available compute tick. A parallel monitor instead operates asynchronously alongside the proposal pipeline, avoiding added forward latency during nominal execution but contributing detection latency and trip delay to the physical response budget. Either architectural placement must ensure privilege isolation. The untrusted proposer must never be able to bypass the selected output, and the system must preserve the dedicated sensing and actuation channels required by the fallback controller. As @sec-placement-two-paths-one-die will show from the resources each candidate placement shares, isolating these execution domains across memory spaces or heterogeneous processing cores is what allows the safety monitor and its fallback mechanisms to survive the very proposer faults that trigger an intervention. The physical stopping budget supplies the non-negotiable temporal and spatial boundaries that this hardware split must satisfy.</p>\n<p>Any valid safety claim must explicitly define which proposer failure modes its sensing, compute, and actuation pathways survive. Placing a monitor on a separate physical core or processor does not establish true independence if that monitor shares a power rail, a memory bus, a sensor link, or an unisolated clock domain with the untrusted proposer. Shared hardware resources require provably bounded interference or a qualified, survivable fallback state. Any environmental condition that the monitor cannot observe directly must remain an explicit, documented operating premise rather than an unstated assumption.</p>\n<p>The running example assumes qualified drive, scanner, and arm safety functions and a rated lease/heartbeat watchdog; the release case must name the scoped evidence supporting those functions. Independence, however, is not certification. The runtime permission path is not the machine's certified functional safety layer, and beneath it sit the certified safety functions embedded within motor drives, safety laser scanners, and the dedicated safety controller. These hardware-interlocked mechanisms carry the formal integrity claims that certification bodies audit against industrial standards. The primary operational duty of the software permission path is to regulate the learned proposer proactively so that the robot never trips those certified hardware interlocks during normal operation. @Sec-enforcement-certified-functions maps each dynamic response developed throughout this chapter onto that underlying certified safety floor once the complete response hierarchy is established, and names the one narrow MCU function that does carry a rating.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h3\">Where the thesis bends</h3>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ A LEARNED POLICY AS THE CONTROL-RATE CONTROLLER\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: the &quot;where the thesis bends&quot; paragraphs immediately below.\n# │ Goal:    price what the lease row of the stopping budget becomes when the\n# │          policy itself emits a command every tick.\n# │ Show:    lease-path travel at the aisle speed for the chunk lease and for a\n# │          per-tick command stream.\n# │ How:     AISLE lease, tick, and bus fields (rows 4 of the stopping budget).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt_length, check\n\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass EnforcementTorquePolicy:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    v_aisle = AISLE.v_aisle  # category: chosen\n    t_lease = AISLE.t_lease  # category: chosen\n    t_tick = AISLE.t_tick  # category: chosen\n    t_bus = AISLE.t_bus  # category: illustrative\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    lease_row = (v_aisle * (t_lease + t_tick + t_bus)).to(millimeter)\n    tick_row = (v_aisle * (t_tick + t_bus)).to(millimeter)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(lease_row.magnitude - 80.6) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;The chunk-lease row is 80.6 mm at 1.3 m/s.&quot;)\n    check(abs(tick_row.magnitude - 2.6) &lt; 1e-6, &quot;A per-tick command stream leaves 2.6 mm.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    lease_row_str = fmt_length(lease_row, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n    tick_row_str = fmt_length(tick_row, unit=millimeter, precision=1)\n]```\n\nWhen a learned locomotion or manipulation policy emits raw joint targets or motor torques at the control rate instead of handing the permission path a multi-step trajectory, the enforcer loses the ability to verify a collision-free stopping continuation before motion begins. It must then either run an active fallback model on every tick or watch its projections induce distributional shift and boundary chattering. Admission and the cyclic check in this chapter both assume that the proposer hands the permission path a plan it can judge before motion: kinematic intent lives in the learned policy, and deterministic motor drives close the torque loops. Much of contemporary robot learning practice abandons this classical division, outputting joint targets at the control rate above stiff proportional-derivative loops [@lee2020learning] or emitting motor torques end-to-end. The third law, proposal is not permission (principle \\ref{pri-vol4-proposal-permission}), does not disappear in such direct control designs. Instead, the range of physical guarantees that the runtime permission path can uphold narrows in precise, quantifiable ways.\n\nFour guarantees survive because each acts directly on raw commands at the causal boundary, without requiring a forward plan. A per-tick limit on rate and amplitude bounds torque slew and joint velocity at the hardware register interface. The kinetic barrier of @eq-enforce-kinetic-torque operates directly in effort space, so a CBF-QP can filter the policy&#x27;s output without converting it to an intermediate kinematic setpoint. The passivity-based energy tank of @sec-enforcement-intentional-contact monitors power exchange and bounds the physical work the policy can inject into the environment during contact. Beneath all of these, low-level geofencing, safely-limited speed monitoring, and torque removal backed by mechanical spring brakes remain permanently active under the software stack. Communication timing improves along one dimension. If a qualified freshness deadline is selected at one tick and an independent watchdog enforces it, policy silence is detected within that tick plus bus transport delay, which reduces the lease-expiration travel row of @tbl-enforcement-stopping-budget from `[EnforcementTorquePolicy.lease_row_str]` to `[EnforcementTorquePolicy.tick_row_str]` at the aisle speed.\n\nWhat is lost is admission. Without an explicit multi-step trajectory, the system cannot verify the collision-free stopping suffix before the robot commits to motion. The enforcer must instead evaluate the machine&#x27;s dynamic stopping reservation on every tick against an active fallback controller that maintains its own internal braking model. On a wheeled mobile base, that fallback is straightforward to formulate. The controller executes a qualified service-braking response along the current heading, followed by wheel restraint after confirmed standstill. On an underactuated balancing machine, such as a bipedal humanoid, the fallback is itself an active balance controller; cutting motor torque produces an immediate uncontrolled fall rather than a safe state.\n\nRuntime projection also alters the closed sensorimotor loop under which the policy was trained. Every time the enforcer projects a proposed action onto the admissible set, it delivers a state to the environment that the policy never intended, precipitating distributional shift in subsequent observations. This penalty surfaces directly in task performance, as quantified by the intervention metrics of @sec-enforcement-minimal-intervention. Frequent overrides cause the machine to chatter erratically along safety boundaries or freeze conservatively short of its goal. To reconcile this tension, modern architectures adopt filter-aware policy training, incorporating the safety filter directly into the training objective so that the neural network learns to avoid the boundary rather than encountering the enforcer for the first time on physical hardware [@hsu2024safetyfilter]. The core thesis still holds at the causal boundary, where untrusted proposals never bypass runtime enforcement. What bends is how much of the safety argument can be settled mathematically before motion begins vs. how much performance the machine must sacrifice to settle the remainder while moving.\n\n## The Fallback Ladder {#sec-enforcement-fallback-ladder}\n\nEven when runtime projection settles that remainder on normal ticks, a safety filter must select its fallback before the admissible control set becomes empty. Once the chosen filter has an empty feasible intersection, it cannot maintain its modeled invariant. A qualified alternate response may mitigate damage or support its own narrower containment claim, but selecting it does not restore the lost guarantee. Physical systems also cannot rely on a single, binary kill switch. Abruptly severing DC bus power whenever a control proposal approaches a barrier boundary induces uncontrolled deceleration, mechanical shock, and incessant false-positive mission aborts. Conversely, complex software optimization is futile during a hardware fault such as an inverter shoot-through, because the fault destroys gate-driver field-effect transistors (FETs) milliseconds before an interrupt handler can even dispatch.\n\nTo resolve these competing physical realities, the permission path maintains a deterministic ladder of resident protective responses. The runtime arbiter evaluates plant kinematics, remaining sensor validity, and hardware integrity on every control cycle, selecting deterministically among minimally invasive projection, closed-loop position holding, controlled deceleration, or complete torque removal. The name records an ordering by how much control authority each response retains, from a corrected proposal down to unpowered restraint, not a sequence to descend. The arbiter never climbs down the rungs one at a time; it jumps directly to the one whose prerequisites still hold.\n\n::: {#dfn-enforcement-dynamic-fallback-ladder .callout-definition title=&quot;Dynamic fallback ladder&quot;}\n\n***Dynamic fallback ladder***} is the set of resident protective responses ($\\ControlInput \\in \\{\\SafeSetProjection(\\NominalControlCommand), \\PoweredHoldCommand, \\ControlledStopCommand, \\TorqueCutoffCommand\\}$). A deterministic arbiter selects one of them on every tick according to the check that failed (an invariant violation, a timing overrun, or a hardware fault) and the measured state of the plant. One trigger can therefore select different rungs in motion and at standstill.\n\n1.  **Significance**: Physical AI systems cannot treat all faults identically. Cutting actuator power on dynamic, underactuated, or high-momentum plants (for example, legged robots mid-stride or multirotors in flight) precipitates immediate catastrophic falls or tumbling. These platforms require state-matched active deceleration before executing terminal torque removal.\n2.  **Distinction**: Unlike software exception handlers that unwind the call stack or terminate a user-space thread, a dynamic fallback ladder operates against physical momentum. The arbiter matches each rung to the remaining valid sensor channels, surviving control authority, and available stopping clearance.\n3.  **Common pitfall**: Treating the rungs as an escalation sequence where lower rungs, such as safe torque off (STO), inherently provide faster or safer arrest. Each rung possesses distinct entry prerequisites; invoking an unpowered torque inhibit on an airborne drone or an unbraked mobile base allows the machine to coast freely without arresting motion.\n\n:::\n\n### The four rungs of the fallback ladder {#sec-enforcement-four-rungs}\n\nWalking down protective responses one step at a time like a software exception cascade fails on physical hardware: commanding a standstill position hold while the base is rolling at `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_aisle_str]` saturates the drives, while cutting drive torque before active braking completes lets the vehicle coast into the obstacle. The dynamic fallback architecture therefore organizes protection across four resident responses (`PROJECT`, `HOLD`, `STOP`, and `INHIBIT`). The deterministic arbiter samples measured plant state (velocity, position, payload loading) and fault severity on every millisecond tick to dispatch the response whose compute, sensing, and actuation prerequisites still hold:\n\n1. `PROJECT`, minimal-intervention projection in MCU firmware. The primary safety filter executes on every control cycle while the feasible control set is nonempty ($\\SafeControlSet \\neq \\emptyset$), the proposal lease is fresh, and tracking error remains within bounds ($\\|\\TrackingErrorDistance(\\TimeInstant)\\| \\le \\TrackingErrorBound$). It computes the minimal-intervention torque $\\CertifiedControlAction = \\arg\\min_{\\ControlInput} \\|\\ControlInput - \\NominalControlCommand\\|^2$ subject to active barrier half-spaces within its `[EnforcementCycleBudget.qp_us_str]` $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$ cycle allocation. It makes the smallest feasible effort correction under the stated invariance assumptions; the planner must still account for any changed task-space path.\n2. `HOLD`, powered hold at standstill in MCU firmware. When the machine is stationary, the arbiter selects an energized position hold, analogous to an IEC 60204-1 Category 2 stop: safe stop 2 (SS2) followed by safe operating stop (SOS), the drive function that monitors standstill. Its protective validity stems from that certified standstill monitor rather than the position loop gain. Because IEC 60204-1 permits only Category 0 or Category 1 for an emergency stop, HOLD can never serve as an E-stop response [@iec60204]. The drives maintain coordinate $\\PoweredHoldState$ under closed-loop position control, modulating inverter phase currents against gravity and static loads. While this rung incurs continuous Joule heating in motor windings, it eliminates friction brake wear and preserves kinematic calibration, enabling instantaneous task resumption when a valid trajectory proposal arrives. On the warehouse mobile manipulator, this rung handles lapsed leases or lost host heartbeats specifically when the platform is already at standstill.\n3. `STOP`, controlled dynamic stop in MCU firmware. When a machine in active transit loses proposal authority, tracking error diverges, or an obstacle breaches the stopping envelope, the response is a resident, state-matched controlled stop, analogous to an IEC 60204-1 Category 1 stop (safe stop 1, SS1). Its certified counterpart is the ramp-monitored variant (SS1-r), which checks deceleration against a bounded profile throughout the arrest; the unmonitored, time-controlled variant (SS1-t) merely removes torque after a fixed delay without verifying that the planned ramp actually decelerated the load [@iec61800part52]. The resident jerk-limited S-curve stop suffix of @sec-enforcement-stopping-envelopes serves this role provided the entry state falls within its validated domain. Executed autonomously by bare-metal MCU firmware directly from local SRAM, this stop peaks at the credible deceleration $\\CredibleBrakingDeceleration$, returning kinetic energy through motor inverters to the DC bus for absorption by the qualified regeneration path. Power removal and mechanical brake clamping occur only after confirmed zero velocity.\n4. `INHIBIT`, torque inhibit and spring-brake clamping by hardware interlocks. When the safety microcontroller suffers a brownout, clock loss, or watchdog timeout, all software decision logic ceases. Dedicated, out-of-band hardware interlocks trigger STO to de-energize gate drive power supplies and drop hold current to electromechanical spring-applied brakes. This terminal rung removes motor torque without software intervention; the mechanical brakes provide only their independently qualified holding or dynamic stopping capacity.\n\n: **Machine Stop Categories**: IEC 60204-1 distinguishes uncontrolled power removal (Category 0), controlled stopping followed by power removal (Category 1), and controlled stopping with power retained (Category 2). A category defines electrical power status rather than stopping distance; every rung therefore requires its own empirically validated stopping measurement.\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ THE STOP RUNG ON THE MACHINE'S BASE</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: the controlled-stop paragraph immediately below.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    show that the stop rung executes the resident C2 suffix.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    aisle speed, credible deceleration, suffix duration and distance.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopAtAisleSpeed: the S-curve stop</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          (braking plus jerk-ramp travel, and its duration) at AISLE.v_aisle</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          and AISLE.a_brake with the jerk bound AISLE.j_brake.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_velocity, fmt_acceleration, check</p>\n<p>STOP = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopAtAisleSpeed</p>\n<p>class EnforcementStopRung:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v_aisle = STOP.speed  # category: chosen a_brake = STOP.a_brake  # category: illustrative</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>duration = STOP.result.stop_time.to(millisecond) distance = (STOP.result.braking_travel + STOP.result.jerk_ramp_travel).to(millimeter)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(duration.magnitude - 931.25) &lt; 1e-6, \"The S-curve stop from 1.3 m/s takes 931.25 ms.\") check(abs(distance.magnitude - 605.3125) &lt; 1e-6, \"The S-curve stop from 1.3 m/s covers 605.31 mm.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>v_aisle_str = fmt_velocity(v_aisle, precision=1) a_brake_str = fmt_acceleration(a_brake, precision=0) duration_str = fmt_latency(duration, unit=millisecond, precision=2) distance_str = fmt_length(distance, unit=millimeter, precision=2) ]```</p>\n<p>When a machine in motion loses proposal authority or violates a tracking bound, the arbiter engages a controlled dynamic stop, operating analogously to an IEC 60204-1 Category 1 stop. On the warehouse mobile manipulator base, this rung executes the resident stop suffix pre-admitted alongside the motion trajectory, rather than attempting to synthesize a stopping trajectory on the fly during a fault. In steady-state cruising with zero acceleration, this pre-computed profile matches the operating state at fault onset directly; nonzero acceleration states require their own qualified state-matched suffixes. From an aisle cruise velocity of <code>[EnforcementStopRung.v_aisle_str]</code>, the jerk-limited S-curve suffix sustains a credible peak deceleration of <code>[EnforcementStopRung.a_brake_str]</code> between its jerk transitions. The entire maneuver spans <code>[EnforcementStopRung.duration_str]</code> and traverses <code>[EnforcementStopRung.distance_str]</code>, the stop-suffix travel the stopping budget of @sec-enforcement-stopping-envelopes reserves at this speed. Because the deceleration phase channels kinetic energy back through the inverter into the DC bus, bus capacitance and braking choppers must absorb this regenerative surge without triggering a bus overvoltage fault. Complete electrical power removal, if mandated by protocol, occurs only after sensors verify standstill.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ THE HELD-UP PERMISSION RAIL AND THE UNBUDGETED INHIBIT RUNG\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: the inhibit paragraph immediately below.\n# │ Goal:    compare logic/coil hold-up with the selected stop sequence;\n# │          inverter torque survival is a separate qualification premise.\n# │ Show:    spring-brake engage range, hold-up, flag-to-brakes-set time, margin.\n# │ How:     RM D15 rail fields; the S-curve suffix at the inspected-floor\n# │          ceiling (EnforcementStoppingBudget) with the friction-bound peak\n# │          and the load&#x27;s jerk bound.\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.physics.robotics import calc_scurve_stop_time\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_latency, check\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\n\nclass EnforcementRailHoldup:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    holdup = RM.permission_rail_holdup  # category: chosen\n    engage_min = RM.spring_brake_engage_min  # category: illustrative\n    engage_max = RM.spring_brake_engage_max  # category: illustrative\n    tick = AISLE.t_tick  # category: chosen\n    bus = AISLE.t_bus  # category: illustrative\n    onset = AISLE.t_brake_onset  # category: illustrative\n    a_inspected = EnforcementStoppingBudget.a_inspected  # category: derived (mu_inspected_floor * g)\n    v_inspected = EnforcementStoppingBudget.ceiling_inspected  # category: derived (inspected-floor ceiling)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    flag_to_onset = (tick + bus + onset).to(millisecond)\n    longest_stop = calc_scurve_stop_time(v_inspected, a_inspected, EnforcementStoppingBudget.j_brake).to(millisecond)\n    flag_to_set = (flag_to_onset + longest_stop + engage_max).to(millisecond)\n    margin = (holdup - flag_to_set).to(millisecond)\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(abs(holdup.to(second).magnitude - 2.0) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;The permission rail holds up for 2.0 s.&quot;)\n    check(abs(flag_to_onset.magnitude - 22.0) &lt; 1e-9, &quot;Stop onset follows the flag within 22 ms.&quot;)\n    check(abs(flag_to_set.magnitude - 1313.43) &lt; 5e-3, &quot;Flag to all brakes set is 1313.43 ms.&quot;)\n    check(abs(margin.magnitude - 686.57) &lt; 5e-3, &quot;The hold-up leaves 686.57 ms.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    engage_min_ms_str = fmt_latency(engage_min, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    engage_max_ms_str = fmt_latency(engage_max, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    holdup_str = fmt_latency(holdup, unit=second, precision=0)\n    flag_to_onset_str = fmt_latency(flag_to_onset, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    longest_stop_str = fmt_latency(longest_stop, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    flag_to_set_str = fmt_latency(flag_to_set, unit=millisecond, precision=2)\n    margin_str = fmt_latency(margin, unit=millisecond, precision=2)\n]```\n\nBeyond firmware-driven stops, a separate out-of-band circuit can inhibit motor torque generation entirely. Drive STO cuts gate-drive bias to isolate inverter FETs, but it does not disconnect the DC bus, energize mechanical friction brakes, or actively decelerate a moving rotor; an unbraked motor will simply coast under its existing momentum. In robust architectures, an external safety controller simultaneously drives a separately rated contactor and spring-applied mechanical brakes. Engineers must evaluate the dynamic stopping distance of this combined pathway as an integrated system, because dropping drive torque while an arm is under gravitational load or while a vehicle is at speed can exacerbate the hazard. For the warehouse mobile manipulator, this pathway, STO combined with nine spring brakes that engage between `[EnforcementRailHoldup.engage_min_ms_str]` and `[EnforcementRailHoldup.engage_max_ms_str]` after coil flux decay, constitutes the unbudgeted INHIBIT rung. It serves exclusively as a hardware backstop for MCU cycle failure, and the primary stopping envelope claims do not rely on it.\n\nAn undervoltage fault on the shared drive bus can invoke the STOP rung only while inverter gate drives, angular encoders, and control logic retain adequate voltage for closed-loop deceleration. The isolated permission rail maintains power to logic circuits, transceivers, and brake coils for `[EnforcementRailHoldup.holdup_str]`, but it does not supply the motor currents required for braking torque. Timing guarantees for powered stopping therefore assume a surviving drive bus. Under this condition, dynamic braking begins within `[EnforcementRailHoldup.flag_to_onset_str]` of the fault flag; the hold-up hypothetical uses the pre-dispatch inspected-floor ceiling of `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.ceiling_inspected_str]`, rather than an admitted operating configuration, and its stop requires `[EnforcementRailHoldup.longest_stop_str]`. Mechanical brake engagement at standstill adds at most `[EnforcementRailHoldup.engage_max_ms_str]`. This entire sequence completes within `[EnforcementRailHoldup.flag_to_set_str]`, preserving a `[EnforcementRailHoldup.margin_str]` buffer of control logic hold-up.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↩ The isolated permission rail and its capacitive hold-up are established in @sec-body-permission-rail-isolation.\n:::\n\nThis analysis confirms the control-supply duration budget, whereas the electrical drive-energy budget requires separate qualification. Systems engineers must prove through empirical testing that the DC bus capacitance, inverter power stages, and regenerative dump resistors sustain controlled deceleration under worst-case electrical fault conditions. If the drive stage cannot guarantee this power integrity, the system must either adopt an unpowered dynamic braking configuration or restrict its operational safety envelope; INHIBIT cannot be credited with standard Category 1 stopping performance. Similarly, at standstill, an undervoltage fault must trigger verified mechanical brake engagement before the logic supply decays, rather than attempting to maintain an active HOLD indefinitely on finite capacitance.\n\nPhysical AI systems engineers must distinguish between **fail-safe** and **fail-operational** plants when configuring terminal fallbacks. In a machine already at a mechanically restrained standstill, removing drive torque is safe once mechanical engagement is verified. On underactuated, open-loop unstable, or high-momentum platforms, however, abruptly removing actuator torque directly initiates a catastrophic failure mode:\n\n1. **Aerial platforms (multirotors and eVTOLs)**: Cutting motor torque drops aerodynamic thrust to zero instantly, transforming an airborne vehicle into an uncontrolled ballistic projectile.\n2. **Dynamic legged systems (bipedal humanoids)**: During dynamic locomotion, the robot&#x27;s center of mass frequently falls outside the support polygon; stability depends on active ground-reaction forces and momentum exchange. Suddenly removing joint torques causes an unrecoverable fall, with impact severity governed by gait phase and available compliance.\n3. **High-speed autonomous vehicles**: De-energizing steering actuators or electric traction motors at highway speeds strips away the control authority required to execute a coordinated deceleration, leaving the vehicle at the mercy of passive road friction.\n\nThe physical channel that dispatches a hardware torque-removal request is the out-of-band interlock that @sec-nervous-actuation-authority traced (@fig-real-estop). In it, dual linked emergency-stop contacts, two force-guided relays whose external device monitoring (EDM) contacts block a restart after a weld, and two STO inputs on the drive leave no single fault able to defeat the trip. What the INHIBIT rung needs from that circuit is its third stage, drawn again here with the brake in its own loop (@fig-12-estop-interlock), because removing drive torque ($\\CommandedJointTorque = 0$) and arresting physical momentum are distinct functions that need separate validation:\n\n1.  **Torque removal (Stage 3, drive side):** De-energizing the safety relays drops the drive&#x27;s STO inputs, which de-energize the gate drive power supply for the three-phase inverter bridge, rendering the power MOSFETs or insulated-gate bipolar transistors (IGBTs) high-impedance, so the motor can no longer produce electromagnetic torque. STO alone does not apply mechanical braking. An unbraked motor carries momentum and may coast freely.\n2.  **Mechanical restraint (Stage 3, brake side):** A separate coil circuit powers the spring-applied electromechanical friction brake. When power is removed, coil magnetic flux decays, allowing internal springs to physically clamp friction pads against the motor shaft, applying the brake without electrical power. A holding-brake rating does not establish the ability to arrest a moving load; dynamic stopping requires separate energy, torque, and response qualification.\n\n::: {#fig-12-estop-interlock fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Illustrative emergency-stop circuit**: Stages 1 and 2 are the out-of-band interlock circuit (Chapter 4); Stage 3 maps to the numbered list above. Two linked contacts each hold a force-guided relay, the relays&#x27; enable contacts hold the drive&#x27;s two Safe Torque Off (STO) inputs, and their EDM contacts in series block a restart after a weld. The spring-applied brake runs on its own supply, and a supply contactor, if fitted, provides galvanic disconnection. A moving motor can coast after STO, so the brake&#x27;s set time and the physical stopping distance must be measured for the load and state.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Two-channel schematic in three labeled stages. Stage 1, dual-channel E-stop: a red positive-break E-stop mechanically linked to normally closed contacts S1 and S2, each fed from 24 volts. Stage 2, redundant relays with EDM: S1 drives the coil of force-guided relay K1 and S2 the coil of K2; each relay has a normally open enable contact and a normally closed EDM contact, and the EDM contacts in series run with a dashed teal line to a reset monitor, restart only if K1 and K2 released. Stage 3, separate stop functions: K1 and K2 send STO1 and STO2 enable to the drive, whose gate-drive supply feeds an inverter bridge driving motor M, tagged in red tau equals 0 and noted may coast. A dashed box above the drive reads supply contactor, if fitted. A brake supply on its own circuit, switched by K1 and K2, contacts not drawn, feeds a spring brake that clamps on power loss and acts on the motor shaft, with a dashed tag reading brake set time: measure.&quot;}\n\n:::\n\nThat separation between software control and hardwired, out-of-band trip circuits is not a stylistic preference. When the processor running the control loop stalls or corrupts its own state, only a physically independent path can still remove drive enable and clamp the brakes. The precedent that the lineage table of @sec-boundary-bedrock-laws attaches to the third law shows the cost of removing that hardware path and trusting software logic to gate a high-energy actuator on its own (war story \\ref{ws-enforcement-therac-25-software-interlock-elimination}).\n\n::: {#ws-enforcement-therac-25-software-interlock-elimination .callout-war-story title=&quot;Therac-25 software interlock elimination&quot;}\n**Context**: Between 1985 and 1987, the Therac-25 medical linear accelerator administered massive radiation overdoses to six cancer patients after system designers eliminated the electromechanical interlocks used in previous models, assigning safety-critical beam containment entirely to software [@leveson1993investigating].\n\n**Mechanism**: Software race conditions and shared memory corruption in the operator interface allowed the machine to fire its high-current electron beam (25 MeV) without rotating the tungsten target and beam-flattening filter into position. Because the physical microswitches that previously interlocked beam activation had been removed, the underlying hardware possessed no independent means to detect that the operational mode conflicted with the turntable state.\n\n**Impact**: Six patients received radiation doses estimated between 4,000 and 20,000 rads (up to 100 times the therapeutic level), causing severe tissue necrosis and three direct fatalities [@leveson1993investigating].\n\n**Response**: Investigators mandated the restoration of independent hardware interlocks, requiring positive electromechanical verification of turntable configuration prior to beam generation, alongside formal software safety lifecycle standards.\n\n**Systems lesson**: A software-based protection layer cannot serve as its own independent backstop. A runtime barrier filter may exercise binding authority over nominal control proposals, but the architecture still requires an out-of-band, physically independent trip mechanism to survive processor lockups, memory corruption, and firmware bugs. Because torque removal alone does not guarantee deceleration, the surviving physical response must match the plant&#x27;s dynamic envelope.\n:::\n\nThe four rungs run on two paths (@fig-12-fallback-ladder). `PROJECT`, `HOLD`, and `STOP` run in safety MCU firmware at the `[EnforcementLeaseStop.loop_rate_str]` tick and reach the drives through one arbiter, which selects a single actuation command $\\SelectedActuationCommand$; `INHIBIT` bypasses the firmware on a hardware path that removes gate-drive supply (STO) and drops spring-brake holding current when the processor itself fails. The rungs trade response latency, control authority, and recovery cost, and the arbiter selects directly among those whose entry prerequisites still hold. A controlled stop demands verified drive electronics and sufficient stopping clearance; losing drive integrity forces unpowered mitigation with strictly weaker physical guarantees. Selection is also only as sound as the state evidence behind it. The 2023 Cruise pullover, which @sec-boundary-when-fails set beside Tempe as a failure after impact, selected a motion fallback on the strength of a missing track (war story \\ref{ws-enforcement-cruise-fallback-dragging}).\n\n::: {#fig-12-fallback-ladder fig-env=&quot;figure&quot; fig-pos=&quot;t&quot; fig-cap=&quot;**Fallback responses and the paths that run them**: The plant state and the failed check select one response directly; the four are alternatives, not steps of an escalation. PROJECT, HOLD, and STOP run in the safety MCU&#x27;s firmware and reach the drives through its arbiter, which here passes the projected command. INHIBIT is the one response that survives loss of the MCU itself, so it bypasses the arbiter on a hardware path that removes gate-drive supply and lets the spring brakes set. On a moving machine that path also removes motor braking, and the machine coasts until the separately qualified brakes take hold.&quot; fig-alt=&quot;Four rows, each with a condition card on the left joined by an arrow to a response card. The first three responses, PROJECT, HOLD, and STOP, sit inside a dashed box labeled safety MCU firmware, 1 kHz, and feed a multiplexer whose selected input, PROJECT, connects to an output arrow labeled u act entering a tall box labeled drives and brakes. The fourth response, INHIBIT, sits in a separate dashed box labeled hardware interlock and connects directly to the drives box by an arrow labeled STO, brake supply. The PROJECT condition, proposal u nom admitted, is drawn dashed in bronze.&quot;}\n\n:::\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ THE TEMPE AND CRUISE RECORDS</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: the Cruise war story below, the Tempe pitfall, and</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          @fig-12-uber-tempe-timeline.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    print the investigators' values instead of hand-typed literals.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    Tempe first detection 5.6 s and recognition 1.2 s before impact;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          Cruise pullover about 20 ft (6.1 m) at up to 7.7 mph (3.4 m/s).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     cited literature (NTSB/HAR-19/03 and the Quinn</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          Emanuel report), the same values @sec-boundary-when-fails loads.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import Literature from mlsysim.core.units import * from mlsysim.fmt import fmt_latency, fmt_length, fmt_velocity, check</p>\n<p>class EnforcementIncidentRecord:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">category: literature. NTSB/HAR-19/03 (2019), p. v and table pp. 15-16</h1>\n<p>t_detect = 5.6 <em> second  # first detection, before impact t_recognize = 1.2 </em> second  # imminent collision recognized, before impact</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">category: literature. Quinn Emanuel report to the Cruise boards (2024), p. 10</h1>\n<p>d_drag = 20.0 <em> foot  # approximate distance moved during the pullover v_drag = 7.7 </em> mph  # maximum speed during the pullover</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>d_drag_m = d_drag.to(meter) v_drag_ms = v_drag.to(meter / second)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(t_recognize &lt; t_detect, \"Recognition comes after first detection.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">fig-12-uber-tempe-timeline fig-alt hand-types 5.6 s and 1.2 s.</h1>\n<p>check(abs(t_detect.magnitude - 5.6) &lt; 1e-9 and abs(t_recognize.magnitude - 1.2) &lt; 1e-9, \"The NTSB times are 5.6 s and 1.2 s.\") check(abs(d_drag_m.magnitude - 6.096) &lt; 1e-3, \"20 ft is 6.1 m.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>t_detect_str = fmt_latency(t_detect, unit=second, precision=1) t_recognize_str = fmt_latency(t_recognize, unit=second, precision=1) d_drag_str = fmt_length(d_drag, unit=foot, precision=0) d_drag_m_str = fmt_length(d_drag_m, unit=meter, precision=1) v_drag_str = fmt_velocity(v_drag, unit=mph, precision=1) v_drag_ms_str = fmt_velocity(v_drag_ms, unit=meter / second, precision=1) ]```</p>\n<p><strong>Context</strong>: In October 2023, an autonomous Cruise robotaxi in San Francisco encountered a pedestrian who had been struck by an adjacent human-driven vehicle and propelled into the robotaxi's travel lane [@exponent2024cruise].</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism</strong>: The autonomous vehicle initiated an immediate emergency stop upon detecting the impact. However, the collision-classification subsystem erroneously localized the pedestrian to the side of the vehicle rather than under the chassis, categorizing the event as a side collision. The operational design domain for side collisions specified a secondary pullover maneuver to clear traffic. Because the perception pipeline had tracked the pedestrian only intermittently before the collision, the tracker marked the lane ahead as clear, prompting the vehicle to accelerate from standstill into the pullover [@exponent2024cruise].</p>\n<p><strong>Impact</strong>: Executing the pullover dragged the pedestrian, who remained pinned beneath the vehicle's floorpan, approximately <code>[EnforcementIncidentRecord.d_drag_str]</code> (<code>[EnforcementIncidentRecord.d_drag_m_str]</code>) at speeds reaching <code>[EnforcementIncidentRecord.v_drag_str]</code> (<code>[EnforcementIncidentRecord.v_drag_ms_str]</code>), causing severe trauma [@quinnemanuel2024cruise]. The California DMV subsequently suspended Cruise's autonomous testing and deployment permits, prompting Cruise to halt fleet operations nationwide and recall software across 950 vehicles [@cadmv2023cruise; @exponent2024cruise].</p>\n<p><strong>Response</strong>: A post-collision fallback must prohibit renewed motion whenever state uncertainty persists. In this scenario, retaining a locked standstill and mandating physical inspection prevents the system from treating missing perceptual tracks as free space. Teleoperation feeds cannot substitute for verified local sensor coverage.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems lesson</strong>: An embodied fallback cannot infer free space from the absence of sensor tracks. Every transition across fallback states requires positive evidence verifying that assumed physical invariants hold, paired with failsafe physical restraints while state ambiguity remains unresolved.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h3\">Industrial stop categories and the safe torque off trap</h3>\n<p>On a moving mobile base or a gravity-loaded arm, Safe Torque Off (STO) is not an active brake: removing drive torque immediately leaves the base coasting on wheel momentum and lets the arm drop during the milliseconds before its mechanical friction brakes clamp. Decelerating the mobile manipulator safely instead uses closed-loop motor torque during the active braking phase and engages mechanical holding brakes only once the plant reaches confirmed standstill. Industrial standards distinguish machine-level stop categories from drive-level safety functions across this sequence. IEC 60204-1 classifies whether electrical power remains connected during and after deceleration. Functional safety standards such as IEC 61800-5-2 define the drive functions, including STO, SS1, and SS2, that execute individual stages of a response, and the <code>HOLD</code> and <code>STOP</code> rungs borrowed their names from them. @Tbl-12-iec60204-stopping-categories maps these formal classifications to their concrete physical obligations.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>Response</th><th>Motor torque and power</th><th>Physical stop obligation</th><th>Appropriate use</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Admitted filtering</td><td>Drive remains active</td><td>Recheck the feasible kinetic barrier and tracking bound every tick</td><td>Nominal operation inside the validated envelope</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Controlled hold (Category 2 / SS2-like)</td><td>Power remains for braking and hold</td><td>Verify stopping distance, thermal headroom, and static load support</td><td>Hold when feedback and torque authority remain available</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Controlled stop then torque removal (Category 1 / SS1-like)</td><td>Power remains through deceleration; torque is removed afterward</td><td>Verify the full braking and transition time under load</td><td>Moving machine with enough reserved clearance</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Torque removal (Category 0 / STO-like)</td><td>Drive cannot produce commanded torque; DC bus isolation is a separate function</td><td>A moving motor may coast; a separate brake or mechanical restraint needs its own timing and rating</td><td>State for which loss of torque has been shown safe</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Stop functions and physical response</strong>: Machine stop categories, related drive functions, and the plant response each leaves to be tested. No stop category alone supplies a safety integrity rating, a brake, galvanic isolation, or a guaranteed stopping distance. {#tbl-12-iec60204-stopping-categories tbl-colwidths=\"[18,21,34,27]\"}</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h3\">Certified safety functions beneath the permission path</h3>\n<p>Citing an unrated numerical QP solver or software evidence-age check as the primary hazard control in a safety case fails certification audit immediately, because complex solver code without formal tool qualification and hardware diagnostic coverage cannot carry a safety integrity claim. In an embodied deployment, not all firmware executing the fallback ladder carries formal functional safety certification, so a certification engineer partitions software claims by the integrity of the underlying hardware substrate. On a production machine, formal guarantees rest primarily on a second, lower layer: dedicated safety functions in the motor drives, the optical safety scanner, and the arm's safety controller, each architected and validated to a Performance Level (PL) under ISO 13849-1 [@iso13849part1]. A Performance Level grades a safety function rather than a component, by the architecture category of its channels, their mean time to dangerous failure, their diagnostic coverage, and their defenses against common-cause failure (@sec-nervous-silicon-partitioning); IEC 61508 grades the same functions by Safety Integrity Level (SIL) [@iec61508]. These hardened functions substantiate the certified protective envelope. Above them, the MCU permission path operates primarily as an unrated performance layer designed to keep the learned policy inside the admissible set, preventing unnecessary trips of the hard safety floor. Only one narrow component of this MCU layer carries a safety rating, the watchdog that revokes authority when the chunk lease expires or the host heartbeat falls silent.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>#| echo: false\n#| warning: false\n#| message: false\n# ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ THE ARM&#x27;S ROBOT CLASS AND ITS SPEED-AND-SEPARATION DISTANCE\n# ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n# │ Context: the certified-functions table and the arm SSM paragraph below.\n# │ Goal:    classify the arm under ISO 10218-1:2025 and evaluate the terms of the\n# │          SSM protective separation distance that the chapter already owns.\n# │ Show:    arm mass against the Class I limit; S_h, S_r, S_s and their sum;\n# │          the human term&#x27;s share.\n# │ How:     RM.arm.mass; Scenarios.LoadedArmJointGravityStop (descending-\n# │          joint stop and its separation terms, with AISLE.tau_delay as the\n# │          reaction time if the vision path were the separation sensor and\n# │          AISLE.v_human_approach, ISO 13855 K).\n# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfrom mlsysim import *\nfrom mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_velocity, fmt_percent, check\n\nRM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator\nAISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle\nJOINT = Scenarios.LoadedArmJointGravityStop\n\nclass EnforcementCertifiedFunctions:\n    # ┌── 1. LOAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    arm_mass = RM.arm.mass  # category: datasheet\n    class1_mass = 10.0 * kilogram  # category: standard (ISO 10218-1:2025 Class I limit, per Hartmann et al. 2026)\n    class1_speed = 0.25 * meter / second  # category: standard (ISO 10218-1:2025 Class I limit)\n    v_h = AISLE.v_human_approach  # category: datasheet (ISO 13855 K)\n    t_r = AISLE.tau_delay  # category: derived (vision-path delay; not a safety-rated sensing path)\n    v_r = JOINT.tcp_speed  # category: illustrative (descending tool point)\n    s_s = JOINT.tool_stop_descending  # category: illustrative (biased tool stop)\n\n    # ┌── 2. EXECUTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    t_s = JOINT.stop_time_descending.to(millisecond)\n    s_h = JOINT.separation_person.to(millimeter)\n    s_r = JOINT.separation_reaction.to(millimeter)\n    s_partial = JOINT.separation_total.to(millimeter)\n    share_h = JOINT.separation_person_share\n\n    # ┌── 3. GUARD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    check(arm_mass &gt; class1_mass, &quot;The registered arm exceeds the Class I mass limit, so it is Class II.&quot;)\n    check(v_r &gt; class1_speed, &quot;The descending tool point also exceeds the Class I speed limit.&quot;)\n    check(abs(t_s.magnitude - 165.517) &lt; 5e-3, &quot;The biased joint stop takes about 166 ms.&quot;)\n    check(share_h &gt; 0.6, &quot;The person&#x27;s own approach dominates the separation distance.&quot;)\n\n    # ┌── 4. OUTPUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    arm_mass_str = fmt(arm_mass.to(kilogram).magnitude, precision=0)\n    class1_mass_str = fmt(class1_mass.magnitude, precision=0)\n    t_s_str = fmt_latency(round(t_s.magnitude) * millisecond, unit=millisecond, precision=0)\n    t_r_str = fmt_latency(t_r, unit=millisecond, precision=1)\n    v_h_str = fmt_velocity(v_h, precision=1)\n    v_r_str = fmt_velocity(v_r, precision=2)\n    s_h_str = fmt_length(round(s_h.magnitude) * millimeter, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    s_r_str = fmt_length(round(s_r.magnitude) * millimeter, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    s_s_str = fmt_length(round(s_s.magnitude) * millimeter, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    s_partial_str = fmt_length(round(s_partial.magnitude) * millimeter, unit=millimeter, precision=0)\n    share_h_str = fmt_percent(round(share_h, 2), precision=0, style=&quot;prose&quot;)\n]```\n\n@Tbl-12-certified-functions maps each response and speed channel onto its certified industrial counterpart. The core drive functions derive from IEC 61800-5-2 [@iec61800part52]: **safe torque off (STO)**, **safe stop 1 (SS1)**, **safe stop 2 (SS2)**, **safe operating stop (SOS)** (which monitors standstill), **safely limited speed (SLS)**, and **safe brake control (SBC)** (which drives the mechanical brake coil through a monitored output). For the mobile base, ISO 3691-4 assigns required performance level (PLr) d to braking system control and personnel detection within the travel path, while assigning PLr b to the electromechanical parking brake [@iso3691part4; @tuv2021iso3691]. For robotic manipulators, ISO 10218-1:2025 classifies systems into Class I (manipulator mass under `[EnforcementCertifiedFunctions.class1_mass_str]` kg with strictly limited force and velocity) and Class II, mandating PL d (or SIL 2) across all safety functions on Class II arms [@iso10218part1; @hartmann2026evolution]. Because the warehouse arm weighs `[EnforcementCertifiedFunctions.arm_mass_str]` kg, it is governed by Class II mandates.\n\n| Response or channel                  | Certified counterpart                                        | Governing requirement                               | Required level on this machine                      |\n|:-------------------------------------|:-------------------------------------------------------------|:----------------------------------------------------|:----------------------------------------------------|\n| `PROJECT`, feasibility, evidence-age | None; the admission checks are not claimed as safety rated   | None                                                | None claimed; recorded as a premise                 |\n| Lease expiry and heartbeat loss      | MCU watchdog that commands STO or SS1 on the drives          | ISO 13849-1; IEC 61508 for the MCU firmware         | Set by the risk assessment for the stop it triggers |\n| Base aisle-speed ceiling             | SLS on the drive wheels, switched by the scanner&#x27;s field set | IEC 61800-5-2; speed control under ISO 3691-4       | Set by ISO 3691-4 and the risk assessment           |\n| Person at the rack end               | Scanner protective field that selects `STOP`                 | ISO 3691-4 person detection; ISO 13855 field sizing | PL d                                                |\n| `STOP` on the base                   | Ramp-monitored SS1 (SS1-r), then parking brake               | IEC 61800-5-2; ISO 3691-4 braking control           | PL d for braking control; PL b for parking brake    |\n| `HOLD`                               | SS2 into SOS                                                 | IEC 61800-5-2                                       | PL d on the arm; base set by the risk assessment    |\n| `INHIBIT`                            | STO with SBC                                                 | IEC 61800-5-2; IEC 60204-1 Category 0               | PL d on the arm; base as for braking control        |\n| Arm TCP, latch, and handover limits  | Safety-rated monitored speed in the arm&#x27;s safety controller  | ISO 10218-1:2025                                    | PL d (Class II)                                     |\n| Handover to the coworker&#x27;s hand      | Speed and separation monitoring or power and force limiting  | ISO 10218-2:2025                                    | Set by the application risk assessment              |\n\n: **The permission path above its certified floor**: Each response and speed channel of this chapter beside the safety-rated function that carries the certified claim on a production machine, the requirement that governs it, and the performance level that requirement sets for this machine. Only the first row has no certified counterpart; its checks are the performance layer. The second row is the one rated function on the MCU. {#tbl-12-certified-functions tbl-colwidths=&quot;[24,28,26,22]&quot;}\n\nAs the first row of @tbl-12-certified-functions indicates, the online CBF-QP, feasibility tests, and sensor evidence-age checks operate without formal safety ratings. Safety cases therefore cannot cite these complex numerical filters as certified protective measures; instead, the enforcement record treats their correct execution as an operational premise. System architects face two choices when structuring this boundary. An engineering team can treat all MCU-level admission checks strictly as an unrated performance layer, relying entirely on the certified hardware floor to prevent harm. Alternatively, the team can certify the entire MCU firmware stack to functional safety standards (such as IEC 61508 or ISO 26262), subjecting matrix solvers and barrier algorithms to exhaustive formal methods, tool qualification, and deterministic execution bounds.\n\nThe running machine assumes the hybrid path. Its qualified watchdog, the table&#x27;s second row, independently commands STO or SS1 at the drive level when the chunk lease expires or the host heartbeat falls silent. Its bounded scope reduces the qualification burden, while isolation, failure behavior, timing, and lifecycle evidence remain necessary. Meanwhile, the complex admission checks remain unrated, leaving the certified optical scanner and drive-integrated safety functions to handle primary hazard mitigation. This split architecture demands strict physical and temporal isolation between the rated watchdog and unrated solver routines to eliminate common-cause faults.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ How the release case treats the certified watchdog as a rated safety function and the unrated admission checks as empirical premises is developed in @sec-release-standards-map.\n:::\n\nThe robotic arm introduces collaborative operational modes beyond those required by the mobile base. **Speed and separation monitoring (SSM)** continuously modulates or halts arm velocity to maintain a protective clearance envelope around human workers, while **power and force limiting (PFL)** caps transient and quasi-static contact forces during intentional physical collaboration. Standard ISO 10218-2:2025 incorporates the collaborative requirements previously defined in ISO/TS 15066 [@iso10218part2; @isots15066]. The 2025 standard formalizes SSM within section 5.14.5 and normative Annex L, and specifies PFL limits across anatomical regions in section 5.14.6 and informative Annex M [@hartmann2026evolution]. The protective separation distance is governed by:\n$$\\ProtectiveSeparationDistance = \\PersonApproachTravel + \\RobotReactionTravel + \\RobotStoppingTravel + \\IntrusionDistance + \\PersonPositionUncertainty + \\RobotPositionUncertainty,$$\nwhere $\\PersonApproachTravel = \\HumanWalkingSpeedBound (\\RobotReactionTime + \\StoppingDuration)$ represents worker displacement during robot reaction latency $\\RobotReactionTime$ and stopping duration $\\StoppingDuration$. Next, $\\RobotReactionTravel = \\RobotApproachSpeed \\RobotReactionTime$ is robot displacement prior to brake onset, and $\\RobotStoppingTravel$ is the physical braking distance. The term $\\IntrusionDistance$ is the penetration distance permitted by sensor resolution. Finally, $\\PersonPositionUncertainty$ and $\\RobotPositionUncertainty$ denote positional uncertainties for the human and robot, respectively [@marvel2017implementing].\n\nEvaluating this expression for the descending arm highlights the relative weight of human vs. robot motion. The ISO 13855 human approach speed is $\\HumanWalkingSpeedBound =$ `[EnforcementCertifiedFunctions.v_h_str]` [@iso13855]. The vision-path latency is $\\RobotReactionTime =$ `[EnforcementCertifiedFunctions.t_r_str]`, and the tool-center-point velocity is $\\RobotApproachSpeed =$ `[EnforcementCertifiedFunctions.v_r_str]`. The gravity-biased joint deceleration from @sec-enforcement-exhausted-authority completes in $\\StoppingDuration =$ `[EnforcementCertifiedFunctions.t_s_str]` across $\\RobotStoppingTravel =$ `[EnforcementCertifiedFunctions.s_s_str]`. With these values, the human displacement term is $\\PersonApproachTravel =$ `[EnforcementCertifiedFunctions.s_h_str]` and the robot reaction displacement is $\\RobotReactionTravel =$ `[EnforcementCertifiedFunctions.s_r_str]`. Together, these preliminary dynamic components total `[EnforcementCertifiedFunctions.s_partial_str]` before accounting for sensor intrusion margin $\\IntrusionDistance$ and spatial uncertainties $\\PersonPositionUncertainty$ and $\\RobotPositionUncertainty$.\n\nHuman movement accounts for `[EnforcementCertifiedFunctions.share_h_str]` of this cumulative margin. Just as in the walking-person stopping budget of @sec-enforcement-composite-budget, human velocity during the combined reaction and deceleration phases dominates the required clearance. Against an approaching human, total stopping duration imposes a far heavier spatial tax than the robot&#x27;s physical braking distance alone. Because standard vision pipelines lack functional safety ratings, a certified SSM implementation must use dedicated, safety-qualified sensors to measure separation and bound $\\RobotReactionTime$.\n\nThe standards distinguish how they treat obstacle velocity. Standard ISO 3691-4 specifies detection assuming a stationary person standing in the travel path [@tuv2021iso3691]; consequently, vehicle testing protocols evaluate protective envelopes against the static-obstacle formulation of @eq-enforce-stopping-envelope. If a worker continues walking toward the vehicle throughout the braking sequence, the relative closing velocity exceeds the standard&#x27;s baseline assumptions. In contrast, SSM explicitly models dynamic human approach velocity ($\\HumanWalkingSpeedBound$), explaining why mobile base navigation requires an explicit aisle-crossing protocol whereas robotic manipulation requires an active separation or contact-limiting channel. For collaborative handoffs, the enforcement record adopts the standardized biomechanical model of the human hand, distinguishing between unconstrained (transient) and clamped (quasi-static) contact scenarios. While simplified models often approximate PFL as a single lumped elastic collision with a fixed force threshold, ISO 10218-2 specifies distinct permissible force and pressure ceilings across individual anatomical regions. A complete architectural map correlating each subsystem, governing regulatory standard, and validation artifact is synthesized in the release decision framework (@tbl-16-standards-map).\n\n::: {#chk-enforcement-fallback-ladder-preemption .callout-checkpoint title=&quot;Choosing the physical fallback&quot;}\n\nBefore selecting a defensive fallback strategy, review the requirements governing state-dependent stopping modes, safety functions, and timing reserves:\n\n- [ ] **State-dependent stop**: Why can powered braking be safer than STO for a moving autonomous mobile robot (AMR), while drive torque inhibit may be appropriate after a separately verified restraint holds a stationary arm?\n- [ ] **Independent functions**: What does STO inhibit, and which separate functions would remove bus power or apply a spring brake?\n- [ ] **Timing and clearance**: Which measured detection, dispatch, drive, and brake delays belong in the stop reservation?\n- [ ] **Lost viability**: What can a fallback still accomplish when the admissible torque set is empty?\n\n:::\n\n## Runtime Enforcement Failure Modes {#sec-enforcement-failure-modes}\n\nEvery guarantee in this chapter has rested on premises about the computer that runs the filter and the plant it models: a held-up logic rail, a bounded cycle, a credible deceleration, and a bus that delivers the admitted command on time. On the warehouse mobile manipulator each of those premises already carries a concrete spatial price. A missed tick costs `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.missed_tick_str]` of travel at the aisle speed. A lease honored by the host instead of the MCU turns a `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.t_stall_str]` planning stall into `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.stall_added_str]` of unbraked travel and a `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.stall_breach_str]` overrun. An unprotected integrator spends `[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.windup_travel_str]` against a `[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.spare_str]` spare. Gravity on the arm and mug takes `[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.bias_pct_str]` of the joint&#x27;s peak torque and quadruples the descending stop. Runtime enforcement failures trace back to four classes, one for each premise:\n\n1. **Common-cause infrastructure failures**: When the learned policy and the safety filter share power rails, clock sources, reset lines, or thermal paths, one fault can disable both. A current surge during hard acceleration can sag a shared supply until it resets both the neural accelerator and the microcontroller that monitors it, and a shared reset or clock fault can silence the monitor at the instant the policy issues an errant command. The running machine therefore holds its permission logic on an isolated, held-up rail (@sec-enforcement-four-rungs).\n2. **Timing overruns and execution jitter**: A quadratic program that stalls on an ill-conditioned active set, or a filter scheduled on a non-deterministic operating system, spends braking clearance while it computes. An overrunning routine cannot be trusted to detect its own stall, which is why the declared `[EnforcementCycleBudget.wcet_us_str]` $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$ cycle comes with a qualified preempting timer or independent mechanism that selects the resident fallback when a deadline expires (@sec-enforcement-loop-budget).\n3. **Plant mismatch and unobservable state drift**: The filter evaluates safety against a model. A floor film that lowers friction, a payload that shifts the center of mass, or winding heat that lowers peak torque removes control authority the model still credits, so the solver can admit a deceleration the hardware cannot deliver. Where onboard sensors cannot observe the shift, the safe set must carry qualified uncertainty margins or the operating domain must restrict speed, as the complete optimistic inspected-floor screen falls to `[EnforcementStoppingBudget.ceiling_inspected_complete_str]`, before driven-wheel load share and available braking are qualified (@sec-enforcement-stopping-envelopes).\n4. **Interconnect contention and transport delays**: State reads and command writes must cross physical buses. Unprioritized telemetry or logging can queue a joint reading until it is stale, or hold a filtered command in a transmit queue while the drive keeps executing the previous high-speed setpoint. The running machine carries its admitted command in a cyclic EtherCAT frame (stage 5 of the cycle budget) and charges the board link to the dispatch-to-admission budget.\n\nNone of the four yields to a better solver or an automotive-grade microcontroller. Each is answered by evidence. Common causes and contention need isolation and bounded interference, which @sec-placement tests on silicon. Overruns need a verified execution bound with an independent fallback. Plant mismatch needs worst-case rather than nominal parameter margins. All four need a record of every enforcement decision, with its solver latency, active constraints, and sensor timestamps, so that an incident can be traced to the policy, the plant, or the infrastructure.\n\n## The Enforcement Record {#sec-enforcement-telemetry}\n\nWhen a warehouse mobile manipulator halts at a rack end because runtime safety logic rejected a motion proposal, forensic analysis requires unambiguous provenance: which threshold failed, which physical detector measured the violation, and which fallback rung took control of the actuators. The **enforcement record** is the tenth record in the record chain, the point where offline characterization meets online execution. It formalizes runtime safety across three cooperating artifacts:\n\n1. **Enforcement contract**: The signed, immutable specification compiled into microcontroller firmware that fixes qualified tracking bounds $\\TrackingErrorBound$, barrier coefficients, timer deadlines, delivered-effort error and response-delay bounds, and fallback routing tables before deployment.\n2. **Admitted command**: The record&#x27;s per-tick output. On each millisecond control cycle, the microcontroller gatekeeper generates an admitted command for transmission across the causal boundary. This payload contains the admitted or projected control $\\CertifiedControlAction$, the verified barrier margin $\\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState)$, tracking error validation $\\TrackingErrorDistance(\\TimeInstant) \\le \\TrackingErrorBound$, and the remaining hardware lease. The gatekeeper forwards only the bare setpoint and protective envelope to the motor drives, committing the complete supervisory state to the audit log.\n3. **Enforcement audit log**: The high-rate forensic stream that captures per-tick proposal evaluations, intervention vectors $\\FiniteChange \\ControlInput = \\CertifiedControlAction - \\NominalControlCommand$, quadratic programming (QP) solve latencies, and veto events in non-volatile storage.\n\nTogether, these artifacts render safety decisions fully auditable without burdening the actuator bus. The contract predefines the admissible operating envelope, the admitted command tags the immediate actuator setpoint, and the audit log preserves the causal chain of evidence. Real-time enforcement depends entirely on the protected command path, deterministic loop timing, and verified physical braking capabilities.\n\n### The admitted command schema {#sec-enforcement-admitted-command-schema}\n\nIf the gatekeeper emits a bare torque vector without a monotonic sequence counter, a digest of the admitted parent trajectory, or the remaining lease duration, a replayed frame or a stalled microcontroller cannot be distinguished from a live command, and a post-incident audit cannot reconstruct which safety margins were checked on that millisecond tick. At each control cycle, the gatekeeper therefore produces an admitted command that establishes runtime authority at the drive interface (@tbl-12-admitted-command-schema). Its fields explicitly bind the actuator setpoint to its parent trajectory, verified safety margins, and active fallback state. Because each entry records a decision made under assumed physical conditions, runtime containment still relies on those assumptions matching physical reality.\n\n| Field Name                 | Type &amp; Units            | Physical System Invariant &amp; Validation Rule                                                                                                                       | Mobile Manipulator Parameter / Value                                                                                                                           |\n|:---------------------------|:------------------------|:------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|:---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `token_sequence_id`        | `uint64_t` (counter)    | Monotonically increasing admitted-command ID on MCU clock                                                                                                         | Increments every `[EnforcementLeaseStop.tick_ms_str]` tick                                                                                              |\n| `timestamp_mcu_ns`         | `uint64_t` (ns)         | Absolute MCU monotonic timestamp of admission                                                                                                                     | Latch instant at causal boundary                                                                                                                               |\n| `parent_trajectory_digest` | `uint8_t[32]` (SHA-256) | Cryptographic link to the admitted trajectory record and chunk lease                                                                                              | SHA-256 of active trajectory record                                                                                                                            |\n| `admitted_control_vector`  | `float32[N_act]` (N·m)  | Filtered torque $\\CertifiedControlAction$ realized only under the qualified effort-error and response-delay contract; nominal if safe, projected if near boundary | Per-axis qualified torque envelope; `[EnforcementAuthorityLoss.torque_limit_str]` only for the characterized arm axis                                   |\n| `intervention_vector`      | `float32[N_act]` (N·m)  | Intervention delta $\\FiniteChange \\ControlInput = \\CertifiedControlAction - \\NominalControlCommand$; zero when the admitted command equals the nominal command    | Nonzero only during CBF-QP projection                                                                                                                          |\n| `barrier_margin`           | `float32` (normalized)  | Dimensionless $\\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState)/\\BarrierNormalizationScale$ with positive scale and barrier identity bound in the contract                 | Must satisfy $\\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState) \\ge 0$                                                                                                   |\n| `tracking_error`           | `float32` (m)           | Instantaneous tracking error norm $\\TrackingErrorDistance(\\TimeInstant) = \\lVert \\MeasuredTrackedPosition - \\ReferencePosition \\rVert$                            | Base position error: $\\TrackingErrorDistance(\\TimeInstant) \\le$ `[EnforcementTrackingBound.eps_track_str]`; arm channels use their own qualified bounds |\n| `active_fallback_rung`     | `uint8_t` (enum)        | Active execution state: `PROJECT`, `HOLD`, `STOP`, `INHIBIT`                                                                                                      | `0x01` (`PROJECT`) during nominal tracking                                                                                                                     |\n| `lease_remaining_ns`       | `uint32_t` (ns)         | Remaining validity duration of current chunk lease                                                                                                                | Trips `STOP` / `HOLD` upon reaching zero                                                                                                                       |\n| `crc32_token`              | `uint32_t` (checksum)   | CRC-32 over the log entry, for audit integrity                                                                                                                    | Log reader rejects a corrupt entry; the drive-facing setpoint carries its own safety-layer CRC                                                                 |\n\n: **Admitted command schema**: The per-tick output of the enforcement record, the tenth record in the record chain. Formed on every `[EnforcementLeaseStop.tick_ms_str]` tick by the microcontroller and written to the audit log, identifying the selected command, checked assumptions, and state-matched response. The drives receive only the setpoint, torque limit, and active rung inside a black-channel safety message; drive hardware containment relies on the local safety watchdog rather than the record itself. {#tbl-12-admitted-command-schema tbl-colwidths=&quot;[27,17,31,25]&quot;}\n\nConstructing the enforcement record synthesizes four upstream records across the offline and runtime evidence strands, where the offline strand establishes physical plant capabilities and empirical policy competence while the runtime strand tracks live execution state. From the runtime strand, the trajectory record of @sec-planning-trajectory-contract supplies the admitted reference and its precomputed, dynamically feasible stop suffix. From the offline strand, the evaluation record of @sec-evaluation-logs provides runtime monitor specifications and the empirical coverage-gap catalog that define the active supervisory channels. The policy manifest of @sec-training-policy-manifest defines the declared ODD so that the enforcer&#x27;s validity region is restricted to well-sampled operating regimes. The limit records of @sec-body-measuring-limits establish empirical braking rates, actuator onset delays, and torque envelopes alongside their operating preconditions. To these upstream parameters, the enforcement record contributes the formal guarantees of the gatekeeping pipeline. These are the qualified tracking bound $\\TrackingErrorBound$ and the stopping metrics ($\\StoppingDuration, \\StoppingDistance$, which bound the duration and displacement required to arrest plant momentum). They also include the control barrier function coefficients, the discrete execution period, the per-check thresholds mapped to fallback-ladder rungs, and the dynamic validity boundary.\n\nThe `parent_trajectory_digest` binds each admitted command to its originating trajectory and active chunk lease, providing forensic traceability back to the planner&#x27;s proposal. Digests bind content for integrity checks; sequence counters and monotonic freshness checks reject stale or replayed commands within the protected drive interface. Sender authenticity additionally requires an authenticated channel or message authentication. Similarly, the `lease_remaining_ns` field conveys remaining execution authority, but this parameter guarantees safety only when the drive firmware actively enforces lease expiration by triggering a deterministic physical fallback. Streaming these fields to the audit log, alongside nominal proposals, intervention deltas, barrier margins, tracking errors, and solver latencies, gives engineers the causal evidence needed to reconstruct anomalous behaviors within the temporal resolution of the sensor pipeline.\n\nThis division of responsibilities dictates an architectural split at the fieldbus boundary. Motor drives have no operational need for trajectory digests or barrier margins; they require immediate setpoints, current limits, and active fallback commands delivered with fail-safe transport guarantees. Industrial systems handle this through established safety bus architectures. For example, Safety over EtherCAT (FSoE) implements the **black-channel principle**, treating the underlying communication bus as inherently untrusted [@iec61784-3-2021]. FSoE encapsulates drive commands within a safety container featuring dedicated cyclic redundancy checks (CRCs), unique connection identifiers, strict sequence counters, and independent watchdog timers. These measures detect communication faults; sender authentication against malicious traffic requires a separate security mechanism. When a communication watchdog expires, the drive autonomously engages its safe stop state without waiting for microcontroller intervention. The rich diagnostic context (digests, barrier margins, intervention vectors, and solver runtimes) streams to the audit log instead. An ad hoc safety-communication scheme for drive control must independently justify the fault detection and response properties covered by established functional-safety standards.\n\n::: {.column-margin .margin-pointer}\n↪ The authority record&#x27;s per-tick entries, which log the ladder rung of any active fallback, are developed in @sec-intervention-evidence-tuples.\n:::\n</code></pre>\n<p>#| echo: false #| warning: false #| message: false</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ THE ENFORCEMENT RECORD ON THE MACHINE</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Context: the record list, the routing paragraph, and the approach-ceiling</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          paragraph of @sec-enforcement-telemetry.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Goal:    fill the record's fields with the base's and the arm's own values.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ Show:    tracking bound; resident stop at the aisle speed and drive limit;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          both base configurations; the latch approach and its projection;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          the free-space TCP limit and the handover ceiling under accept_item.</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│ How:     calc_scurve_stop_time / _distance on RM/AISLE with the jerk bound</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          and inspected-floor peak from EnforcementStoppingBudget;</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          calc_contact_yield_deadline for the door; the handover ceilings</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">│          from EnforcementContactForce (@sec-enforcement-intentional-contact).</h1>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>from mlsysim import * from mlsysim.physics.robotics import calc_scurve_stop_distance, calc_scurve_stop_time, calc_contact_yield_deadline from mlsysim.fmt import fmt, fmt_length, fmt_latency, fmt_velocity, fmt_acceleration, check</p>\n<p>RM = Embodied.MobileManipulator.WarehouseMobileManipulator AISLE = Embodied.Scenario.WarehouseAisle</p>\n<p>class EnforcementRecordMachine:</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 1. LOAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>eps_track = AISLE.eps_track  # category: illustrative v_aisle = AISLE.v_aisle  # category: chosen v_drive = RM.base.max_velocity  # category: illustrative (drive limit) a_brake = AISLE.a_brake  # category: illustrative mu_min = EnforcementStoppingBudget.mu_min  # category: derived (a_brake / g) mu_inspected = AISLE.mu_inspected_floor  # category: illustrative a_inspected = EnforcementStoppingBudget.a_inspected  # category: derived (mu_inspected_floor <em> g) v_inspected = EnforcementStoppingBudget.ceiling_inspected  # category: derived v_restricted = 1.0 </em> meter / second  # category: chosen (restricted-mode aisle speed, below the inspected-floor ceiling) v_latch_high = AISLE.v_latch_high  # category: illustrative (policy's proposed approach) v_latch = AISLE.v_latch_approach  # category: chosen (target-ODD approach) k_latch = AISLE.k_latch  # category: illustrative f_latch_limit = AISLE.f_latch_limit  # category: illustrative t_contact = AISLE.t_contact_response  # category: illustrative (contact loop) tcp_limit = RM.tcp_speed_limit  # category: chosen (free-space TCP limit) v_handover = AISLE.v_handover  # category: chosen (handover approach under accept_item)</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 2. EXECUTE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>j_brake = EnforcementStoppingBudget.j_brake  # category: derived (reference polynomial's peak jerk) _aisle = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopAtAisleSpeed.result _drive = Scenarios.WarehouseFinishedStopAtDriveLimit.result t_stop_aisle = _aisle.stop_time.to(millisecond) d_stop_aisle = (_aisle.braking_travel + _aisle.jerk_ramp_travel).to(millimeter) t_stop_drive = _drive.stop_time.to(millisecond) d_stop_drive = (_drive.braking_travel + _drive.jerk_ramp_travel).to(millimeter) t_stop_restricted = calc_scurve_stop_time(v_restricted, a_inspected, j_brake).to(millisecond) d_stop_restricted = calc_scurve_stop_distance(v_restricted, a_inspected, j_brake).to(millimeter) t_yield_high = calc_contact_yield_deadline(f_latch_limit, k_latch, v_latch_high).to(millisecond) v_handover_ceiling = EnforcementContactForce.v_ceiling  # derived in calc-enforcement-contact-force v_handover_ceiling_clamped = EnforcementContactForce.v_ceiling_clamped  # derived in calc-enforcement-contact-force</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 3. GUARD ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>check(abs(eps_track.to(millimeter).magnitude - 40.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"The base's tracking bound is 40 mm.\") check(abs(t_stop_aisle.magnitude - 931.25) &lt; 1e-6, \"The S-curve stop from 1.3 m/s takes 931.25 ms.\") check(abs(d_stop_aisle.magnitude - 605.3125) &lt; 1e-6, \"The S-curve stop from 1.3 m/s covers 605.31 mm.\") check(abs(t_stop_drive.magnitude - 1031.25) &lt; 1e-6, \"The S-curve stop from 1.5 m/s takes 1031.25 ms.\") check(abs(d_stop_drive.magnitude - 773.4375) &lt; 1e-6, \"The S-curve stop from 1.5 m/s covers 773.44 mm.\") check(abs(a_inspected.to(meter / second**2).magnitude - 1.1768) &lt; 5e-4, \"The inspected-floor peak is 1.177 m/s^2.\") check(v_restricted &lt; v_inspected, \"The restricted speed must sit under the inspected-floor ceiling.\") check(abs(t_stop_restricted.magnitude - 1015.25) &lt; 5e-2, \"The restricted stop from 1.0 m/s takes 1015.3 ms.\") check(abs(d_stop_restricted.magnitude - 507.63) &lt; 5e-2, \"The restricted stop from 1.0 m/s covers 507.6 mm.\") check(abs(t_yield_high.magnitude - 2.5) &lt; 1e-9, \"At 0.10 m/s the contact deadline is 2.5 ms.\") check(t_yield_high &gt; t_contact, \"The faster approach passes only the onset-delay comparison, not the full contact admission test.\") check(v_latch &lt; v_latch_high, \"The target-ODD approach is the slower one.\") check(abs(tcp_limit.to(meter / second).magnitude - 1.0) &lt; 1e-9, \"The free-space TCP limit is 1.0 m/s.\") check(abs(v_handover.to(meter / second).magnitude - 0.10) &lt; 1e-9, \"The handover approach ceiling is 0.10 m/s.\") check(v_handover &lt; v_handover_ceiling, \"The handover cap sits under the free-hand contact-force ceiling.\") check(abs(v_handover_ceiling.magnitude - 0.2415) &lt; 5e-5, \"The free-hand contact-force ceiling is 0.24 m/s.\") check(abs(v_handover_ceiling_clamped.magnitude - 0.0527) &lt; 5e-5, \"The clamped-hand contact-force ceiling is 0.053 m/s.\") check(v_handover &gt; v_handover_ceiling_clamped, \"The handover cap exceeds the clamped ceiling, so clamping must be excluded.\")</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h1\">┌── 4. OUTPUT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</h1>\n<p>eps_track_str = fmt_length(eps_track, unit=millimeter, precision=0) v_aisle_str = fmt_velocity(v_aisle, precision=1) v_drive_str = fmt_velocity(v_drive, precision=1) t_stop_aisle_str = fmt_latency(t_stop_aisle, unit=millisecond, precision=2) d_stop_aisle_str = fmt_length(d_stop_aisle, unit=millimeter, precision=2) t_stop_drive_str = fmt_latency(t_stop_drive, unit=millisecond, precision=2, commas=True) d_stop_drive_str = fmt_length(d_stop_drive, unit=millimeter, precision=2) mu_min_str = fmt(mu_min, precision=3) mu_inspected_str = fmt(mu_inspected, precision=2) a_inspected_str = fmt_acceleration(a_inspected, precision=3) v_inspected_str = fmt_velocity(v_inspected, precision=2) v_restricted_str = fmt_velocity(v_restricted, precision=0) t_stop_restricted_str = fmt_latency(t_stop_restricted, unit=millisecond, precision=1) d_stop_restricted_str = fmt_length(d_stop_restricted, unit=millimeter, precision=1) v_latch_high_str = fmt_velocity(v_latch_high, precision=2) v_latch_str = fmt_velocity(v_latch, precision=2) t_yield_high_str = fmt_latency(t_yield_high, unit=millisecond, precision=1) t_contact_str = fmt_latency(t_contact, unit=millisecond, precision=0) tcp_limit_str = fmt_velocity(tcp_limit, precision=0) v_handover_str = fmt_velocity(v_handover, precision=2) v_handover_ceiling_str = fmt_velocity(v_handover_ceiling, precision=2) v_handover_ceiling_clamped_str = fmt_velocity(v_handover_ceiling_clamped, precision=3) ]```</p>\n<p>Filled in for the warehouse mobile manipulator, the enforcement record fixes the base's stopping parameters at the rack end alongside the arm's validity region and monitoring channels at the doorway and packing station. The tracking bound $\\TrackingErrorBound$ is <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.eps_track_str]</code> (matching row 7 of @tbl-enforcement-stopping-budget), and the resident stopping trajectory is the jerk-limited S-curve suffix, which takes <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.t_stop_aisle_str]</code> and covers <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.d_stop_aisle_str]</code> from the <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.v_aisle_str]</code> aisle speed, or <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.t_stop_drive_str]</code> and <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.d_stop_drive_str]</code> from the <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.v_drive_str]</code> drive limit. These spatial bounds are evaluated on a <code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.tick_str]</code> loop period with a declared WCET of <code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.wcet_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$ inside a <code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.deadline_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$ deadline, supervised by three parallel timers: the <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.lease_ms_str]</code> chunk lease, the <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.heartbeat_rate_hz_str]</code> $\\text{Hz}$ host heartbeat with its <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.heartbeat_timeout_ms_str]</code> silence timeout, and the <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.self_watchdog_ms_str]</code> hardware watchdog.</p>\n<p>For the manipulator, the validity region governs both the doorway and the packing station. The baseline door policy matches the nominal target ODD of the evaluation record (the nominal plate, standard door resistance, a stationary base, and the guarded approach, whereas the recovery update analyzed in @sec-evaluation-recovery-update qualifies the displaced-plate cell under its own distinct record rather than expanding the baseline region). The packing station admits picking a mug from the conveyor under a live intent lease followed by coworker handover. Across these zones, manipulator monitoring channels enforce dynamic velocity ceilings at the TCP. Inside the door zone, the approach-speed channel from the evaluation record's <code>runtime_monitor_specs</code> caps tool velocity at <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.v_latch_str]</code>, whereas in unconstrained space the conveyor mug pick operates under the nominal <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.tcp_limit_str]</code> free-space TCP limit. While a coworker holds <code>accept_item</code> (@sec-intervention-human-authority), a handover channel engages before the separating distance closes to the arm's biased stop plus its blind-travel displacement and caps the approach toward the coworker's hand at <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.v_handover_str]</code>, well below the <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.v_handover_ceiling_str]</code> contact-force ceiling for a free hand capable of recoil. Because a hand trapped against a rack upright or structural link permits an impact speed of at most <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.v_handover_ceiling_clamped_str]</code>, this handover cap is valid only when physical workstation geometry provably precludes clamping (@sec-enforcement-intentional-contact), a condition that the release argument must substantiate.</p>\n<p>For the mobile base, operating assumptions distinguish between nominal and low-friction surfaces, each carrying its own cryptographic digest in the release manifest. The standard configuration requires a qualified directional braking lower bound encompassing driven-wheel load share, traction, torque, and stability, where a friction coefficient of at least <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.mu_min_str]</code> serves only as a necessary optimistic all-weight screen rather than sufficient evidence that the design deceleration holds. By contrast, a restricted configuration accommodates degraded traction down to an inspected-floor friction of <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.mu_inspected_str]</code>. The restricted illustration stipulates that its <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.a_inspected_str]</code> braking rate is available (since inspection alone does not establish that lower bound), and its deceleration suffix uses that stipulation to stop in <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.t_stop_restricted_str]</code> across <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.d_stop_restricted_str]</code> from <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.v_restricted_str]</code> beneath the <code>[EnforcementStoppingBudget.ceiling_inspected_complete_str]</code> complete inspected-floor screen, whose braking premise still requires qualification.</p>\n<p>Each base entry incorporates the <code>[EnforcementStoppingBudget.delta_da_str]</code> dispatch-to-admission allocation and its own scenario-specific remaining clearance. The <code>[EnforcementStoppingBudget.reserve_d_str]</code> stopping reserve belongs to the dry static-obstacle aisle candidate; the restricted configuration must recompute its reserve from its own speed and braking rate. Because this allocation represents an analytical budget, neither configuration is certified for admission until physical deceleration measurements under maximum payload confirm the modeled margin.</p>\n<p>Each numerical entry in the record carries the evidence category of its limit record: <em>measured</em>, <em>derived</em>, <em>datasheet</em>, or <em>illustrative</em>. These categories reflect fundamentally different degrees of operational confidence. The base's <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.t_stop_aisle_str]</code> stopping time, for instance, is a <em>derived</em> value. It follows mathematically from the geometry of the S-curve suffix under a stipulated jerk bound and an illustrative deceleration figure, assuming that motor drives faithfully deliver commanded current and tires maintain continuous traction. Only loaded stopping trials on the actual operating surface establish a <em>measured</em> value. Physical tests reveal empirical non-idealities that escape closed-form derivations, including electromechanical brake onset scatter, structural compliance, and surface contamination. Even then, empirical data supports a formal bound only when combined with worst-case fault models and defined operating limits. Explicitly recording the evidence category prevents an integrator from mistaking a derived stopping estimate for a verified hardware limit.</p>\n<p>The record also carries the routing of @sec-enforcement-fallback-ladder as part of its contract. An arriving proposal can neither renew an active command nor revoke its authority merely by being transmitted, and each lease or heartbeat expiry, cycle loss, or bus undervoltage selects the rung that matches the machine's state and the hardware that survives. Every field of the record is either an integrity token or an operational threshold evaluated against live execution state. These checks examine the proposal's sequence counter and checksum, its stored dispatch age against <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.age_threshold_str]</code>, reconstructed perception latency against validity and stopping-margin criteria, active elapsed time against the chunk lease, tracking error against the allowable envelope $\\TrackingErrorBound$, joint-velocity and torque-slew ceilings, barrier margins, phase-current and bus-voltage limits, and hardware watchdog timeouts. The contract binds each threshold to the independent sensing hardware that measures it and to the specific fallback rung selected upon violation. @Tbl-12-fallback-hierarchy organizes these rungs by residual system authority and specifies the hardware timer that triggers each transition.</p>\n<p>↪ <strong>Byte layout:</strong> The enforcement record's fields, types, and units are given in @sec-appendix-systems-wire-enforcement.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Trigger and authority</th><th>Timing check</th><th>Physical response to validate</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><code>PROJECT</code></td><td>Fresh proposal, healthy heartbeat, feasible kinetic barrier; an invalid replacement is refused; active setpoints continue only while their own evidence, lease, state, and stop reserve remain valid</td><td><code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.tick_ms_str]</code> cycle; declared full-cycle WCET <code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.wcet_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$ inside a <code>[EnforcementCycleBudget.deadline_us_str]</code> $\\MicroUnitPrefix\\text{s}$ deadline</td><td>Track admitted torque within qualified error and brake reserve</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>HOLD</code></td><td>At standstill, active elapsed time beyond the <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.lease_ms_str]</code> lease, host heartbeat silent beyond <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.heartbeat_timeout_ms_str]</code>, while powered support remains available</td><td>Expiry detected on the MCU clock within one tick</td><td>Powered support against gravity and payload; on undervoltage, secure qualified restraint before support expires</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>STOP</code></td><td>In motion, lease or heartbeat expiry, shared-rail undervoltage, tracking error beyond $\\TrackingErrorBound$, an empty feasible control set, or a tripped disturbance observer or solver deadline, while powered braking remains available</td><td>Include detection, dispatch, drive rise, and brake response</td><td>Resident stop on the base, state-matched stop on the arm; measured stop distance under load; no claim after viability is lost</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>INHIBIT</code></td><td>Independent wired supervisor or external trip detects MCU cycle loss</td><td>Assumed <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.self_watchdog_ms_str]</code> detection plus measured drive, contactor, or brake response</td><td>STO removes motor torque and the spring brakes set after coil decay; unbudgeted, so no stopping claim rests on it</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Enforcement state contract</strong>: The <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.chunk_rate_hz_str]</code> $\\text{Hz}$ proposal, <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.lease_ms_str]</code> chunk lease, <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.heartbeat_rate_hz_str]</code> $\\text{Hz}$ host heartbeat, and <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.loop_rate_str]</code> MCU loop have distinct renewal and failure paths. {#tbl-12-fallback-hierarchy tbl-colwidths=\"[18,29,22,31]\"}</p>\n<p>Because runtime safety guarantees require that plant dynamics remain inside the modeled operational envelope, the enforcement record bounds execution within an explicit validity region. The manipulator's doorway speed ceiling illustrates how empirical evidence defines these bounds. The policy's proposed <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.v_latch_high_str]</code> approach, refused in @sec-enforcement-authority-refuse on evidential and physical grounds, passes only the narrowest timing test, since its <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.t_yield_high_str]</code> unbraked compression time from first contact to the latch force limit exceeds the <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.t_contact_str]</code> contact-loop cycle. That naive unbraked deadline ignores both the force already accumulated before the tripwire fires and the compression distance the arm still needs to decelerate to rest, so the record keeps both grounds for refusal beside that pass, the post-trip stop that @sec-enforcement-intentional-contact sized and the absence of trials at that speed, ensuring that no reader mistakes the timing pass for admission. The enforcer therefore clamps the command to the <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.v_latch_str]</code> evidence-based ceiling, evaluates full contact dynamics, and records the active constraint in the audit log. In contrast, the coworker handover ceiling is derived from the structural impact model (@eq-release-unbraked-force), which caps approach velocity below the contact-force limit where modeled impact forces meet allowable biomechanical thresholds for an unconstrained hand. Faster proposals are projected onto this boundary and logged as intervention vectors.</p>\n<p>When onboard estimators detect a state outside the validity envelope, such as an undeclared payload mass, the supervisory monitor flags the violation, revokes policy authority, and commands a state-matched stop. Environmental conditions that lack direct onboard sensing, however, cannot trigger dynamic runtime vetoes. Surface friction at the wheel-floor interface represents an unobservable state, because mobile bases carry no transducers to measure instantaneous traction coefficients beneath their wheels. The standard configuration therefore relies on verified floor friction as an operating precondition, while low-friction operations require manual floor inspection and commissioning of the restricted configuration.</p>\n<p>↪ The floor-friction premise is enforced as an entry of the residual-claims register in @sec-frontier-residual-claims-register.</p>\n<p>An enforcement record connects proposed neural trajectories to validated physical invariants, operational thresholds, and deterministic fallback responses. Algorithmic correctness, however, cannot compensate for an execution platform that violates its timing or braking commitments under load. An enforcer whose control barrier functions are mathematically verified will still fail if the couplings it still shares with the host, the board-link DMA into its memory, a supply rail, or a clock, delay its cycle under load during high-speed transit. In physical artificial intelligence, safety failures manifest in the time domain just as decisively as in algorithmic logic.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h2\">Fallacies and Pitfalls</h2>\n<p>A physical safety filter prevents collisions only as long as its control loop meets hard real-time execution deadlines and its state feedback accurately reflects reality. Safety qualification cannot rely solely on evaluating nominal control barrier equations under idealized laboratory conditions; real systems fail when host processors lock up, state estimators drift, or perception pipelines misclassify obstacles. Production testing must therefore exercise system response precisely when these core execution and sensing assumptions break down.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: <em>Assuming high-level policy computers can be relied upon to execute graceful shutdown.</em></p>\n<p><strong>A software exception handler on an application processor cannot arrest physical momentum when the operating system kernel panics.</strong> Consider a warehouse mobile manipulator whose high-level application processor experiences an operating system kernel panic while navigating an aisle at <code>[EnforcementStoppingBudget.v_aisle_str]</code>. Once the host kernel crashes, it can no longer generate deceleration trajectories or dispatch motor setpoints. Cutting the drive enable line to zero torque is equally hazardous, leaving the unbraked base to coast under momentum directly into the aisle racks. Relying on an operating system shutdown handler or a user-space exception hook assumes that the host software stack remains functional, an assumption that fails during kernel panics, hardware lockups, or memory exhaustion. Physical fail-safety requires stopping clearance and torque authority that persist independently of the host. In a robust architecture, the downstream MCU detects the missing heartbeat within its <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.heartbeat_timeout_ms_str]</code> timeout, comfortably within the stopping budget's reserved lease margin. The MCU then immediately executes its resident jerk-limited stop suffix, decelerating the base to a complete stop in <code>[EnforcementStopRung.duration_str]</code> across <code>[EnforcementStopRung.distance_str]</code>. While host shutdown routines manage orderly transitions during routine operation, true fault tolerance demands that stopping authority resides in hardware and low-level firmware that survive host death.</p>\n<p><strong>Architect's countermeasure</strong>: Decouple physical stopping authority from host processor lifecycles with two MCU timers that the host cannot service on the MCU's behalf. The heartbeat timer, refreshed by the host's periodic heartbeat, catches a host that has gone silent, which is what a kernel panic produces. The chunk lease, refreshed only by a valid admitted proposal, catches a host that is still alive and still sending heartbeats but no longer delivering valid motion. When either expires, the MCU intervenes without waiting for host acknowledgment, selecting the precompiled, state-matched stop suffix within its validated entry envelope and actively commanding motor torques to bring the robot to a controlled halt.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>A mathematically correct safety filter guarantees safety even when underlying state feedback has drifted.</em></p>\n<p><strong>A barrier certificate evaluated against a drifted or corrupted state estimate provides zero physical protection.</strong> The mobile base's <code>[EnforcementRecordMachine.eps_track_str]</code> tracking certificate bounds true physical position error only under qualified state feedback assumptions. In practice, comparing the reference trajectory against encoder-reported position serves merely as a runtime proxy; that agreement alone cannot verify the actual physical bound. Wheel slip or an optical encoder with an undetected scale bias can lead the state estimator to report that the base is farther from the aisle rack than its true physical location. The CBF-QP solver then easily satisfies its barrier inequality on the corrupted estimate, while the physical base exhausts clearance margins that the stopping budget never granted. Only an independent sensing channel, such as an IMU or a safety lidar measuring direct range to the rack, can expose this divergence, and only if the tracking error exceeds the diagnostic monitor's observability threshold. System qualification must bound how far the estimate can drift before detection occurs. The stopping budget must account for this worst-case drift in its localization allowance $\\LocalizationBound$, rather than crediting that same uncertainty to the dynamic tracking inset. To preserve formal safety guarantees, the qualification framework must explicitly document which physical error source each budget allowance covers.</p>\n<p><strong>Architect's countermeasure</strong>: Implement redundant, heterogeneous sensor cross-checks that continuously validate kinematic wheel odometry against independent physical channels, such as optical flow, time-of-flight lidar, or secondary motor encoders. Expand the safety filter's localization allowance $\\LocalizationBound$ to absorb the maximum unobservable drift that can accumulate before diagnostic cross-checks trip, guaranteeing that the contracted safe set remains physically invariant even under worst-case sensor degradation.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: <em>Coupling safety braking authority to stochastic semantic classification rather than deterministic spatial clearance.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Emergency stopping authority must be bound to deterministic spatial clearance rather than upstream semantic classification.</strong> When an automated vehicle conditions collision avoidance on object classification and trajectory forecasting, perception errors can silently disable emergency intervention. The March 2018 Tempe collision, analyzed in @sec-boundary-when-fails, shows the sequence (@fig-12-uber-tempe-timeline). Although the developmental automated driving system (ADS) first detected the pedestrian pushing a bicycle <code>[EnforcementIncidentRecord.t_detect_str]</code> before collision, repeated perception toggling between vehicle, other, and bicyclist classifications reset the tracking filter, discarding historical motion at each switch and treating the obstacle as an unpredicted static entity [@ntsb2019uber, sec. 1.5.5.2]. When the pipeline finally registered an imminent crash <code>[EnforcementIncidentRecord.t_recognize_str]</code> before impact, high-level policy suppressed actuator intervention for a full 1-second delay to avoid vehicle swerving, after which the planner issued only a gradual deceleration request. The architectural failure sits at the base of the timeline. The vehicle's factory-installed Volvo autonomous emergency braking (AEB) interlock had been suppressed during ADS operation. An independent geometric safety barrier avoids these cascading failures by separating spatial clearance from semantic inference. It commands an emergency stop based solely on physical distance and closing speed, regardless of what label the neural perception pipeline assigns to the obstacle.</p>\n<p><strong>Architect's countermeasure</strong>: Enforce the causal separation of perception and safety by establishing an independent, non-semantic geometric barrier. A safety-rated sensor channel, such as a time-of-flight lidar curtain, radar, or safety scanner, must maintain direct hardware veto authority to command a Category 1 stop. This safety layer evaluates occupancy and closing rate directly, completely bypassing the neural perception pipeline, semantic classifiers, and trajectory forecasting models.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>Assuming a continuous-time barrier certificate holds under discrete Zero-Order Hold sampling without an inter-sample kinematic buffer.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Checking a continuous-time barrier inequality at discrete sampling instants does not guarantee inter-sample safety.</strong> In digital control architectures, an actuator applies a constant torque or current command over the entire zero-order hold (ZOH) interval $\\PermissionLoopPeriod =$ <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.tick_ms_str]</code>. If an active-set QP solver computes a minimally invasive control input at sample $\\TimeInstant_k$ that places the plant directly on the boundary of the safe set ($\\SafetyBoundaryManifold$), continuous physical dynamics unfold during the intervening hold period. Between $\\TimeInstant_k$ and $\\TimeInstant_{k+1}$, physical acceleration under the held actuator command can drive the true state across the boundary before the next control cycle executes. Equating a continuous barrier certificate $\\BarrierFunctionRate + \\LinearBarrierDecayRate \\BarrierFunction \\ge 0$ directly with discrete-time invariance without an inter-sample buffer invites boundary violations whose severity grows with velocity and sample period.</p>\n<p><strong>Architect's countermeasure</strong>: Contract discrete-time barrier certificates by the worst-case inter-sample kinematic drift buffer, $\\FiniteChange \\ZohHoldTravel = \\BodySpeed \\PermissionLoopPeriod + \\frac{1}{2} \\ProfileAccelerationBound \\PermissionLoopPeriod^2$ evaluated at the highest reachable speed $\\BodySpeed$. In a <code>[EnforcementLeaseStop.loop_rate_str]</code> control loop operating at the <code>[EnforcementZOHBuffer.v_str]</code> drive limit with an independently stipulated approach-acceleration upper bound of <code>[EnforcementZOHBuffer.a_str]</code>, this inter-sample displacement bound equals <code>[EnforcementZOHBuffer.dzoh_str]</code>, the drift that the discretization condition of @sec-enforcement-invariance-conditions charges. Enforcing the barrier condition against the tightened safe set $\\TightenedDiscreteSafeSet = \\{\\MechanicalPlantState \\mid \\BarrierFunction(\\MechanicalPlantState) \\ge \\FiniteChange \\ZohHoldTravel\\}$ preserves physical clearance throughout the hold duration when the barrier measures spatial distance. When accounting for this margin in the stopping budget, architects must avoid double-counting, because any travel already allocated to the budget's one-tick fault detection interval already covers this displacement. When evaluating non-spatial or kinetic barriers, engineers cannot rely solely on a geometric displacement bound; the implementation must bound the worst-case evolution of the barrier function itself under the held input across the full sample interval $[\\TimeInstant_k, \\TimeInstant_{k+1}]$.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h2\">Summary</h2>\n<p>Runtime enforcement grants an admitted plan continuing, conditional permission to drive physical hardware. On every control cycle the enforcer evaluates live plant state against the tracking bound, the stopping reservation, and the actuator authority that remains. It passes a safe proposal through, projects a correctable one back into the admissible set, and selects a resident fallback, without waiting for the learned proposer, when no correction exists or the proposer falls silent.</p>", "quizzes": []};
